x_comment('x_addressText-NC',"The predicate #$addressText-NC maps a particular element of #$ContactLocation to a non-canonical #$Address-LocationDesignator, ADDRESS. The `NC' in the name stands for `non-canonical'.   (#$addressText-NC LOCATION ADDRESS) means that the #$ContactLocation LOCATION has the non-canonicalized address ADDRESS. For example, one value  for #$addressText-NC of the #$Cycorp #$MailingLocation is `Cycorp, 3721 Executive Center Dr., Ste. 100, Austin, TX 78731-1615.'  Note that this address as printed does not conform to the US Postal Service's recommended standard for printed addresses, and so relative to this standard it is not `canonical.' Non-canonical means that  ADDRESS may not conform to the canonicalization scheme(s) used for other known addresses for LOCATION.  These other (presumed canonical) addresses should be related to LOCATION using the predicate #$addressText. It really only makes sense to use #$addressText-NC in contexts (such as the logical schemas for certain database tables) where LOCATION may have both canonical and non-canonical addresses entered for it.  In contexts where the differences between canonicalized and non-canonicalized addresses is not an issue, just use #$addressText.  See also #$ContactLocation.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a6bc0).
