x_comment('x_nameString-NC',"(#$nameString-NC TERM STRING) means that the #$ProperNameString STRING names the thing denoted by TERM in a non-canonical way. The `NC' in the name stands for `non-canonical'.  For example, one value for #$nameString-NC for the Cyc constant #$Siegel might be `NICK SIEGEL'. This name as printed does not conform to the US Postal Service's recommended standard for printed names, and so relative to this standard it is not `canonical.'  (One US Postal Service `canonical' form would be `MR NICHOLAS SIEGEL').  Similarly, `JORDAN' might be the non-canonical form of the name of a Middle Eastern country.  A canonical form (official UN English version) of the name is `HASHEMITE KINGDOM OF JORDAN'. Non-canonical means that  NAME is not known to conform to the canonicalization scheme(s) used for other names entered for OBJECT.  These other (presumed canonical) names should be related to OBJECT using the predicate #$nameString. It really only makes sense to use #$nameString-NC in contexts (such as the #$LogicalTableSchemas for certain database tables) where OBJECT may have both canonical and non-canonical names entered for it, and the distinction between canonical and non-canonical forms somehow matters.  In contexts where the differences between canonicalized and non-canonicalized names is not an issue, just use #$nameString.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a6c62).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a6c62,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
