x_comment('x_EconomicSector-Localized',"Each instance of the collection #$EconomicSector-Localized is a particular major economic sector of some place: a large #$Group of producers or service providers in a defined geographic area.  For instance, the United States' manufacturing sector would be an element of #$EconomicSector-Localized, and would have particular U.S. manufacturers as #$groupMembers.   The 'world agriculture sector' and 'the technology sector in the United States' refer to instances of #$EconomicSector-Localized.  'The U.S. Automobile Industry' is too specific and is an instance of a particular #$Industry-Localized.   (Similarly, the collection #$AutomobileIndustry is an instance of #$IndustryType.)   Note that the main kinds of economic sector (#$EconomicSectorTypes like #$AgriculturalEconomicSector) are #$Collections (of particular geographically defined instances of #$EconomicSector-Localized); they are not themselves individual economic sectors in this scheme.  That is, there are several instances of the collection #$ManufacturingEconomicSector, such as the German manufacturing sector, the Mexican manufacturing sector, etc.  There is also the worldwide manufacturing sector.  Thus #$ManufacturingEconomicSector is a specialization, not an instance, of #$EconomicSector-Localized.  Note that this is for major sectors of an economy, not for specific industries; see #$Industry-Localized and #$IndustryType.  See also #$MtOfIndustryTypeFn and #$MtOfIndustryOfRegionFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a6d23).
