x_comment(x_presupposes,"Presupposition should be distinguished from precondition or\nlogical consequence.  If in normal unreflective use of natural language,\nas a general rule the assertion of PROP1 and the assertion of the negation\nof PROP1 both at least strongly suggest the truth of PROP2, then PROP1\npresupposes PROP2. If there are any presuppositions at all, there\n is a unique PROP2 such that (#$presupposes PROP1 PROP2) and that is the\nconjunction of all the presuppositions of PROP1.  A precondition for my\neating rice might be that some farmer planted the rice, but an assertion\nthat I did not eat the rice does not suggest anything about farmers.\nAnother precondition might be that I was hungry, but if I did not eat the\nrice it is not suggested that I was hungry. Rather the contrary is more\nnearly likely to be true.  But if I say I did not know that Fermat's\ntheorem has been proven, there is a presupposition that Fermat's theorem\nhas been proven.  That presupposition could be cancelled by the assertion\n`The supposed proof is invalid.'") :- x_cid(a65b16526a6eab).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a6eab,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
