x_comment(x_caloricContent,"(#$caloricContent STUFF MASS CAL) means that MASS of STUFF, if burned, releases CAL of energy. CAL will generally be stated using the unit function #$Calorie. For example, (#$caloricContent PeachPie001 (#$Gram 1) (#$Calorie 7)) means that PeachPie001 has 7 Calories per gram. To state the caloric content of a homogenous type of substance, for example to state that #$Water-Ingestible has 0 calories per gram, use (#$implies (#$isa ?WATER #$Water-Ingestible) (#$caloricContent ?WATER (#$Gram 1) (#$Calorie 0))). This predicate applies not only to food, but to anything that has any tangible part, and it is measured quantitatively and absolutely, with the arg3 representing the stored energy of the substance in kilocalories per MASS. If it can't burn, CAL will be equivalent to zero calories, regardless of the unit of mass. If it can burn, it will have a non-negative value for CAL. To specify the relative caloric content of food, use #$caloricContentOfFood, which allows qualitative values like High in addition to numeric values.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a6f57).
