x_comment(x_ContextOfPCWFn,"(#$ContextOfPCWFn WORK) denotes the #$PropositionalInformationThing associated with WORK. The expression (#$ContextOfPCWFn WORK) serves the same role as expressions that denote the PIT of a particular physical instance of WORK (such as (#$PITOfIBTFn COPY67OFWORK)), except that  (#$ContextOfPCWFn WORK) denotes the PIT of the abstract object WORK, which a copy of WORK instantiates. For example if we think of (#$ContextOfPCWFn WORK) as the entirety of the content of WORK, (#$ContextOfPCWFn #$MobyDickNovel) denotes the entirety of the content of MobyDick, whereas (#$PITOfIBTFn Copy787OfMobyDick) denotes the entirety of the content of Copy787OfMobyDick. Note that we assert as a default rule that whatever is true in the PIT of a #$PropositionalConceptualWork is true in the PIT an instantiation of that work, but not the converse. (A copy of Moby Dick may contain an introductory critical essay, or publication information -- information not contained in #$MobyDickNovel. Thus the PIT of a copy of MobyDick may be such that more things are true in it than are true in the PIT of #$MobyDickNovel). This rule is made default to account for the fact that a copy of a book remains a copy of Moby Dick even after damage which results in a small loss of the information that it contains.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a6ff9).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a6ff9,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
