x_comment(x_nearForRolesInEventType,"This predicate relates objects according to nearness conventions associated with roles in an event type.  (#$nearForRolesInEventType OBJ1 OBJ2 ROLE1 ROLE2 EVENTTYPE)\n\nmeans that OBJ1 and OBJ2 are near each other with respect to the roles\n\nROLE1 and ROLE2 in an event of type EVENTTYPE. For instance,\n\n(#$nearForRolesInEventType OBJ1 OBJ2 ROLE1 ROLE2 EVENTTYPE) means that\n\nwere PLAYER1 and PLAYER2 playing ROLE1 and ROLE2 respectively in an\n\nEVENT of type EVENTTYPE they would be near each other according to the\n\nconventions associated with those roles in that event type. (see\n\n#$nearnessForRolesInEventType).  Note that (#$nearForRolesInEventType\n\nOBJ1 OBJ2 ROLE1 ROLE2 EVENTTYPE) doesn't imply that OBJ1 is playing\n\nROLE1 and OBJ2 is playing ROLE2 in an event of type EVENTTYPE but only\n\nthat they'd be deemed to be near each other, relative to these roles,\n\nif they were participating in an event of type EVENTTYPE.  For\n\ninstance, if Person001 is one kilometer from GasStation001 we might\n\nassert that (#$nearForRolesInEventType PersonOO1 GasStation001\n\n#$transportees #$toLocation #$TransportWithMotorizedLandVehicle) but\n\nit would not necessarily be simultaneously true that (#$nearForRolesInEventType\n\nPerson001 GasStation001 #$performedBy #$toLocation\n\n#$Walking-Generic).  Also note that while (#$nearForRolesInEventType\n\nOBJ1 OBJ2 ROLE1 ROLE2 EVENTTYPE) doesn't imply that OBJ1 and OBJ2 are participating in an event of type EVENTTYPE it does imply that OBJ1 and OBJ2  are capable of playing ROLE1 and ROLE2 respectively in events of type EVENTTYPE.\n\nSee #$nearnessForRolesInEventType for a useful corresponding predicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a707e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a707e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526a707e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
