x_comment('x_Vibration-Translational',"The center of mass of an #$objectMoving in a #$Vibration-Translational periodically translates back and forth due as a function of some location dependent potential.  For example, a ball bounces up and down.  When the ball is high its potential energy is high and rising kinetic energy is low and decreasing.  Eventually kinetic energy peters out and the ball comes to a stop turns around and starts heading downward.  This kind of motion applies to non-circular elements of #$Orbiting, mass spring systems, swinging pendulums.  A bus shuttling to and fro (see #$Translation-BackAndForth) is not an exemplar since there is no equilibrium position for the bus.  Note that  #$Vibration-Translational is different from vibrational movement due to shape change.  For example, if you hit a bell it vibrates by changing its shape periodically.  Its center of mass does not necessarily move back and forth.  Therefore a bell ringing which does not also rock back and forth is not an #$objectMoving in a #$Vibration-Translational.  Note that the most typical exemplars of #$Vibration-Translational (e.g. a pendulum swinging) are also elements of #$Translation-NoLocationChange.  However, not changing location is not a necessary property since, e.g. a ball bouncing its way across a stage is clearly an element of #$Vibration-Translational and does undergo location change.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a70a7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a70a7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526a70a7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
