x_comment('x_enables-RemovesDisablingFactors',"#$enables-RemovesDisablingFactors expresses the notion that an\nagent may be 'enabled' by an event in the following sense: the event\nremoves all factors that are 'disabling', i.e., that would cause the agent\nnot to be capable of doing a given action -- put another way: all factors\nthat would prevent the agent from performing the action. More accurately,\n(#$enables-RemovesDisablingFactors SIT AGENT ACT_TYPE) is true iff SIT\nobtains, there are no factors preventing AGENT from performing an instance\nof ACT_TYPE, and SIT is the cause of there being no such disabling\nfactors. Hence #$enables-RemovesDisablingFactors does not imply that the\nagent is in fact capable of doing a token of ACT_TYPE: it merely entails\nthat no factor that would disable her is present. Note that in general this\nis a stronger notion than merely removing incapacitating factors. Example:\ngiving an ax or a saw to a physically fit adult individual would typically\nenable him to chop down a tree in this sense, since in typical\ncircumstances not having an ax would be disabling -- though perhaps not\nincapacitating. Complex events such as military tasks may also be regarded\nas typical examples of this concept -- e.g., in a\n#$ReliefInPlace-MilitaryTask the relieved unit is enabled in this sense to\ndisengage and perform service support tasks.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a70b5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a70b5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526a70b5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526a70b5,2635).
