x_comment(x_CharacterStringToken,"The collection of concrete tokens of abstract    character-strings.   More precisely, #$CharacterStringToken    is a specialization of #$HumanAccessibleIBT each of whose    instances is a specific physical representation of some    particular instance of #$CharacterString.  (Note that a    character-string might consist of only a single character,   in which case a token of the string effectively represents    the character itself; thus for simplicity #$CharacterToken    has been made a specialization of #$CharacterStringToken.)   Character-string tokens exist in at least three different    basic forms: as inscriptions, sounds, or electromagnetic    encodings (see #$CharacterStringToken-Inscribed,    #$CharacterStringToken-Sounded, and #$CharacterStringToken-ElectroMagnetic).  Abstract character-strings themselves are inherently    neither inscriptional nor sonic nor electromagnetic, so the same    string can be represented by tokens of these different forms.     Character-string tokens often, but not always, consist in    spatial or temporal series of character tokens (see    #$CharacterStringToken-Serial).") :- x_cid(a65b16526a7436).
