x_comment(x_eventPred,"This predicate is used to construct 'non-Davidsonian'\n\nrepresentations of actions and events, which are of the form WALKS(Mary,\n\nMarys-Home). In the usual 'Davidsonian' style, this would be expressed as 'there is\n\nan event X such that X is a #$Walking-Generic, etc.'. Notice that, unlike\n\nits Davidsonian counterpart, it makes sense to say that WALKS(Mary,\n\nMarys-Home) is true at some times -- namely when Mary *is walking* home --\n\nbut not at others -- e.g., after she has arrived. The problem is that we\n\ndon't have such predicates as WALKS in Cyc. #$eventPred is intended to fill\n\nthis gap in a general and simple way, and without introducing a lot of\n\nredundant duplication. Suppose EVTYPE is a subcollection of #$Event, LIST1\n\nis a n-ary list of the form SLOT01 ... SLOT0n, where each element is an\n\ninstance of #$ActorSlot, and LIST2 is a n-ary list of things satisfying the\n\n#$arg2Isa constraint on their respective actor slot. Then (#$eventPred\n\nEVTYPE LIST1 LIST2) is true at a time t iff t if the 1st element of LIST2\n\nis playing role SLOT01 in an event of type EVTYPE, the 2nd element of LIST2\n\nis playing role SLOT02, and so on. Consider the example above: we can form\n\n(#$eventPred #$Walking-Generic (#$TheList #$performedBy #$toLocation)\n\n(#$TheList MARY HOME)) to represent the clause WALKS(Mary, Home) as\n\ndescribed in the example. The meaning of this #$ELSentence-Assertible is expressed by\n\nthe sentence 'Mary is walking home'.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a7475).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a7475,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526a7475,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526a7475,4438).
