x_comment('x_CharacterStringToken-ElectroMagnetic',"A specialization of both #$CharacterStringToken and \n\n  #$ElectroMagneticIBT each of whose instances \n\n  represents or realizes a particular abstract instance of \n\n  #$CharacterString in some language, writing system, or encoding \n\n  scheme.  Most electromagnetic character-string tokens are (not \n\n  necessarily spatially contiguous) physical series of electromagnetic \n\n  character tokens (see #$CharacterToken-ElectroMagnetic and \n\n  #$CharacterStringToken-Serial).  An electromagnetic string token \n\n  can literally be stored (e.g. in a computer's memory or on a \n\n  floppy disk) and transmitted (e.g. via wires or as radio signals) \n\n  using certain electronic devices.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a74f3).
