x_comment(x_bayesParent,"A predicate used for probability statements.  (#$bayesParent BAYES-VARIABLE1 BAYES-VARIABLE2 BAYESNET) means that the #$BayesDiscreteOutcome associated with BAYES-VARIABLE2 influences the #$BayesDiscreteOutcome associated with BAYES-VARIABLE1, and that BAYES-VARIABLE2 is an immediate 'parent node' of the BAYES-VARIABLE1 node in the Bayesian Network BAYESNET representing probabilistic influence in the applicable #$Microtheory.  That is, the second bayes variable is a direct parent of the first in the #$BayesNet.   Note that BAYES-VARIABLE1/outcome and BAYES-VARIABLE2/outcome cannot be #$conditionallyIndependent in the #$Microtheory if one is a #$bayesParent of the other.  Given the outcomes of all of a node's #$bayesParent nodes, then all of its further ancestor nodes and other non-descendant nodes are #$conditionallyIndependent of it in the applicable #$Microtheory.  To relate a node to the set of all of its parent nodes, use #$bayesParentSet.   The direction is obtained from the conditional dependence --- given the outcome of a node --- among its #$bayesParents (the 'explaining away' effect), which does not seem to apply among its Bayesian 'child' nodes.  Many Bayesian network theorists consider that the directions on the links correspond to the direction of causal influence, and hence to the direction of time. ") :- x_cid(a65b16526a74fa).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a74fa,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526a74fa,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526a74fa,4741).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526a74fa,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526a74fa,[]).
x_comment(x_groupCohesion,"(#$groupCohesion GROUP DEGREE) indicates that the members of the group GROUP have the degree DEGREE of loyalty to          GROUP.  Group loyalty may be based on a variety of motives.             For example, in a business the degree of #$groupCohesion may depend             upon the extent to which its employees desire the             success of the business.  In a military organization #$groupCohesion may depend              upon the extent to which its members believe in the purpose or purposes for             which the organization exists.  In a criminal organization it may depend             upon how much the members can benefit from the success of the organization's             endeavors.  Note that a particular degree of #$groupCohesion             does not imply any degree of loyalty between members of the group on an             individual level.  For example, a criminal organization may have a high degree             of #$groupCohesion but this does not imply that any one member will be loyal to             other members where the success or well-being of the organization is not             at stake. See also #$Loyalty-Status.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a74fb).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a74fb,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
