x_comment(x_SubeventSequenceGraphMicrotheory,"A collection of microtheories.  Each microtheory of this type gives information concerning a graph, GRAPH, that is purported to represent the sequencing rules for instances of some script type, SCRIPTTYPE.  i.e., (#$relationInstanceAll #$graphRepresents GRAPH SCRIPTTYPE).  If MT is an instance of #$SubeventSequenceGraphMicrotheory then it should define the graph(#$PathSystem), state all the nodes (#$nodeInSystem), each of which should be script-types and state links between the nodes which represent script types, instances of which may or could be in a #$followsInProgression relationship during the instances of the SCRIPTTYPE that is being constrained (if we are focussing mainly on the temporal ordering of the subevents).  For instance, suppose we define a car safety checking script type (SimpleCarSafetyCheck) and want to define the sequence of events which must occur in any such event.  Suppose that the first step is to check the tires (TireCheck, a kind of event), the second step is to check fluid levels (FluidLevelCheck, a kind of event), the third and final is to check the brakes (BrakeCheck, a kind of event).  We define a path system (e.g., #$DirectedMultigraph) and assert that it represents (see #$graphRepresents or #$graphRepresentsType) all instances of SimpleCarSafetyCheck.  It should also be asserted that the last required event-type is a sink node (#$sinkNodeInSystem) We assert that TireCheck, BrakeCheck, and FluidLevelCheck are all nodes in the path system, CarSafetyCheckGraph and create instances of #$Path-Simple to link the event-types instances of which will be in a #$followsInProgression relation and then use #$linkFromToInSystem to articulate the links.  Note that this becomes more complicated in cases in which the type of event that precedes an event has implications for the type of event that should follow.  For instance, in a gas buying event, if one pays after getting out of the car one should pump after paying.  However, if one pays after closing the tank one should get into the car after paying.  In those cases we need to use #$weightOfLink.  If a link between two nodes is n and we want events of type EVENTTYPE to follow sequences of events of this type, then EVENTTYPE should be linked to the second event type with a link of weight n.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a74fe).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a74fe,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526a74fe,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526a74fe,4745).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526a74fe,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526a74fe,[]).
