x_comment('x_GeneralizedQuantifier-Conservative',"If GEN is a conservative generalized quantifier, then for any\n\ndomain of discourse and any pair of collections C1, C2 defined therein,\n\n(GEN C1 C2) is true iff (GEN C1 (#$CollectionIntersection2Fn C1 C2)) is. In\n\ninformal terms, a generalized quantifier is conservative if the elements of\n\nC2 that are not in C1 do not affect the truth-conditions of the\n\nquantifier: if we increased the extension of C2 with elements that are not\n\nalso in C1, we would not change the truth-value of (GEN C1 C2). Take, e.g.,\n\n`every mailman runs': if the extension of the class of runners is augmented\n\nby adding\n\nrunners who aren't mailmen, it makes no difference to the truth of that\n\nsentence. Contrast with `only mailmen drive those funny trucks': if the\n\nextension of funny-truck drivers is augmented with individuals who aren't\n\nmailmen, then that sentence would change from true to false.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a762c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a762c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526a762c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
