x_comment('x_CharacterStringToken-Serial',"A specialization of both #$CharacterStringToken and\n  #$SeriesWithoutRepetition each of whose instances thus (1) \n  #$tokensString a certain character-string and (2) is itself a series \n  of instances of #$CharacterToken.  Most inscribed and electromagnetic \n  character-string tokens are serial (see #$CharacterStringToken-Inscribed\n  and #$CharacterStringToken-ElectroMagnetic).  Conversely, most sounded \n  character-string tokens are not serial, as they cannot normally \n  be divided into discrete sounded character tokens (see\n  #$CharacterStringToken-Sounded).  Inscribed \n  serial string tokens are instances of #$PhysicalSeries, and are \n  #$seriesOrderedBy instances of #$SpatialPredicate.\n  Sounded serial string tokens, though rare, do ostensibly occur, \n  for example, when words are \"spelled aloud\" or when one recites \n  the alphabet; they are #$seriesOrderedBy instances of \n  #$TemporalPredicate.  An example of an inscribed character-string \n  token that is not serial is an illegible signature that cannot be \n  divided into discrete inscribed character tokens.  A given serial \n  string token T #$tokensString a particular string S just in case \n  each of the component character tokens in T #$tokensCharacter the \n  corresponding component character in S (and T and S are the \n  same length).") :- x_cid(a65b16526a76de).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a76de,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
