x_comment(x_CycLClosedDenotationalTerm,"The collection of all closed denotational terms in the CycL language.  An expression is said to be \"closed\" if it contains no free variables (see #$CycLClosedExpression).  A CycL term is said to be \"denotational\" if it is the right sort of term to have a denotation (or value) in the universe of discourse (see #$CycLDenotationalTerm).  CycL sentences, while terms of CycL, are not considered denotational terms.  Each instance of #$CycLClosedDenotationalTerm is either a #$CycLClosedAtomicTerm (i.e. a #$CycLConstant or #$SubLAtomicTerm) or a #$CycLClosedNonAtomicTerm (i.e. a \"NAT\" with no free variables).  Examples of closed denotational terms include `#$Muffet', `(#$JuvenileFn #$Dog)', `(#$TheSetOf ?X (#$objectHasColor ?X GreenColor))', and `212'.  Note that these are also examples: `(#$BorderBetweenFn #$Canada #$Mexico)' (despite the fact that it fails actually to denote anything) and `(#$JuvenileFn #$isa #$genls #$JuvenileFn)'\n\n(despite the fact that it is not semantically well-formed).") :- x_cid(a65b16526a77d9).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a77d9,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526a77d9,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
