x_comment('x_ComputerProcess-Generic',"The collection of events in which a 'process' is executed on a #$ComputationalSystem, using the #$ComputationalSystem's resources. 'Process' here refers to the act of a CPU or multiple CPUs executing the  instructions in a #$ComputerProgramCopy.  A process ends when it is terminated (by its own instructions, the operating system, a power outage, etc.) A #$ComputerProcess-Generic can be suspended or swapped out by the #$OperatingSystem (whereupon it temporarily ceases to exist) but when it is swapped back in, it will still be the same #$ComputerProcess-Generic. Hence, the time during which a #$ComputerProcess-Generic exists can be a discontinuous time interval. #$ComputerProcess-Generics can exist on multiple #$Computers (e.g. a single instance of #$ComputerProcess-Generic distributed over a network), but cease to exist on a #$Computer when that #$Computer is rebooted. Note that this is slightly more general than the notion of process in standard computer terminology, e.g. 'a Unix process', which corresponds more closely with #$ComputerProcess-SingleComputer.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a7915).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a7915,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
