x_comment(x_objectAttached,"(#$objectAttached ATTACHING OBJ) means that OBJ is the thing attached to something else in ATTACHING.  Often it is useful to think of actors in #$AttachmentEvents as \"attached\" and \"attachees\", i.e., the thing attached versus the thing to which something is attached.  For instance, if I attach a rearview mirror to my windshield, the rearview mirror seems to be the item attached while the windshield is the \"attachee\".   We use the predicate #$objectAttached to relate an attachment event to the \"thing attached\" in an attachment event.\n\nWhen a much smaller object gets stuck to a larger object, the smaller object is usally deemed play the role #$objectAttached.  For instance, if a person becomes attached to a burr, the burr plays the role #$objectAttached and the person plays the role #$objectAdheredTo in the attachment event.  If two burrs become attached to each other they both play the role #$objectOfAttachment and there is no designated \"attached\" and \"attachee\".  Note that as a default rule (#$objectAttached ATTACHING SMALL) relates an attaching event to the smaller of the relevant actors in the ATTACHING, but there may be counterexamples.  For instance, a trailer may be larger than the car by which it is towed and yet be deemed to play the role #$objectAttached in the event in which the car is connected to the trailer.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a7b1b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a7b1b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526a7b1b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
