x_comment('x_GeographicalDirection-General',"A specialization of #$GeographicalDirection whose instances are general, as opposed to precise or \"direct\", directions.  More exactly: with respect to a specified reference point, a given general direction (e.g. #$North-Generally) is an instance of #$VectorInterval that comprises the wedge-shaped set of vectors that point within 22.5 degrees in either horizontal direction of the corresponding \"direct\" direction (viz. #$North-Directly).  The vertical direction of these vectors is in effect ignored, and can be anything strictly between 0 degrees (\"straight up\") and 180 degrees (\"straight down\").  Thus the \"wedge\" itself has a 45 degree interior horizontal angle, and none of the eight reified general directions \"overlap\".  Compare #$GeographicalDirection-Direct.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a7b30).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a7b30,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
