x_comment(x_PresuppositionVocabTerm,"The collection of CycL terms\n\n that \n\n enable a basic representation of commonsense knowledge \n\nthat \"pack in\" or \"gloss over\" assumptions that, for a machine,\n\n would generally need to be made explicit.  By\n\n definition, instances of \n\n#$PresuppositionVocabTerm are axiomatically \n\n related to other predicates that make all or most of\n\n the assumptions in such natural language expressions\n\n explicit.  Consider, for example, the Cyc treatment\n\n of statements about evidence, which (appropriately)\n\n involves ascribing an amount of evidence that an \n\n #$IntelligentAgent has in support of some belief (see\n\n #$evidentialSupportForBelief).  However, in ordinary\n\n English, people who make statements about evidence \n\ngenerally do not make them in terms of agents or beliefs.\n\n Often, they make them in terms of an amount of evidence\n\n and some #$Individual or #$Collection (e.g., \"there\n\n is plenty of evidence for Nessie,\" \"there is no\n\n evidence of UFOs\").  Obviously, representing such compacted \n\nforms of knowledge about evidence using \n\n#$evidentialSupportForBelief is problematic.  Thus we\n\n need #$evidentialSupportForPhenomena-AgentUnspecified,\n\n which takes an amount and a collection -- and so \n\n allows for representation of such commonly understood claims about\n\n evidence, and is understood by Cyc via axioms\n\n to the explicit, or 'longhand' CycL involving\n\n #$evidentialSupportForBelief.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a7de7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a7de7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526a7de7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526a7de7,8685).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526a7de7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526a7de7,[]).
