x_comment(x_anteriorTo,"(#$anteriorTo OBJ-1 OBJ-2) means that OBJ-1 lies in the #$AnteriorDirection of OBJ-2, when viewed in the frame of reference of the object with #$AnimalBodyShape (often, a #$Person) that they are located in.  In a #$Person, #$anteriorTo almost always corresponds to #$ventralTo, but in a #$Dog or other four-legged animal, #$anteriorTo usually corresponds to the inverse of #$caudalTo.  Note that if OBJ-1 is #$anteriorTo OBJ-2, OBJ-2 is posterior to OBJ-1; thus, no distinct predicate is needed to represent `posterior to'.  OBJ-1 and OBJ-2 are often, but not always, #$AnimalBodyRegions.  #$anteriorTo is used in the #$Head-AnimalBodyPart and #$Foot-AnimalBodyPart instead of #$ventralTo.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a8039).
