x_comment(x_ArbitraryCommandExecutionFault,"#$ArbitraryCommandExecutionFault is an attribute of some instances of #$ComputerProgram-CW, and an important specialization of #$CommandExecutionFault.  A program has an #$ArbitraryCommandExecutionFault if an attacker can manipulate the program into executing code chosen by the attacker.  An attacker may exploit an #$ArbitraryCommandExecutionFault in a number of ways: by sending commands directly to the program in the form of a malicious format string containing embedded metacharacters, or by installing an executable which the program (perhaps during some future login session) later executes (see #$TrojanAccommodatingFault), or by writing an executable which is received by the vulnerable machine and which the faulty program then executes without taking proper security measures (see #$FileCommandExecutionFault).  The commands that can be thus executed are \"arbitrary\" in the sense that the attacker can execute any command at the privilege level of the program.  The fact that many programs with the fault run with elevated privileges is what makes this fault particularly dangerous.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a849b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a849b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526a849b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
