x_comment('x_maximalConstraint-Isa',"(#$maximalConstraint-Isa PRED N COL) says that the known\nmaximal constraint for the Nth argument place of PRED is any instance\nof COL.  Here, 'maximal constraint' means that typically, the best or\nmost sensible collection, any instance of which could fill the Nth\nargument place, is COL.  The maximal #$isa constraint for a predicate\nneed not always be determined by its #$argIsa assertions, though\nsometimes it will be (when it its not, the maximal #$isa constraint is\ndetermined by the predicates #$typicalArgIsa assertions).  An example\nof a predicate for which the #$argIsa assertions do not determine a\nmaximal #$isa constraint could be, #$primaryFunction, whose #$arg1Isa\nis #$SomethingExisting.  This isn't maximal, since this allows one to\nconstruct well-formed instances of #$ELSentence-Assertible by putting\nan instance of #$HumanInfant in the arg1 position, and we generally do\nnot consider infants to be things with a 'primary function' (see the\n#$comment on #$primaryFunction), though we *could*.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a869e).
