x_comment(x_inTheSuburbOf,"(#$inTheSuburbOf SUB CITY) means that SUB is in the suburban area of CITY. This #$BinaryPredicate relates an #$UrbanArea to the main city of which it is a suburb, see below. The notion of suburb can be difficult to delineate and would necessitate more work, see the #$cyclistNotes. The notion of suburb that this term assumes corresponds to the traditional definition of an area lying immediately outside a city of significant importance for the region it's located in. Because #$inTheSuburbOf relates a city and a part only of its suburb there is no clause of contiguity when applying this relation. The importance of the city can be evaluated in different ways corresponding for instance to the physical, historical, political, military properties of an area. Note additionally that while 'being in the suburb of' can be to some extend considered as an inheritable property, we chose to define #$inTheSuburbOf as functional in its second argument and an area can be in the suburb of only one city. Note also that this relation wouldn't be transitive if the first argument ranged on #$City and for instance, while (#$inTheSuburbOf #$JimsCity #$CityOfPasadenaCA) and (#$inTheSuburbOf #$CityOfPasadenaCA #$CityOfLosAngelesCA) we do not want to conclude (#$inTheSuburbOf #$JimsCity #$CityOfLosAngelesCA), we would use instead a more general relation and assert (#$inTheVicinityOf-Geographical #$JimsCity #$CityOfLosAngelesCA) in #$UnitedStatesGeographyDualistMt. Positive examples, both #$CityOfIvry-sur-SeineFrance and #$CityOfVillejuifFrance are in the suburb of #$CityOfParisFrance, the first one is spatially contiguous to Paris while the second isn't. Let's emphasize on the fact that while a city like Paris has many areas in its suburban area, each of these is in the suburb of one only, namely Paris.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a9230).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a9230,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526a9230,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526a9230,15599).
