x_comment(x_CyclicCommutativeRelation,"The collection #$CyclicCommutativeRelation is a\n\n#$RelationshipType and a spec of #$Relation.  An instance of\n\n#$CyclicCommutativeRelation is a relation which is commutative with\n\nrespect to even permutations of its arguments and is anti-commutative\n\nwith respect to odd permutations of its arguments.  (See the\n\naccompanying #$cyclistNotes on this constant for the definitions of\n\neven and odd permutations.)  If REL is both a\n\n#$CyclicCommutativeRelation and a #$Predicate and if the term\n\n(REL . ARGS) is true, then (REL . ARGS-PRIME) is true whenever\n\nARGS-PRIME is an even permutation of ARGS and (#$not (REL .\n\nARGS-PRIME)) is true whenever ARGS-PRIME is an odd permutation of\n\nARGS.  If REL is a #$CyclicCommutativeRelation and a\n\n#$Function-Denotational, then the term (REL .  ARGS) #$equals the term\n\n(REL . ARGS-PRIME) whenever ARGS-PRIME is an even permutation of ARGS\n\nand the two are different whenever ARGS-PRIME is an odd permutation of\n\nARGS.  For example, the predicate `#$counterClockwise' #$isa\n\n#$CyclicCommutativeRelation.  If POINT-1, POINT-2, and POINT-3 are\n\nthree points such that (#$counterClockwise POINT-1 POINT-2 POINT-3)\n\nholds, then it is necessary and sufficient that (#$counterClockwise\n\nPOINT-3 POINT-1 POINT-2) holds as well.  Furthermore the above hold if\n\nand only if (#$not (#$counterClockwise POINT-2 POINT-1 POINT-3))\n\nholds.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a97af).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a97af,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526a97af,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526a97af,17488).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526a97af,[]).
