x_comment('x_signifiesTo-Symbol',"This is a specialized representation (semiotic) predicate meaning representation due to some arbitrary symbolic convention, such as when the English word 'Parthenon' represents the actual Parthenon, when a roaring lion represents MGM Studios, or when the abstract tetrahedron represent 'Fire' in alchemy. (#$signifiesTo-Symbol THING1 THING2 AGENT) means that, to AGENT, THING1 represents THING2 because of some arbitrary convention or association of the two (that is known to AGENT).  THING1 need not resemble THING2 in any way, nor need it be causally related to THING2.  THING1 acts as an 'arbitrary sign' of THING2.  This is called 'symbolic' representation.  Compare this with the predicates #$signifiesTo-Icon and #$signifiesTo-Index.  It's possible for all three to apply to the same representation.  The spoken word 'Bow-Wow' is not only a conventional 'symbol' of barking but also an 'icon' because it resembles what it represents.  If the basis for the representation is unknown, use the more general predicate #$signifiesTo.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a9811).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a9811,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526a9811,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526a9811,17697).
