x_comment(x_LocalLinkFlowCapacityFn,"The function #$LocalLinkFlowCapacityFn, applied to a link within a #$NetworkFlowSystem-Bounded as in (LocalLinkFlowCapacityFn LINK SYSTEM), denotes the given, local 'flow capacity' for LINK alone, regardless of its connections to the rest of SYSTEM.  A 'flow capacity' is a number that is the measure of the maximum capacity of LINK, in isolation from other links, for the flow of something along the link, such as vehicles-per-hour, gallons-per-minute, or some other flow or quantity that conforms to the #$NetworkFlowSystem-Bounded rules.  (To find out what kind of flow is assumed by a particular #$NetworkFlowSystem-Bounded, see the predicate #$flowTypeOfSystem.)  Because it is a local measure on LINK, #$LocalLinkFlowCapacityFn need not take into account flow bottlenecks upstream or downstream from it (for that, see #$SituatedLinkFlowCapacityFn).  Technical note: Every link in a #$NetworkFlowSystem-Bounded has a direction.  If the value of #$LocalLinkFlowCapacityFn is negative, it refers to minimum flow in the backward direction.  This is unusual.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a983d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a983d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
