x_comment(x_SituatedLinkFlowCapacityFn,"The function #$SituatedLinkFlowCapacityFn, applied to a link and the #$NetworkFlowSystem-Bounded of which it is a part, as in (SituatedLinkFlowCapacityFn LINK SYSTEM), denotes the net (usally calculated) 'flow capacity' for LINK taking into account the effects of its connections to the rest of SYSTEM.  Here the 'flow capacity' is a number that is the measure of the maximum capacity of LINK, as restricted by any connections to other links, for the flow of something along the link, such as vehicles-per-hour, gallons-per-minute, or some other flow or quantity that conforms to the #$NetworkFlowSystem-Bounded rules.  (To find out what kind of flow is assumed by a particular #$NetworkFlowSystem-Bounded, see the predicate #$flowTypeOfSystem.)  Because it is a 'situated' or 'context-dependent' measure on LINK, it must take into account any flow bottlenecks upstream or downstream from it (for the local total capacity of the link in isolation, see #$LocalLinkFlowCapacityFn). Technical notes: The value of #$SituatedLinkFlowCapacityFn is often calculated from the #$LocalLinkFlowCapacityFn values of all the links in the #$PathSystem using an algorithm like the Ford-Fulkerson algorithm. Every link in a #$NetworkFlowSystem-Bounded has a direction.  If the value of #$SituatedLinkFlowCapacityFn is negative, it refers to minimum flow in the backward direction.  This is unusual.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a9870).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a9870,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
