x_comment(x_NTShellPathUnderspecificationFault,"A fault common to all versions of #$MSWindowsNT (4.0 to 2000 inclusive).  Upon startup, #$MSWindowsNT checks the value of the \"Shell\" registry key, HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\WindowsNT\\CurrentVersion\\Winlogon\\Shell, to determine the name of the executable that should be loaded as the Shell.  By default, this value specifies Explorer.exe.  The fault is the result of a combination of factors: (1) the fact that the file Explorer.exe is there specified by a relative rather than absolute pathname, and (2) the algorithm according to which #$MSWindowsNT searches the filesystem when a file is specified with a relative pathname.  (Hence the #$genlAttributes to #$UnderspecifiedPathFault).  A malicious user could exploit this fault by placing a malicious file called \"Explorer.exe\" somewhere in the filesystem which lies shallower in the filesystem searchspace than the \"real\" Explorer.exe.  Depending upon the privileges of the user logging in, the trojan Explorer.exe could extend #$AdministratorAccount privileges to the hacker.  Microsoft has published patches for this fault which insert a special-case subroutine into the startup code.  In effect, the patch prepends '%systemroot%' to the value of the salient #$RegistryKey.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a98dc).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a98dc,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
