x_comment(x_EcologicalRegion,"A collection of geographical regions.  Each instance of #$EcologicalRegion is a region having one or more characteristic ecosystems.  From knowledge of those ecosystems, we can posit whether certain organisms can forage, reproduce, and live successfully there.  Information about ecological regions typically also includes what kinds of organisms are in fact found there.  In theory, any arbitrary continuous region could be analyzed as an ecological region, but most regions identified in practice have some kind of sameness or systematic interconnection in their topology, climate, and biology.  Examples: the #$WesternDesertOfEgypt, the #$GreatBarrierReef, the #$Amazon-Region.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a992f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a992f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526a992f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526a992f,18178).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526a992f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526a992f,[]).
x_comment(x_LocalLinkCutSetFlowCapacityFn,"The function #$LocalLinkCutSetFlowCapacityFn, applied to a set of some things and to a #$NetworkFlowSystem-Bounded in which they are links forming a 'cutset', as in (#$LocalLinkCutSetFlowCapacityFn LINKCUTSET FLOWSYS), denotes the sum of all the #$LocalLinkFlowCapacityFn values of the links in LINKCUTSET, in FLOWSYS.  This is without regard to the links' connections to other links in FLOWSYS (for the context-sensitive version, see #$SituatedLinkCutSetFlowCapacityFn).  The result is the total of the #$LocalLinkFlowCapacityFn values on all the links going in or out of that node.  Note: The predicate #$flowTypeOfSystem is used to find what kind of 'flow' is represented in a particular #$NetworkFlowSystem-Bounded.  See also #$SituatedLinkCutSetFlowCapacityFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a9930).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a9930,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
