x_comment(x_UnderspecifiedPathFault,"An #$OperatingSystem has this fault if, in those situations where it is called upon to retrieve or execute some file without being given the file's full location (path), it looks for the file in a manner that makes insertion and execution of trojan programs a fairly trivial matter.  Note that both #$UnixOSes and #$MSWindowsOperatingSystems may have this fault.  In #$UnixOSes the fault is usually due to the OS's including the user's current directory in the $PATH environment variable; this makes it easy for a malicious user to insert malicious programs called 'ls', 'more', 'grep', etc.  In the case of #$MSWindowsOperatingSystems, the fault is usually due to the order in which the OS searches the filesystem in looking for the relevant file (this search may or may not include checking the user's HKEY_CURRENT_USER\\Environment\\Path registry setting).  One common and very dangerous fault of this kind is the #$NTShellPathUnderspecificationFault.  Commands executed via exploitation of an #$UnderspecifiedPathFault are executed with the privileges of the user running the command.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a9956).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a9956,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
