x_comment('x_needs-Prop',"This predicate relates an agent to a proposition\n\n(represented by a sentence that expresses it) that s/he stands in need of being true in order to achieve or further some (unspecified) goal. The intended sense of \"needs\" includes, but is weaker than, strict physical necessity; see the comment on #$needs-Generic for a fuller account.  (#$needs-Prop AGENT SENT) means that AGENT needs or requires the proposition expressed by SENT to be true.  This implies that SENT is not currently true, but is indeterminate as to whether AGENT #$knows this (though in many cases s/he will). For example, the statement \"I need you to come here\" can be represented as my needing it to be the case that you are here; \"Joe needs a haircut\" can be represented as Joe needing it to be the case that his hair is cut (whether Joe himself realizes this or not).  What AGENT needs SENT to be true for (i.e. what goal of hers it would further) and what will happen to AGENT if SENT fails to become true (e.g. whether she will die, suffer an important loss, or just be noticeably inconvenienced) are left unspecified in the above formula, though in practice they might be inferable from other assertions in the context (see e.g. #$needsFor).  Note that #$needs-Prop is _not_ a #$PropositionalAttitudeSlot.  This is because, as the above examples show, #$needs-Prop represents a more-or-less \"objective\" requirement of AGENT's for SENT to be true, and does not entail (or preclude) a corresponding \"felt need\" on AGENT's part.  A felt need of this sort is perhaps best represented as an assertion that a given agent both #$knows that s/he objectively needs some proposition to be true and #$desires that it be true.  See also #$needs, #$needs-Type, #$needsToDo, and #$satisfiesNeed.") :- x_cid(a65b16526a9cea).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526a9cea,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526a9cea,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
