x_comment('x_cultureProscribes-Categorical',"(#$cultureProscribes-Categorical CULTURE ACTTYPE ROLE) \n  means that within CULTURE it is considered improper or immoral\n  or wrong to play ROLE in instances of ACTTYPE.  For example, \n  most cultures consider it wrong to take a human life.  This does not \n  entail, however, that CULTURE has a (hypothetical) proscription \n  against playing ROLE in instances of ACTTYPE with respect to every \n  possible condition; i.e. there might exist exceptional situations in \n  which playing ROLE in an ACTTYPE is not proscribed (and might even \n  be prescribed) by CULTURE.  One might be required to take human life,\n  for example, in wartime.  This predicate applies equally to moral \n  rules, rules of conduct or etiquette, and customs.  See also \n  #$cultureProscribes-Hypothetical,#$culturePrescribes-Categorical, \n  and #$culturePrescribes-Hypothetical.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa10b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa10b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa10b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
