x_comment(x_ArgNotIsaPredicate,"The collection of #$ArgTypePredicates (q.v.) that specify argument-types directly (by referencing certain collections) and impose constraints that _forbid_ a thing's being an instance of a collection thus specified.  More precisely, each instance of #$ArgNotIsaPredicate PRED takes a #$Relation RELN as its first argument (or \"arg1\"), and has at least one other argument-place (say the Mth) for specifying a forbidden argument-type for one of RELN's argument-places (say the Nth).  (The value of N might be fixed or might be given by yet another of PRED's arguments.)  Suppose M=2 and N=1.  PRED then takes a #$Collection as its second argument, and a closed atomic sentence (or \"GAF\") of the form (PRED RELN COL ..) puts a negative \"#$isa\" constraint on RELN's first argument-place that is based on COL.  If PRED is a \"single-argument\" constraint predicate, the GAF entails that a closed formula of the form (RELN ARG1 ..) is semantically well-formed only if ARG1 is _not_ an instance of (i.e. #$isa) COL.  If PRED is an \"inter-argument\" constraint predicate, the GAF expresses a slightly more complicated, conditional negative #$isa constraint on a pair of RELN's argument-places (see #$InterArgNotIsaPredicate).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa350).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa350,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa350,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa350,22342).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa350,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa350,[]).
