x_comment(x_SpecsWithQuantityFn,"(#$SpecsWithQuantityFn COL ATT) is the collection\n of all specs of COL that have ATT.  For example,\n (#$SpecsWithQuantityFn #$Bird #$Indigenous) is the\n collection of all kinds of birds that are indigenous.\n It is important to keep in mind that \n(#$SpecsWithQuantityFn COL ATT) is always \nan instance of #$CollectionType, and though it is true that\n for every instance C of (#$SpecsWithQuantityFn COL ATT), there is an appropriate quantity slot, QUANTITY-SLOT, such that \n (QUANTITY-SLOT C ATT), it does not follow that\n (QUANTITY-SLOT COL ATT), nor does it follow that\n there are any instances of COL such that \n (QUANTITY-SLOT COL ATT).  In most cases, ATT will\n be an instance of #$TypeLevelQuantity; the reason\n the #$arg2Isa of this function is not #$TypeLevelQuantity is that there\n could be quantities that can apply meaningfully to both\n#$Individuals and #$Collections.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa462).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa462,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa462,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa462,22679).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa462,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa462,[]).
x_comment(x_pragmaticRequirement,"A #$MetaKnowledgePredicate that is used to place a certain kind of pragmatic precondition on the use of a given asserted rule (see #$CycLRuleAssertion). (#$pragmaticRequirement SENTENCE RULE) means that SENTENCE expresses a necessary condition on RULE's being used in any proof carried out by the Cyc inference engine. The effect of asserting this is virtually the same as that of asserting (#$exceptWhen (#$unknownSentence SENTENCE) RULE): it blocks the use of RULE when SENTENCE is not currently \"known\" by the Cyc system to be true. SENTENCE should not contain disjuncts (or canonicalize into disjuncts). Upon assertion to the knowledge base, a #$pragmaticRequirement sentence is transformed by the canonicalizer into a rule involving #$pragmaticallyNormal (q.v.).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa463).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa463,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa463,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa463,22695).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa463,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa463,[]).
x_comment(x_productTypeSoldAt,"(#$productTypeSoldAt PRODTYPE LOCATION) the #$ProductType PRODTYPE can be obtained at LOCATION. LOCATION can be any instance of partially tangible, including a particular supermarket, vending machine, or trunk of someone's car.  The implication is that PRODTYPE can typically be purchased at LOCATION,  not just as a one-time sale.  See #$sellsProductType to relate a product type to an organization or person who sells it instead of a place where it is sold.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa464).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa464,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
