x_comment(x_typesGreaterInQuantity,"(#$typesGreaterInQuantity COL1 COL2 QUANT-TYPE) means\nthat instances of COL1 have more of QUANT-TYPE than instances of COL2.  This cashes out as meaning every instance of COL11 is related to a value of QUANT-TYPE that is greater than any value had by an instance of COL2.  So, for any VAL1, VAL2, if (QUANTITY-PRED INSTANCE-OF-COL1 VAL1) and (QUANTITY-PRED INSTANCE-OF-COL2 VAL2) and VAL1 and VAL2 are both instances of QUANT-TYPE, then (#$followingValue VAL2 VAL1).  Note: QUANTITY-PRED in the above explanation is supposed to be the canonical quantity attribution predicate for use with QUANT-TYPE.  \nExample: (#$typesGreaterInQuantity #$Dog #$Cat #$IntelligenceLevel) means that dogs have higher levels of intelligence than cats.  This entails that any value for argument 2 in #$hasIntelligenceLevel when an instance of #$Dog is in argument 1 is higher than when an instance of #$Cat is in argument 1.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa52b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa52b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa52b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa52b,22995).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa52b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa52b,[]).
x_comment(x_similarInQuantityRelation,"This predicate relates two things in terms of some #$BinaryPredicate that relates them to similar instances of a given collection of #$Quantity[ies]. (#$similarInQuantityRelation T1 T2 BINPRED) means that there are similar instances of #$Quantity QUANT1 and QUANT2, such that (BINPRED T1 QUANT1) and (BINPRED T2 QUANT2). See also #$resemblesInQuantity.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa52c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa52c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
