x_comment(x_normalityForAgent,"The collection of all things by how normal a thing is to a particular agent.  (#$normalityForAgent OBJ COL AGT NORMALITY) means that for the observer AGT, OBJ has the 'normality level' NORMALITY for an element of collection COL. For example (#$normalityForAgent 'Piranha001' #$Fish Charles #$Exotic-Unusual) means that for Charles, the 'Piranha001' is an exotic fish. The COLL attribute is important here: 'Piranha001' could.  be a perfectly normal piranha, yet still be an exotic fish.  Normality is different from but related to frequency (e.g. #$frequencyOfActionType, #$Frequency). For example, a mechanic sees broken cars every day; such cars are common for the mechanic, but not for the general population.  To assert the normality of a thing for a normal observer, see #$normalityOfThing.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa541).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa541,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa541,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa541,23019).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa541,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa541,[]).
x_comment(x_identicalInQuantity,"(#$identicalInQuantity THING1 THING2 QUANT-TYPE) means that the two things THING1 and THING2 have the same values of the quantity type  QUANT-TYPE.  Here 'the same' means complete identity or very close resemblance -- what this means, and what the standard of closeness or identity is, depends on the context, but #$identicalInQuantity and #$differentInQuantity cannot both be correctly asserted of the same two things.  See also #$resemblesInQuantity.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa542).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa542,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa542,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa542,23020).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa542,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa542,[]).
x_comment(x_intimatelyAcquaintedWith,"(#$intimatelyAcquaintedWith X Y) means that X and Y are very closely acquainted and would probably know a lot about each other.  This does NOT mean that they have intimate relations.  This is the level of acquaintance we would expect for #$cohabitingFamilyMembers, #$spouses, very close friends, etc.  We would also expect X and Y to be in frequent contact in the indefinite present, but this is not necessarily true.  We can say, though, that if X and Y are now intimately acquainted, they probably spent a lot of time together at some (perhaps undetermined) time in the past.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa543).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa543,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa543,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa543,23021).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa543,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa543,[]).
x_comment('x_Custom-Made',"The collection of all things which are unique and made for one customer, possibly according to that customer's specifications; made to order.  Instances include tailored clothes, suspension bridges, skyscrapers, and some meals.  Tract houses, manufactured houses, and (most) mobile homes are not instances of this collection, although ") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa544).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa544,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
