xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5c3,[]).
x_comment(x_importanceOfThingInSet,"(#$importanceOfThingInSet THING SET GENERIC-VALUE-PRED) means\nthat of the elements of SET, THING is an important member by some measure of 'importance' that is legitimate in the current microtheory.  Assertions\nabout 'major', 'moderate', 'low' importance of THING should have\n#$highAmountOf, #$mediumAmountOf, and #$lowAmountOf for GENERIC-VALUE-PRED. Importance is a highly\ncontext dependent notion.  This context dependence may be determined\nby the goals of the current conversation, or by the properties of the\n#$Microtheory that a given #$importanceOfThingInSet assertion occurs\nin, or by some implicit agent's judgements about what is important, or\nsome other factors.  Concluding importance is a heuristic matter,\ni.e. there are often not hard and fast rules concluding\n#$importanceOfThingInSet except in more narrowly defined contexts.\nThus, it would probably not be correct to write a rule such as 'If\n?STATE is one of the 10 biggest U.S. states its importance is high.'\nunless, for example, such an assertion were stated in a conversational\nmicrotheory where conversational agents share the belief that 'the\nbigger a thing is, the more important it is'.  On the other hand, a\nrule such as 'If ?STATE is one of the 10 oldest U.S. states its\nimportance is high.'  might be of some worth in the 'HistoriansMt' in\nwhich history experts would be identified as the aforementioned\nimplicit importance evaluating agents.  Finally, a rule such as 'If\n?STATE is one of the 10 most populous U.S. state's then its importance\nis high' is a rule that could be re-used in a wider variety of\napplications.  The original motivation of this constant was to preserve the level of vagueness in a natural language statement such as 'Houston is \nan important oil port.'") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5c4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5c4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5c4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5c4,23181).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5c4,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5c4,[]).
x_comment(x_typicalVolumeOfOutputOfTypeOverDuration,"(#$typicalVolumeOfOutputOfTypeOverDuration EVTTYPE OBJTYPE AMOUNT\nDURATION) says that typically, instances of EVTTYPE produce AMOUNT of\nOBJTYPE as its #$outputsCreated over DURATION. For example,\n(#$typicalVolumeOfOutputOfTypeOverDuration MilkingACowByHand #$CowsMilk-Product\n#$lowAmountOf #$AFewMinutesDuration).  The 'Lowness' here is relative to some\n unspecified parent collection (say, MilkingACow) whose typical level of output  over #$AFewMinutesDuration is\nrelatively high.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5c5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5c5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5c5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5c5,23205).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5c5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5c5,[]).
x_comment(x_evidentialSupportForPhenomena,"(#$evidentialSupportForPhenomena AGT PHENOM GVP)\n allows us to say that AGT has\n  level associated with GVP of evidence supporting AGT's belief in the existence of\n  PHENOM, without requiring us to spell this out\n in terms of the propositional content of AGT's belief.\n For example, (#$evidentialSupportForPhenomena #$FoxMulder-FBIAgent \n  #AlienBeing #$highToVeryHighAmountOf) says that Fox Mulder\n has a great deal of evidence supporting his belief in AlienBeings.\n  To say that someone has evidence for his or her\n belief *that* something is the case, see #$evidentialSupportForBelief.)\n  #$evidentialSupportForPhenomena is useful for parsing English \n sentences, which frequently omit references to\n  the belief that the evidence supports, and often \n contain only references to the object \nof the supported belief.  See also #$evidentialSupportForBelief.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5c6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5c6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5c6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5c6,23212).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5c6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5c6,[]).
x_comment(x_levelOfCompatibility,"(#$levelOfCompatibility ?OLD ?NEW ?LEVEL) means that ?NEW can do anything that ?OLD can do, with a degree of sureness specified by ?LEVEL.  This predicate should be used when two things are less than completely compatible.  See also #$compatibleWithIn and #$upwardCompatibleProgram.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5c7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5c7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5c7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
