xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5cf,[]).
x_comment(x_istMakesLikelihood,"(#$istMakesLikelihood INDPROP DEPPROP PROB) means that the\ntruth of INDPROP in the context of the assertion makes the likelihood\nthat DEPROP is also true in the context of the assertion PROB.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5d0).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5d0,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5d0,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5d0,23241).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5d0,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5d0,[]).
x_comment(x_JoinPathsIntoCycleFn,"An instance of #$UnaryFunction.  When applied to a #$PathChain of the form (#$TheList POINT(1) PATH(1) ... POINT(n) PATH(n) POINT(n+1)) where the concatenation of all PATH(k)s results in a #$Path-Cyclic, #$JoinPathsIntoCycleFn returns as its value the #$Path-Cyclic obtained by that concatenation.  Note that #$JoinPathsIntoCycleFn is a partial function.  Note also that in order for (#$JoinPathsIntoCycleFn (#$TheList POINT(1) PATH(1) ... POINT(n) PATH(n) POINT(n+1))) to be defined, all PATH(k)s must be different instances of #$Path-Simple, and all POINT(k)s must be different, except POINT(1) = POINT(n+1).  Note, finally, that in a #$PathSystem, it is usually convenient to use nodes and links in the system as items in the list, as opposed to including non-node points along links.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5d1).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5d1,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5d1,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5d1,23244).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5d1,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5d1,[]).
x_comment(x_wantsToRelnChange,"`Change' here means change in cardinality.  (#$wantsToRelnChange AGT COL PRED GVP) means that AGT #$desires to be in the relation PRED with the amount suffested by GVP; more [#$highAmountOf] or fewer [#$lowAmountOf] instances of the collection COL.  PRED is a #$BinaryPredicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5d2).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5d2,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5d2,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5d2,23245).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5d2,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5d2,[]).
x_comment(x_CausingAnotherObjectsTranslationalMotion,"A specialization of both #$ActionOnObject and #$Movement-TranslationEvent.  In each instance of #$CausingAnotherObjectsTranslationalMotion, one object causes another object to undergo a translational motion.  The object causing the motion is related to the event by #$providerOfMotiveForce (q.v.), while the object which is caused to move is related by #$objectActedOn and #$objectMoving (qq.v.).  One way to cause another object's motion is to carry it along with oneself (see the specialization #$CarryingWhileLocomoting); other ways include throwing it, kicking it, or knocking it away.  Some instances  of #$CausingAnotherObjectsTranslationalMotion may also be instances of #$Translation-Complete (such as instances of #$CarryingWhileLocomoting), whereas others (such as instances of #$PumpingFluid) may be instances of #$FluidFlow-Translation.  Instances of #$CausingAnotherObjectsTranslationalMotion include a train transporting passengers, a person pushing a coin into a vending machine, the release of a bowstring which propels an arrow, and a magnet attracting a nail.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5d3).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5d3,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5d3,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5d3,23246).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5d3,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5d3,[]).
x_comment(x_BaseballInning,"#$BaseballInning is a specialization of #$Event-Organized and #$AthleticActivity.  Each instance of #$BaseballInning is a subevent of some instance of #$BaseballGame (q.v.), or of some attempt to complete an instance of #$BaseballGame.  In Major League Baseball a complete baseball game consists of nine innings, unless it is extended due to a tie score after the ninth, or shortened (1) because the home team needs none of its half of the ninth inning or only a fraction of it, or (2) because the umpire calls the game. (Note that National Association leagues may adopt a rule providing that one or both games of a doubleheader shall be seven innings in length. In such games, any of these rules applying to the ninth inning shall apply to the seventh inning.). If a game is called, it is a regulation game: (1) If five innings have been completed; (2) If the home team has scored more runs in four or four and a fraction half innings than the visiting team has scored in five completed half innings; (3) If the home team scores one or more runs in its half of the fifth inning to tie the score. (d) If each team has the same number of runs when the game ends, the umpire shall declare it a \"Tie Game.\" (e) If a game is called before it has become a regulation game, the umpire shall declare it \"No Game.\"  Each instance of #$BaseballInning consists of at least two instances of #$HalfInning (q.v). During each #$BaseballInning, each #$BaseballTeam takes its turn trying to score baseball runs.  A team's turn during an inning is called a #$HalfInning.  By tradition, between the two half innings of the seventh inning of a game, there occurs an instance of #$MassOrganizedStretch performed by the members of the game's audience.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5d4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5d4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5d4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5d4,23247).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5d4,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5d4,[]).
x_comment(x_helpfulActions,"The predicate #$helpfulActions is used to indicate that an agent engaged in a particular effort would (normally) increase the chances of success in that effort by taking a certain role in events of a certain kind.  (#$helpfulActions ENDEAVOR SIT-TYPE ROLE DEGREE) means that the #$PurposefulAction ENDEAVOR would (normally) be helped along if its agent participated as described by ROLE in a situation of the type SIT-TYPE.  DEGREE is an instance of #$GenericValuePredicate used to suggest how helpful such an occurrence would be for succeeding in ENDEAVOR.  For example, for an agent trying to win in a football or soccer game, performing instances of #$InterceptingAPass contributes to a #$highToVeryHighAmountOf success in that game.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5d5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5d5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5d5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5d5,23248).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5d5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5d5,[]).
x_comment(x_RelationAllExistsFn,"An #$IndeterminateTermDenotingFunction (q.v.) that is closely related to #$relationAllExists.  If both (#$relationAllExists PRED INDEP-COL DEP-COL) and (#$isa INDEP-INS INDEP-COL) hold,  then (PRED INDEP-INS (#$RelationAllExistsFn INDEP-INS PRED INDEP-COL DEP-COL) also holds.  Thus the term '(#$RelationAllExistsFn INDEP-INS PRED INDEP-COL DEP-COL)' \"indeterminately denotes\" some instance of DEP-COL that is related to INDEP-INS by PRED.  For instance, since (#$relationAllExists #$citizens #$Country #$Person) and (#$isa #$Malaysia #$Country) both hold, so does (#$citizens #$Malaysia (#$RelationAllExistsFn #$Malaysia #$citizens #$Country #$Person)).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5d6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5d6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5d6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5d6,23249).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5d6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5d6,[]).
x_comment(x_usefulActorAttribute,"For the event Arg1, it is useful if any entry on Arg1's Arg2 slot has a value of Arg4 for its Arg3 slot.  Arg5 describes how useful.  (Note that if Arg5 is Low or #$veryLowAmountOf that does not just mean it is not useful, that means it is detrimental.)  For example:  (#$usefulActorAttribute Surgery #$objectActedOn constitutionLevel Strong #$highAmountOf) means its Highly useful for the #$objectActedOn in a Surgery event to have an Strong Constitution.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5d7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5d7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5d7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5d7,23250).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5d7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5d7,[]).
x_comment('x_pathConcatenationOf-Simple',"An instance of #$BinaryPredicate. (#$pathConcatenationOf-Simple CHAIN PATH) means that PATH is the #$Path-Simple obtained by 'path-concatenating' all of the instances of #$Path-Simple in the #$PathChain CHAIN. See also #$JoinPathsFn .") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5d8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5d8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5d8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5d8,23251).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5d8,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5d8,[]).
x_comment(x_PurposefulActionFeature,"A collection of collections.  Each instance of \n         #$PurposefulActionFeature is a collection of instances of #$PurposefulAction.  These collections can be used to meaningfully ascribe to purposeful actions various features; e.g. that they are successful or unsuccessful,\n          well-planned or spontaneous, important or of no consequence, etc..") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5d9).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5d9,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5d9,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5d9,23252).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5d9,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5d9,[]).
x_comment(x_SuccessfulAction,"The collection of purposeful actions that are considered \"successful\".  \"Success\" is a context dependent notion, but for the purposes of this constant can be thought to be relativized to the peformer of the action.  So, a purposeful action is an instance of this collection just in case the performer considered it successful given the reasons for (or the goals of) performing the action.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5da).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5da,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5da,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5da,23255).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5da,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5da,[]).
x_comment(x_significantSubEvents,"'(#$significantSubEvents RESULT-TYPE COMPONENT-TYPE PERF-QUANT-TYPE QUANTITY)' means that performing acts of type COMPONENT-TYPE with the quantity QUANTITY is required for the performance of RESULT-TYPE to have a high degree of PERF-QUANT-TYPE.  For example, '(#$significantSubEvents #$SpeedSkatingSportsEvent #$BodyMovementEvent #$Effectiveness (#$HighToVeryHighAmountFn #$Agility))' means that performing a speed skating sporting event with a high degree of effectiveness requires performing some body movement events with a high to very high amount of agility.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5db).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5db,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5db,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5db,23256).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5db,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5db,[]).
x_comment(x_performanceLevel,"This predicate relates performers to particular individual actions.  (#$performanceLevel OBJ EVT ROLE QUANT) means that the individual OBJ plays the role ROLE in the action EVT, and does so with the quantity indicated by QUANT.  Such an assertion expresses actual performance in a particular action; for example, (#$performanceLevel Joe DiggingHole54001 #$performedBy (#$LowAmountFn #$Strength)) means Joe exerted a low level of strength while digging that particular hole.  This does not imply much about Joe's potential to exert strength, about strength required in general for hole-digging, etc., it just talks about that one single event.  To talk about typical or expected performance, use #$skillLevel.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5dc).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5dc,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5dc,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5dc,23257).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5dc,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5dc,[]).
x_comment(x_skillRequired,"(#$skillRequired ACT-TYPE OTHER-TYPE PERF-LEVEL) means that if one is to successfully perform an instance of some kind of action (ACT-TYPE), then one must be capable of performing instances of another kind of action (OTHER-TYPE) with the performance attribute at a level at least equal to PERF-LEVEL .  For example, (#$skillRequired PlayingVolleyball ServingAVolleyball (#$LowAmountFn #$Competence)) holds just in case playing volleyball requires at least a low amount of competence in serving a volleyball.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5dd).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5dd,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5dd,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5dd,23258).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5dd,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5dd,[]).
x_comment(x_DissidentGroup,"The collection of groups of people which are openly opposed to some authority.  Since being a dissident is essentially a relationship, for any dissident group that relationship should be explicitly stated using #$hasDissidentStatusIn.  For example: (#$hasDissidentStatusIn #$CommitteeForDefenseOfLegitimateRights #$SaudiArabia).  Some instances of #$DissidentGroup will also be instances of #$Organizations, being instances of #$PoliticalOrganization, #$ReligiousOrganization, or both.  Some instances of #$DissidentGroup will not be instances of #$Organization.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5de).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5de,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5de,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5de,23259).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5de,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5de,[]).
x_comment('x_TruckBomb-VehicleDelivered',"A specialization of #$Bomb-RoadVehicleDelivered.  Each instance of #$TruckBomb-VehicleDelivered is a bomb contained in a truck.  Typically a truck containing a #$TruckBomb-VehicleDelivered is left near the intended target and detonated remotely or by a timer device.  Truck bombs are weapons with only terrorist applications.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5df).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5df,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5df,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5df,23260).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5df,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5df,[]).
x_comment('x_Surveillance-InformationGatheringActivity',"The collection of all information-gathering  activities in which the activities of an    agent or the activities in and around a place   are monitored with some regularity.  This   is a subtly different activity from     reconnaissance, which primarily involves     travelling to an area in order to gather    information about its usefulness as a     strategic resource, or travelling to an   enemy site to determine information about    the enemy that would prove strategic.  An example   of surveillance that is not reconnaissance:    a private eye covertly observing someone   through a window.  An example of  reconnaissance that is not surveillance:    that same private eye, earlier in the day,    looking around    the person's back yard for a    tree to climb, from which to get a good    view of the window.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5e0).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5e0,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5e0,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5e0,23261).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5e0,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5e0,[]).
x_comment(x_MetersPerSecond,"(#$MetersPerSecond NUMBER) is the rate or speed of NUMBER meters per second.  Notice that this result is not presently thought of as incorporating a vector, although it might be modified to do so at some point in the future if this should prove appropriate.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5e1).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5e1,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5e1,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5e1,23262).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5e1,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5e1,[]).
x_comment(x_eyeColor,"This predicate is used to state the eye-color of a human or other animal. (#$eyeColor ANIMAL COLOR) means that at least one of ANIMAL's eyes (specifically its irises, if it has them) has the color COLOR.  For example, (#$eyeColor #$AmyIrving #$PurpleColor) holds.  Since individual members of most species typically do not have eyes of different colors (Siamese cats being one exception), #$eyeColor behaves like a #$FunctionalSlot (q.v.) with respect to most, but not all, types of animals. See also #$HumanEyeColor.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5e2).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5e2,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5e2,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5e2,23263).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5e2,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5e2,[]).
x_comment(x_HandGeometryScanner,"#$HandGeometryScanner is a subset of #$BiometricAuthenticationDevice.  Individuals in the collection           #$HandGeometryScanner scan a streched hand and attempt to match the angles between fingers, the thicknesses of fingers, and the lengths of fingers with recorded representations of them.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5e3).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5e3,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5e3,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5e3,23264).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5e3,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5e3,[]).
x_comment(x_monetaryUnitIssuedBy,"(#$monetaryUnitIssuedBy UNIT GPE) means that\nthe #$UnitOfMoney UNIT is such that all currency representing it is issued by the #$GeopoliticalEntity GPE.  For example, (#$monetaryUnitIssuedBy #$Dinar-Bahrain #$Bahrain), (#$monetaryUnitIssuedBy #$Euro #$EuropeanUnion), (#$monetaryUnitIssuedBy #$MillionDollars #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5e4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5e4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5e4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5e4,23265).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5e4,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5e4,[]).
x_comment(x_PhysicalEvent,"A specialization of #$Event-Localized.  Each instance of #$PhysicalEvent is a spatially localized event involving one or more physical objects or stuffs.  #$PhysicalEvents typically involve interaction among #$PartiallyTangibles.  But note that a physical event might consist in the creation, destruction, movement, or a change in some physical feature of a single salient physical object.  (See #$PhysicalCreationEvent, #$PhysicalDestructionEvent, #$MovementEvent, and #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent.)  For a contrasting (though not necessarily disjoint) collection, see #$StrictlyMentalEvent.  For events that have both physical and mental components, see the collection #$CompositePhysicalAndMentalEvent.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5e5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5e5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5e5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5e5,23267).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5e5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5e5,[]).
x_comment(x_FixedArityFunction,"A specialization of both  #$Function-Denotational and #$FixedArityRelation (qq.v.);  the collection of functions of fixed arity (see #$arity).  Each instance of #$FixedArityFunction is a function that  always takes a certain fixed number of arguments. Most  functions reified in the Cyc ontology are of fixed arity.  For example, #$GroupFn always takes a single argument and  #$BorderBetweenFn always takes two arguments. This  collection is disjoint with #$VariableArityFunction.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5e6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5e6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa5e6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa5e6,23268).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa5e6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa5e6,[]).
x_comment(x_SlotTrendPredicate,"Instances of #$SlotTrendPredicate are used to describe trends of some quantity. Some of them make explicit the cause of the trend.  Some make explicit the time period over which those trends occur.   #$SlotTrendPredicates, by definition, use the zeroth argument position to indicate the kind of change.  Net increases, decreases, or no net change are the only kinds of change currently (Feb '98) expressible with these constants.  For a net increase see predicates whose names begin with 'increase', for a net decrease see predicates whose names begin with 'decrease'.  For no net change see #$remainsStableImmediately.  See also #$NoteAboutQuantityChangePredicates.  A negative exemplar of a #$SlotTrendPredicate is #$quantityChangeFunction.  Although this predicate is useful for representing trends in quantity changes, the kind of change occurring is referenced not by its zeroth argument but by its fourth argument (a specialization of #$Function-Denotational).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5e7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5e7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
