x_comment(x_doneViaDoing,"This predicate relates two events #$doneBy the same agent such that s/he does the first by virtue of doing the  second.  (#$doneViaDoing EVENT1 EVENT2) means that some agent  fills the #$doneBy actor slot (or a specialization thereof)  with respect to EVENT2 and thereby fills the #$doneBy actor slot  (or a specialization thereof) with respect to EVENT1.  This can happen in at least three different ways.  (1) EVENT2 might  cause EVENT1.  For example, the statement \"John offended Peter  by dressing like Peter's mother\" could be true because of John's  doing a certain act of impersonation that causes an event of  Peter's being offended by John.  (2) EVENT2 might be a proper  subevent of EVENT1.  For example, the statement \"By pressing the  button, the President destroyed Moscow\" could be true and the  button-pressing itself a subevent of the destroying.  (3) EVENT1  and EVENT2 might actually be the _same_ event under different  descriptions, such that \"EVENT1\" is stated to be an instance  of a certain subcollection EVENTTYPE1 of #$Event and \"EVENT2\"  is stated to be an instance of a different (but not disjoint)  subcollection EVENTTYPE2 of #$Event.  EVENTTYPE1 will normally  be a \"broader\" collection that EVENTTYPE2; EVENTTYPE2 might  even be a subcollection of EVENTTYPE1.  For example, the statement  \"Joni communicated by talking\" could be true due to Joni's  performing a #$Talking event that is also a #$Communicating event.   (Although #$Talking is not a proper subcollection of #$Communicating  -- think of talking to oneself -- the latter is \"broader\" than the  former in that talking normally amounts to communicating, and is  but one of many standard ways of communicating.)  #$doneViaDoing is  indeterminate with respect to the distinctions among cases (1) -   (3) above; thus one may assert (#$doneViaDoing EVENT1 EVENT2) without settling the question as to whether EVENT2 causes, is a  subevent of, or is identical with EVENT1.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa5f0).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa5f0,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
