x_comment(x_List,"A specialization of #$Tuple.  Each instance of #$List is a  finite sequence of things with a first and last member-position, with each member-position other than the last having a successor member-position.   As with tuples generally, lists allow for repetition of their members, so that the same item can appear at multiple member-positions in the same list.  A list can be represented formally as a function from a finite index set of counting numbers, beginning with one, into the domain of all #$Things (but note that #$Lists are _not_ explicitly represented as functions in the Cyc ontology).  Unlike an instance of #$Series (q.v.), a list is purely abstract (i.e. both aspatial and atemporal), and the only implied relation between an item and its successor in a list is the successor relation of the list itself.  Technically, #$List is more specific than #$Tuple only in that the index set (see #$tupleIndexSet) for a given list must be the counting numbers in their usual order (or some initial segment thereof), whereas the index set for a tuple, generally speaking, might be any set whatsoever.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa62c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa62c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa62c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa62c,23344).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa62c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa62c,[]).
x_comment(x_PositionsOfSublistInListFn,"An instance of both #$BinaryFunction and #$EvaluatableFunction.  When applied to two lists, LIST1 and LIST2, #$PositionsOfSublistInListFn finds every occurrence of LIST1 as a sublist of LIST2, and returns a list of all the positions in LIST2 (i.e. a list of #$PositiveIntegers) where a copy of LIST1 starts. For any instances LIST1 and LIST2 of #$List, (#$PositionsOfSublistInListFn LIST1 LIST2) is the list LIST* of #$PositiveIntegers defined below.  Let L be the length of LIST1.  The first member of LIST*, if there is any, is the smallest positive integer N1 such that LIST1 = (#$SublistFromToFn LIST2 N1 N1+L).  The second member of LIST*, if there is any, is the second smallest positive integer N2 such that LIST1 = (#$SublistFromToFn LIST2 N2 N2+L).  ...  The last member of LIST* is the greatest positive integer N such that LIST1 = (#$SublistFromToFn LIST2 N N+L).  Note that if LIST1 is not a sublist of LIST2 or LIST2 is #$TheEmptyList, then (#$PositionsOfSublistInListFn LIST1 LIST2) is #$TheEmptyList.  Note also that if LIST1 is #$TheEmptyList while LIST2 is not, then (#$PositionsOfSublistInListFn LIST1 LIST2) returns a list of _all_ the positions in LIST2 (e.g. if LIST2 = (#$TheList A B C), (#$PositionsOfSublistInListFn #$TheEmptyList LIST2) = (#$TheList 1 2 3)).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa62d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa62d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa62d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa62d,23345).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa62d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa62d,[]).
x_comment(x_PositionOfItemInListFn,"A binary #$ScalarDenotingFunction that takes a non-repeating list and any one of its members to the position of the latter within the former.  Where ITEM is a member (see #$memberOfList) of the #$ListWithoutRepetition LIST, (#$PositionOfItemInListFn ITEM LIST) is the integer corresponding to the position of ITEM in LIST (starting from 1).  For example, (#$PositionOfItemInListFn George (#$TheList John Paul George Ringo)) is 3.  Note that if ITEM were _not_ a member of LIST, then an expression of the form (#$PositionOfItemInListFn ITEM LIST) would not semantically well-formed; see the #$interArgReln assertion on the function.  Also note that ITEM cannot appear more than once in LIST, as the latter is constrained to be a #$ListWithoutRepetition.  See also #$PositionsOfSublistInListFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa62e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa62e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa62e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa62e,23346).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa62e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa62e,[]).
x_comment(x_Isomorphism,"A subcollection of #$Function-Denotational.  A function is an instance of #$Isomorphism if and only if it is a homomorphism that is a #$OneToOneFunction and whose inverse (see #$inverseFunctions) is also a homomorphism.  A homomorphism is a structure-preserving mapping from one structure to another.  A structure consists of a domain of ojects together with a function specifying interpretations, with respect to that domain, of the relation symbols, function symbols, and individual symbols of given language.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa62f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa62f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa62f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa62f,23347).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa62f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa62f,[]).
x_comment(x_MassEMailCommunication,"A specialization of #$EMailCommunication.  Each instance of #$MassEMailCommunication is an event in which an email message is successfully sent, and then later received by dozens to thousands of recipients.  Thus every instance of #$MassEMailCommunication has as sub-events both an instance of #$MassEMailSending and many instances of #$EMailReceiving.  Compare this collection with #$SingleEMailCommunication.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa630).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa630,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa630,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
