x_comment(x_PropositionalAttitudeSlot,"A specialization of #$CognitiveAttitudePredicate (q.v.).  A propositional attitude slot is a binary predicate that relates an agent to a proposition to which the agent bears a certain \"de dicto\" attitude (e.g. belief, doubt, knowledge).  More precisely, each #$PropositionalAttitudeSlot takes as arguments an #$IntelligentAgent (and/or in some cases a #$PerceptualAgent) and a #$CycLPropositionalSentence (that is also an #$ELSentence-Assertible), and is used to state that the agent has a certain cognitive attitude toward the propositional content of the sentence.   A defining characeristic of such a predicate -- what makes it de dicto -- is that its second argument-place is \"opaque\" (see #$opaqueArgument): a sentence appearing in that place may _not_ be freely replaced by others that are extensionally (or even logically) equivalent to it.  From the fact that a person believes (de dicto) that Mark Twain wrote \"Tom Sawyer\", for example, it cannot be inferred that s/he believes that Samuel Clemens wrote that book.  Instances of #$PropositionalAttitudeSlot include #$goals, #$beliefs, #$knows, and #$expects.  Cf. #$DeReAttitudePredicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa64c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa64c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa64c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
