x_comment(x_MassNoun,"A #$SpeechPart and a specialization of #$MassNoun-Generic  (q.v.).  This is the collection of all ordinary mass nouns.  Mass nouns are typically used to refer to \"stufflike\" things, such as homogeneous substances or processes/conditions/concepts without subdivisions (e.g. instances, or instances of instances, of #$StuffType (q.v.)).  In English syntax, mass nouns are always singular in form, are preceded in indefinite descriptions with the word 'some' rather than 'a' or 'an', and cannot be preceded with a numeric determiner (e.g. 'three').  Examples of English mass nouns include 'water'and 'glamour'.  Cf #$CountNoun.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa7f1).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa7f1,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa7f1,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
