x_comment(x_GeneticInformationStructure,"A specialization of\n#$AspatialInformationStore. Instances of #$GeneticInformationStructure are\nthe abstract structure of instances of #$GeneticInformation that are\ncarried in the genetic material of a BLO.  For example, instances of\n#$Gene-GIS are the abstract structures instantiated by instances of\n#$Gene-HereditaryUnit. While the genetic information [see\n#$GeneticInformation] can be transferred, copied, inherited or expressed,\nit is said to be encoded [#$structureEncodesGenInfo] in instances of\n#$GeneticInformationStructure, which is abbreviated GIS. Instances of GIS\nare themselves instantiated or realized [#$instantiationOfGIS] in a\nchemical object, where the chemical structure conforms to the abstract\nstructure of the GIS. Each instances of #$GeneticInformationStructure is an\nabstract configuration. Its structure is analyzable in terms of other\nfundamental structures, see #$Nucleotide-GIS. Instances of #$Nucleotide-GIS\nconcatenated in lists form an instance of\n#$NucleicAcidSequence-GIS. Instances of the latter are 'meaningful' since\nthey are the abstract structures that biochemical agents instantiate (see\n#$Gene-GIS)  or the abstract structure of a smaller unit that composes the\nbiological agent (see #$Codon-GIS). For the corresponding family of\nchemical instantiations of GISs, see #$ChemicalGeneticIBT. Note finally\nthat the instances of the collection #$Genome-GIS are lists encoding all of\nthe genetic information carried by one - actual or typical - individual in\nits genetic material, they relate to instances of #$Genome-GenInfo  that\nthey encode via #$structureEncodesGenInfo.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa85c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa85c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa85c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa85c,23941).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa85c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa85c,[]).
x_comment(x_fileContents,"(#$fileContents FILE CONTENT) means that CONTENT is a bit of information contained in the computer file FILE.  Note that this constant represents a broader, more vague concept than that represented by #$subInfoStructures, insofar as the second argument is only restricted to being an instance of #$AspatialInformationStore.  The copy-level analog of this predicate is #$fileCopyContents.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa85d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa85d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa85d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa85d,23965).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa85d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa85d,[]).
x_comment(x_CollectingInformation,"The subcollection of #$Collecting whose instances are events in which instances of #$AspatialInformationStore are collected. #$CollectingInformation  differs from #$InformationGathering in that instances of the former involve more than just the transfer of information.  Instances of #$CollectingInformation may involve actitivites that are not themselves instances of #$InformationTransferEvent. For example, a military reconnaissance flight might be an instance, or subevent of an instance, of #$CollectingInformation, but it involves events - such as the motion of the aircraft - that are not information transfer events.  In addition,  instances of #$CollectingInformation involve the creation of an instance of #$InformationBearingObject, which need not be the case for instances of #$InformationGathering.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa85e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa85e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa85e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa85e,23966).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa85e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa85e,[]).
x_comment(x_screwedTo,"An instance of #$ConnectionPredicate.  (#$screwedTo OBJ1 OBJ2) means that at least one screw (i.e., instance of #$Screw-Connector) holds OBJ1 to OBJ2.  The screw goes through OBJ1, its threads grab onto OBJ2, but it does not go all the way through OBJ2.  Depending upon the type of screw and style of attachment, the head of the screw may be recessed into, flush with, or raised above the side of OBJ1.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa85f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa85f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa85f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
