x_comment(x_sourceOfTerm,"(#$sourceOfTerm TERM SOURCE) means that the #$CycLIndexedTerm TERM has its origin in #$AspatialInformationStore SOURCE (such as a #$PropositionalConceptualWork, a PIT or a WebPage). If TERM is a constant, it models some important concept used in SOURCE. If it is an assertion, then typically it is an assertion which is true in SOURCE (if source is a PIT), or true in the context of SOURCE (#$ContextOfPCWFn SOURCE), if SOURCE is a #$PropositionalConceptualWork.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa877).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa877,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa877,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa877,23991).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa877,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa877,[]).
x_comment(x_physicalExtent,"A #$PhysicalPartPredicate and specialization of #$physicalParts that relates an object with tangible and intangible parts (see #$CompositeTangibleAndIntangibleObject) to its greatest tangible  part.  (#$physicalExtent WHOLE PART) means that PART is the complete part of WHOLE that is #$PartiallyTangible and not itself a  #$CompositeTangibleAndIntangibleObject (and thus does not include  any intangible part of WHOLE).  For example, the #$physicalExtent  of a given copy of \"Moby Dick\" includes the cover, the pages, and  their markings; it excludes the #$AspatialInformationStore and  the #$ConceptualWork that are also in some sense present in that  copy of \"Moby Dick\".") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa878).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa878,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa878,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa878,23992).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa878,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa878,[]).
x_comment(x_InformationBearingObject,"A specialization of both #$InformationBearingThing and #$CompositeTangibleAndIntangibleObject.  Each instance of #$InformationBearingObject (or \"IBO\") is a physical object that can be interpreted to yield information.  #$InformationBearingObject includes: (1) artifacts made solely for the purpose of conveying information (e.g., a newspaper, or a children's science video); (2) artifacts that convey information in addition to their intended function (e.g., Neolithic pottery); and (3) non-artifacts, such as a person's fingerprints or cellular DNA, which may be interpreted to yield information. See the comment on #$InformationBearingThing on the importance of distinguishing the many specializations of this collection from the many specializations of both #$AspatialInformationStore (information which may be instantiated in particular IBOs -- see #$instantiationOfAIT) and #$ConceptualWork (conceptual and/or artistic creations which may be instantiated in particular IBOs -- see #$instantiationOfWork). An important specialization of this collection (which naturally includes most of the things asserted so far in the Cyc #$KnowledgeBase to be instances of #$InformationBearingObject) is #$HumanAccessibleIBO -- objects which can be interpreted to yield information by #$Persons.  See also #$containsInformation.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa879).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa879,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa879,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa879,23993).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa879,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa879,[]).
x_comment(x_FullUseRights,"A collection of instances of #$Situation.  Each instance of this collection is a situation that consists of someone having full use rights to an object.  See the comments on #$ExclusiveUserRights and #$SituationTypeByUserRights for a fuller explanation of how this is cashed out.  This collection represents the concept of \"full use rights\" with respect to an object meaning that if the #$Agent-Generic has this right to the object, it does not place any restrictions on what the #$Agent-Generic may do with the object.  If there are restrictions on what the #$Agent-Generic may do with the object, they arise from other sources, such as formal and informal laws of behavior.  E.g., even if you have #$FullUseRights to your car, you can't violate traffic laws, kill people with it, blow it up in a parking lot, etc.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa87a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa87a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa87a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa87a,23994).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa87a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa87a,[]).
x_comment(x_PrimaryRoleUseRights,"A collection of instances of #$Situation. Each instance of this collection is a situation that consists of someone having primary role use rights to an object. See the comments on #$ExclusiveUserRights and #$SituationTypeByUserRights for a fuller explanation of how this is cashed out.  This collection represents the use right associated with agents being allowed to use an object in events where the object performs its #$primaryFunction.  A rental agreement typically gives the renter this kind of right to use of an object (such as a car, residence, videotape, etc.)  Just because you rent a car, though, does not give you the right to sell it, burn it up, or repaint it.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa87b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa87b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa87b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa87b,23995).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa87b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa87b,[]).
x_comment(x_proximityConstraints,"(#$proximityConstraints SIT ROLE1 ROLE2 RANGE) means that #$Distance-Absolute RANGE is the range of distance that object OBJ can be from the location in which something plays ROLE2 in #$Situation SIT in order for OBJ to play the role ROLE1 in SIT. Cf. #$proximityConstraints-Type.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa87c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa87c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa87c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa87c,23996).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa87c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa87c,[]).
x_comment('x_in-ContFullOf',"An instance of #$SpatialPredicate.  (#$in-ContFullOf CONTAINED CONTAINER) means that CONTAINED is located in CONTAINER, and, moreover, that CONTAINED fills CONTAINER.  That CONTAINED fills CONTAINER implies that the inside surface of CONTAINER touches CONTAINED at virtually all points of that surface.  In order to fill CONTAINER, CONTAINED must either be something that can easily conform itself to the shape of CONTAINER, or something with a relatively rigid shape that happens to fit CONTAINER.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa87d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa87d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa87d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa87d,23997).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa87d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa87d,[]).
x_comment('x_in-Held',"An instance of #$SpatialPredicate.  (#$in-Held OBJ HOLDER) means that the #$SolidTangibleThing OBJ is being held or restrained by the #$HolderGripper HOLDER, which is touching and applying pressure to OBJ.  HOLDER may be either a device (see #$deviceUsed) or an anatomical part (see #$anatomicalPartTypeAffected) used in an instance of #$HoldingAnObject; in either case, OBJ would be the #$objectActedOn (q.v.).  For example, #$in-Held would be used to relate salad held by tongs, a bread crumb in an ant's pincers, a bird in a hand, or a long two-by-four in a vise.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa87e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa87e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa87e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa87e,23998).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa87e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa87e,[]).
x_comment(x_DaytimeHours,"A specialization of #$QualitativeTimeOfDay. #$DaytimeHours is the collection of regularly occurring celestial events during which the #$Sun is above the horizon.  This collection is the temporal complement of #$Night -- every instance of #$DaytimeHours is #$contiguousAfter one instance of #$Night and vice versa (except for days in which the #$Sun neither rises nor sets).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa87f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa87f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa87f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa87f,23999).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa87f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa87f,[]).
x_comment(x_CalendarMinute,"An instance of #$CalendarCoveringType, and a specialization of #$Date.  Each instance of #$CalendarMinute is a minute on a particular calendar. For example, the first minute of the year 2000 is an instance of #$CalendarMinute, representable as (#$MinuteFn 1 (#$HourFn 0 (#$DayFn 1 (#$MonthFn #$January (#$YearFn 2000))))).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa880).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa880,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa880,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa880,24000).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa880,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa880,[]).
x_comment(x_SubLSymbol,"The collection of all #$SubLAtoms that are also \"symbols\" in the SubL language.  That is, each instance of #$SubLSymbol satisfies the #$defnIff SYMBOLP.  Note that `symbol' has a very specific, technical meaning in SubL.  Save for those that are variables (see #$SubLVariable), #$SubLSymbols are rarely used in CycL assertions, except within those built with certain #$CycInferenceDescriptorPredicates like #$defnIff. Examples of SubL symbols include the symbols 'GENLS' and 'CYC-SYSTEM-NON-VARIABLE-SYMBOL-P'. Note also that #$SubLSymbol is a \"quoted-collection\" (see #$quotedCollection).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa881).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa881,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa881,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa881,24001).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa881,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa881,[]).
x_comment('x_in-Permeates',"An instance of #$SpatialPredicate.  (#$in-Permeates LIQUID SOLID) means that the #$LiquidTangibleThing LIQUID is diffused throughout the #$SolidTangibleThing SOLID.  Note that LIQUID can be separated from SOLID by physical (in contrast to chemical) means.  For example, #$in-Permeates would be used to relate the water in a wet sponge to the sponge itself.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa882).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa882,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa882,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa882,24002).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa882,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa882,[]).
x_disjointWith(x_CalendarMinute,x_CalendarWeekend) :- x_cid(a65b16526aa883).
