x_comment(x_sitEnablesAgentTo,"#$sitEnablesAgentTo is a generalization of all enables-* predicates for which the #$arg1Isa #$Situation, with the exception of #$enables-ImpartsSkills. Motivation: occasionally one needs to label a broad class of events as generically `enabling', i.e., as events that enable an agent to do something in one or the other sense of enabling. This predicate should be used sparingly: When describing the enabling relation between a specific event or type of event, an agent and a specific action or type of action, one should use the specific enables-* predicate appropriate to that circumstance.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa891).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa891,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa891,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa891,24017).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa891,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa891,[]).
x_comment('x_maximalConstraint-Genls',"(#$maximalConstraint-Genls PRED N COL) says that the known maximal constraint for the Nth argument place of PRED is a spec of COL.  Here, 'maximal constraint' means that typically, the best or most sensible collection, any spec of which could fill the Nth argument place, is COL.  The maximal #$genls constraint for a predicate need not always be determined by its #$argGenl assertions, though sometimes it will be (when it its not, the maximal #$genls constraint is determined by the predicates #$typicalArgIsa assertions).  An example of a predicate for which the #$argGenls assertions do not determine a maximal #$genls constraint could be, #$primaryFunction, whose #$arg2Genl is #$Situation.  This isn't maximal, since much, much more often than not, the collection we should use with #$primaryFunction will be a spec of the more specific collection, #$Event.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa892).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa892,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa892,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa892,24018).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa892,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa892,[]).
x_comment(x_PreparednessFn,"#$PreparednessFn takes as argument any spec SITTYPE of #$Situation and returns the collection of all instances of #$PreparingForUndesirableSituation that are preparings for SITTYPE. For example, (#$PreparednessFn #$DisasterEvent) denotes the collection of all preparations for natural disasters. See also #$PreparingForUndesirableSituation.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa893).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa893,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa893,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa893,24019).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa893,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa893,[]).
x_comment(x_PreparingForUndesirableSituation,"The collection of #$PurposefulActions in which an agent prepares for a possible situation (#$Situation) that is abnormal, undesirable, and forseeable from the point of view of the agent. The action can be entirely mental, as in #$PlanningToDoSomething, or can involve #$CompositePhysicalAndMentalEvents, such as buying extra food as a preparation for a possible supermarket shortage. To relate #$PreparingForUndesirableSituations to the #$Situations that they prepare for, use the function #$PreparednessFn. See also #$PreventFromHappening and #$PreventionFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa894).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa894,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa894,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa894,24020).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa894,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa894,[]).
x_comment(x_DangerousSituation,"A specialization of #$Situation. In each instance of #$DangerousSituation there is a significant risk of death, injury, or property damage. Instances of #$DangerousSituation which actually result in large numbers of deaths or injuries or large amounts of property damage, are instances of #$DisasterEvent.  If a #$DangerousSituation naturally occurs, it is an instance of #$NaturalHazard.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa895).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa895,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa895,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa895,24021).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa895,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa895,[]).
x_comment(x_SituationTypeTernaryPredicate,"A specialization of #$TernaryPredicate.  Each instance of #$SituationTypeTernaryPredicate is a ternary predicate that takes an instance of #$SomethingExisting as its first argument, a subcollection of #$Situation as its second argument, and an instance of #$Role as its third argument.  Many of these predicate are used to express some version of capability; others may be used to delineate options or potential duties of an agent.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa896).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa896,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa896,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa896,24022).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa896,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa896,[]).
x_comment(x_MentalSituation,"A specialization of #$Situation. Each instance of #$MentalSituation is a mental situation such as a person seeing the color red, or believing that his enemies control the CIA, or desiring that Fidel Castro shave his beard. An important subcollection of this collection is #$AtLeastPartiallyMentalEvent whice is the collection of mental situations that are events, such as thinking about something or tasting something. Other instances of #$MentalSituation are more \"long-standing\" and stable, and hence also instances of #$StaticSituation, e.g., believing something, or having some goal. Cf. #$MentalSituationFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa897).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa897,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa897,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa897,24023).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa897,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa897,[]).
x_comment(x_instrumentalRole,"This predicate is a generalization of #$instrument-Generic that relates an event (or other temporal situation) to an  individual that plays an instrumental role in it (or in bringing  it about).  (#$instrumentalRole SIT INSTR) means that INSTR is used (in a broad sense of `used') by the \"doer\" DOER of SIT in order to \"do\" (or bring about) SIT.  DOER will usually (but  not necessarily) be an #$Agent-Generic.  If SIT is an #$Event,  DOER is s/he who (or that which) fills the #$doneBy actor slot  with respect to SIT.  If SIT is a #$StaticSituation, DOER is  somebody who (or something that) is both a primary  #$situationConstituents of SIT and has played a salient causal  role in bringing SIT about (see #$causalActors).  Consider a  situation of George W. Bush napping on his cot; whether this is  construed as an event or as a static situation, Bush would be its  \"doer\" in the present sense and the cot would be playing an  #$instrumentalRole.  If SIT is an event and INSTR is partially  tangible, use #$instrument-Generic or a specialization thereof.   But if SIT is a static situation or INSTR is an intangible  individual (such as a #$CommunicationConvention or a #$Letter-CW),  #$instrumentalRole itself might need to be used.  For a type-level  version of this predicate, see #$instrumentalRole-Type.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa898).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa898,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa898,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa898,24024).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa898,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa898,[]).
x_comment(x_PhysiologicalCondition,"A specialization of #$SystemCondition.  Each instance of #$PhysiologicalCondition involves an #$Organism-Whole being in some physiological state that is not  typical for that organism's species, modulo considerations about the organism's sex or (in some cases) subspecies.  Thus #$Menstruating, though not typical among all persons, is part of being a healthy adult female, and so is not a specialization of #$PhysiologicalCondition (though it is a type of #$PhysiologicalProcess); #$Pregnancy, on the other hand, is a specialization of #$PhysiologicalCondition.  Note that some but not all\n#$PhysiologicalConditions are events; but they are all #$Situations.\nThus each physiological condition has a temporal start and end, though in some cases (e.g. dwarfism) these will be the same as the start and end of the life of the organism.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa899).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa899,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa899,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa899,24025).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa899,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa899,[]).
x_comment(x_StaticSituation,"A specialization of #$Situation.  Each instance of #$StaticSituation is a state of affairs between two or more things, persisting statically over some time interval. Instances of #$StaticSituation always have a temporal extent, and they usually have a tangible and spatial extent.   As an example, consider the situation of Bill Clinton sitting in his easy chair on the evening of 7/4/96.   There are participant objects such as Bill and the chair, there are relationships such as the seat of the chair supporting his bottom and his weight being off his feet, etc.   In any #$StaticSituation, for the participants in that situation, there is some significant or focal relationship between them which does not change.  In the most typical cases, there is no important change whatsoever, e.g. someone sitting would be such a #$StaticSituation. But some things represented as #$StaticSituations can alternatively be represented as #$Events.  For example, a situation in which geese were flying in a flock would be static (the flock-like spatial relationship between the  geese would be retained) but it would also be dynamic in that the geese were moving, so either representation could be chosen depending on the context.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa89a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa89a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa89a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa89a,24028).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa89a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa89a,[]).
x_comment(x_actorCapacity,"The predicate #$actorCapacity is used to indicate the capacity in which some entity participates in a particular event or situation.  (#$actorCapacity ENTITY SIT CAP) means that ENTITY has an (unspecified) role in SIT with the #$CapacityPredicate CAP.  An important difference between the #$CapacityPredicates and this predicate is that the #$CapacityPredicates are type level with respect to the event -- that is, they take an event type as their third argument while #$actorCapacity takes an instance of #$Situation as its second argument.  An important use of #$actorCapacity is with the capacity predicates #$actsInCapacity-MainFunction or #$actsInCapacity-IntendedFunction to state that a device is serving its #$primaryFunction or intended function (i.e., #$intendedBehaviorCapable) in a certain situation.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa89b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa89b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa89b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa89b,24029).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa89b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa89b,[]).
x_comment(x_appropriateEmotion,"(#$appropriateEmotion SIT ROLE QUANT) means that in the #$Situation SIT, an intelligent agent filling the #$Role ROLE would be expected to feel the quantity of emotion described by QUANT .  If such an agent did not feel that emotion, s/he would generally be considered rude or strange. E.g., (#$HighAmountFn #$Grief) is an #$appropriateEmotion for someone in the audience at a funeral (but not for the workers, the deceased, etc.)") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa89c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa89c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa89c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa89c,24030).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa89c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa89c,[]).
x_comment(x_CycKB,"A specialization of #$KnowledgeBase.  #$CycKB is the collection of all Cyc knowledge bases.  Each instance of #$CycKB is a formalized representation of a vast quantity of fundamental human knowledge: facts, rules of thumb, and heuristics for reasoning about the objects and events of everyday life.  Each copy of #$CycProgram (i.e., each instance of #$CycTheCollection) contains the information represented in exactly one instance of #$CycKB. The Cyc KB (like the #$CycProgram) changes frequently.  Also, #$CycKBs can be specialized for different users and uses.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa89d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa89d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa89d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa89d,24031).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa89d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa89d,[]).
x_comment(x_evaluationDefn,"A #$CycInferenceDescriptorPredicate and #$InferenceRelatedBookkeepingPredicate that is used to relate an Cyc-evaluatable function or predicate to the name of the piece of code that is used to evaluate it.  (#$evaluationDefn RELN NAME) means that the #$SubLSymbol NAME is the name of a piece of Heuristic Level (SubL) code in the Cyc system that is used to compute the value of closed CycL formulas built from (the CycL name of) the #$EvaluatableRelation RELN.  For example, the sentence '(#$evaluationDefn #$MinusFn CYC-MINUS)' is asserted in the Knowledge Base.'") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa89e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa89e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa89e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa89e,24032).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa89e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa89e,[]).
x_comment(x_hangsAround,"An instance of #$ConnectionPredicate.  (#$hangsAround LOOP OBJ) means that the at least somewhat flexible #$SolidTangibleThing LOOP is looped around a piece of the #$SolidTangibleThing OBJ.  LOOP is supported by gripping friction or by LOOP's limited ability to elongate as it moves down on OBJ.  See also the generalization #$hangsFrom.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa89f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa89f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa89f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa89f,24033).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa89f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa89f,[]).
x_comment(x_InterAgentSituation,"The class of static situations that exist as the result of some relationship(s) between some plurality of agents.  For instance, world peace in which all countries in the world are at peace with one another.  See #$InterAgentSituation-TwoAgent for a specialization involving only two agents.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa8a0).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa8a0,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa8a0,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
