x_comment(x_cooperatingParticipants,"This predicate can be used to indicate that a particular agent participates in a particular cooperation event in order to further the goals of that event.  (#$cooperatingParticipants COOP AGENT) means that AGENT participates in COOP in such a way as to further the goal(s) of   COOP, and does so at least in part for that very reason.  (Note that AGENT might also have other reasons for participating in COOP.)  AGENT is both a #$socialParticipants and a #$deliberateActors in COOP, and there is at least one other AGENT2 such that  (#$partnersInCooperation AGENT AGENT2 COOP) holds.  Example-positive: the #$UnitedStatesArmy cooperating with the Red Cross in an instance of #$ReliefOperation.  (But note that the individual soldiers involved in the operation might not be #$cooperatingParticipants.)   Example-negative: a construction worker helping to build a post office, not so that there will be a post office in that area, but in order to earn  money to feed her family.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa955).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa955,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa955,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa955,24255).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aa955,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aa955,[]).
x_comment(x_partnersInCooperation,"(#$partnersInCooperation AGT1 AGT2 COOP) means that AGT1 and AGT2 are #$IntelligentAgents who cooperate with each other in the #$CooperationEvent COOP.  Note that there could be more than two agents who cooperate in COOP, but there are at least two. Accordingly, AGT1 and AGT2 must be distinct agents when (#$partnersInCooperation AGT1 AGT2 COOP) holds.  See also #$cooperatingParticipants.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aa956).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aa956,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aa956,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aa956,24256).
