x_comment(x_InverseFunctionFn,"A #$FunctionDenotingFunction that maps a one-to-one  unary function to its inverse.  (#$InverseFunctionFn FUNCTION)  is the one-to-one function INVERSE that is the inverse (see #$inverseFunctions) of FUNCTION.  Thus, for any things X and  Y, FUNCTION maps X to Y if and only if INVERSE maps Y to X.  For example, in a context involving only monogamous  heterosexual relationships, the inverse of the \"WifeFn\"  would be the \"HusbandFn\".  Note that the condition  stated above is necessary but _not_ sufficient for  one function's being the inverse of another.  This is  because #$Function-Denotationals are understood as being \"functions-in-intension\", making it theoretically possible  for numerically distinct (but extensionally equivalent)  functions to satisfy the above condition with respec to a  given function.  Note also that #$InverseFunctionFn is  \"symmetric\" in that if (#$InverseFunctionFn FUNCTION)  = INVERSE then (#$InverseFunctionFn INVERSE) = FUNCTION;  e.g. in the above example  (#$InverseFunctionFn (#$InverseFunctionFn WifeFn)) = WifeFn. Cf. #$inverseQuantFunctions-Nonsymmetric.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaa7f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaa7f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
