xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab4c,[]).
x_comment('x_temporallySubsumes-TypeType',"A #$TemporalPredicate and a specialization of #$temporallyIntersects-TypeType (q.v.) that is a type-level correlate of #$temporallySubsumes (q.v.).  This predicate is used to state that things of one given type always temporally subsume things of another given type.  (#$temporallySubsumes-TypeType LONGTYPE SHORTTYPE) means that for every instance LONGINST of LONGTYPE, there is an instance SHORTINST of SHORTTYPE such that (#$temporallySubsumes LONGINST SHORTINST).\n<p>\nFor example, (#$temporallySubsumes-TypeType #$CalendarQuarter #$CalendarMonth) means that every calendar quarter subsumes at least one entire calendar month.\n<p>\nSee also #$temporallySubsumedBy-TypeType; and note that (#$temporallySubsumedBy-TypeType SHORTTYPE LONGTYPE) is neither necessary nor sufficient for (#$temporallySubsumes-TypeType LONGTYPE SHORTTYPE).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab4d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab4d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab4d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab4d,24910).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab4d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab4d,[]).
x_comment(x_denotation,"A quaternary #$NLSemanticPredicate that relates natural language words to CycL terms to which they correspond in a certain way.  Some words have multiple such denotations due to the fact that they possess multiple #$WordSenses (q.v.), which word-senses might or might not belong to the same parts of speech (i.e. nouns, verbs, adjectives etc.); thus #$denotation also has argument-places for specifying both a #$SpeechPart (q.v.) and a particular word-sense (as represented by an integer understood in relation to some standardized word-sense numbering scheme).\n<p>\n(#$denotation WORD SPEECH-PART N TERM) means that the CycL correlate (or \"denotation\") of WORD, with respect to SPEECH-PART and the corresponding word-sense numbered N, is TERM.  Examples: (1) #$Place-TheWord, an instance of #$EnglishWord, has the denotation #$PuttingSomethingSomewhere when used as a #$Verb with its word-sense 0, and the denotation #$Place when used as a #$CountNoun with that same word-sense; (2) #$Ring-TheWord currently has four denotation assertions in the Knowledge Base: #$EmittingSound (as a #$Verb with its word-sense 1); #$AudibleSound (as a #$CountNoun with its word-sense 1); #$RingShapedObject (as a #$CountNoun with its word-sense 0); and #$Ring-Jewelry (as a #$CountNoun with its word-sense 2).  An example of a #$denotation sentence that has a NAT (see #$CycLNonAtomicTerm) rather than a constant in its fourth argument-position is\n<p>\n    `(#$denotation #$Density-TheWord #$Adjective 0 (#$IntervalMinFn #$LowAmountFn #$Density))'.\n<p>\nNote that #$denotation is _not_ to be confused with #$denotes (q.v.), which relates denoting CycL terms to things \"out there in the world\" (i.e. in the intended model of the CycL language).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab4e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab4e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab4e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab4e,24915).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab4e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab4e,[]).
x_comment('x_motionPathway-Complete',"This predicate can be used to indicate that the trajectory or trajectories travelled by one or more objects during a movement event follow some spatial path throughout the entire movement. More formally, if <code>MOVE</code> is an instance of #$Translation-SingleTrajectory, <code>(#$motionPathway-Complete MOVE PATH)</code> means that the instance  <code>PATH</code> of #$Path-Spatial contains the entire instance of #$Trajectory along which the objects moving (see #$objectMoving) travel in <code>MOVE</code>.  Note that there can be at most one #$motionPathway-Complete in this case.  If <code>MOVE</code> is an instance of #$Translation-MultiTrajectory, <code>(#$motionPathway-Complete MOVE PATH)</code> means that the instance <code>PATH</code> of #$Path-Spatial contains the entire instance of #$Trajectory along which at least one of the objects moving in <code>MOVE</code> travels.  In this case, more than one #$motionPathway-Complete may exist.  (Note that the instance of #$Traversal determined by the #$trajectory-Complete need not occupy the whole length of <code>PATH</code>.) To explain this predicate somewhat differently, any #$motionPathway-Complete of an instance of #$Movement-TranslationEvent connects the starting and stopping points (the #$fromLocation and #$toLocation) of the whole trajectory of at least one object in the movement event (See also #$pathConnects).   Note that <code>PATH</code> may be a marked or unmarked instance of #$Path-Spatial.  See also #$trajectoryPassesThrough and #$motionPathway-Partial. You can use #$trajectoryPassesThrough to state that an object passes through a particular location on the object's trajectory.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab4f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab4f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab4f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab4f,24922).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab4f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab4f,[]).
x_comment(x_AsymmetricBinaryPredicate,"An instance of #$BinaryPredicateTypeByLogicalFeature and a specialization of #$AntiSymmetricBinaryPredicate (q.v.).  A binary predicate <code>BINPRED</code> is an instance of #$AsymmetricBinaryPredicate if and only if (i) for any <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code>, <code>(BINPRED THING1 THING2)</code> implies <code>(#$not (BINPRED THING2 THING1))</code> and (ii) the single-argument argument type (i.e. #$argIsa and #$argGenl) constraints on <code>BINPRED</code>'s two respective argument-places are \"co-satisfiable\" (see #$cosatisfiableInArgs and #$NoteOnArgumentCosatisfiabilityRequirement).\n<p>\nExamples of asymmetric binary predicates include #$northOf, #$children, #$greaterThan, and #$overrides.  Cf. #$SymmetricBinaryPredicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab50).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab50,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab50,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab50,24923).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab50,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab50,[]).
x_comment(x_postEvents,"A #$BinaryRolePredicate (q.v.) that relates one event to another that follows  it.  <code>(#$postEvents BEFORE AFTER)</code> implies two things:\n<p>\n(1) <code>BEFORE</code> occurs strictly before <code>AFTER</code>: <code>AFTER</code> starts after <code>BEFORE</code> has ended (see the generalization of #$postEvents, #$startsAfterEndingOf).\n<p>\n(2) <code>BEFORE</code> and <code>AFTER</code> are significantly <i>relevant</i> to each other.  This required  \"relevance\" of the two arguments to each other is difficult to formalize but  often intuitively clearcut.  Sometimes <code>AFTER</code> is caused by <code>BEFORE</code>.  For  example, we might say (#$postEvents TakingAShower001 ExitingShower001), but  would not say (#$postEvents BolshevikRelvolution DeathOfJohnLennon) since these two events are not at all relevant to each other.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab51).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab51,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab51,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab51,24926).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab51,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab51,[]).
x_comment(x_directingAgent,"An instance of #$ActorSlot (q.v.) that is a  specialization of #$deliberateActors.  <code>(#$directingAgent EVENT DIRECTOR)</code> means that <code>DIRECTOR</code> deliberately controls or directs <code>EVENT</code>, typically by controlling other participants in <code>EVENT</code> (see #$controls).  <code>AGENT</code> might or might not perform <code>EVENT</code> directly.  If an agent other than <code>DIRECTOR</code> performs (see  #$performedBy) <code>EVENT</code>, then that performer's actions in <code>EVENT</code> are controlled by at least one of <code>EVENT</code>'s directing agents.  (In many cases, of course, <code>EVENT</code> will have only one director.)  Non-performing directors typically control the actual performers by communicating instructions (or orders,  commands, etc.) to them.  For example,  (#$directingAgent PSOBicentennialConcertOfBeethovensNinth WilliamSteinberg)  holds, because Steinberg was the conductor of that concert; he directed  the many other musicians who performed in that event.  Other examples are  (#$directingAgent MassacreAtMyLai LtWilliamCalley) and  (#$directingAgent TateLaBiancaMurders CharlesManson).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab52).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab52,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab52,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab52,24931).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab52,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab52,[]).
x_comment(x_interArgIsaGenl,"<code>(#$interArgIsaGenl RELN INDEPENDENT-ARGNUM INDEPENDENT-COL DEPENDENT-ARGNUM DEPENDENT-COL)</code> means that, when an instance of <code>INDEPENDENT-COL</code> appears as the <code>INDEPENDENT-ARGNUM</code><sup>th</sup> argument to <code>RELN</code>, the corresponding <code>DEPENDENT-ARGNUM</code><sup>th</sup> argument must be a subcollection of <code>DEPENDENT-COL</code>, or the result is not semantically well-formed.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab53).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab53,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab53,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab53,24932).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab53,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab53,[]).
x_comment(x_thereExistAtMost,"An #$ExistentialQuantifier (q.v.) and a specialized (albeit higher arity) version of #$thereExists (q.v.).  Compared to the latter, standard existential quantifier, #$thereExistAtLeast has an extra argument-place for specifying how many things (at the most) satisfy a given condition (specified by an #$ELSentence-Assertible, in which the also-specified #$ELVariable will typically occur free).  A closed sentence of the form (#$thereExistAtMost NUM VAR SENT) means that there are at most NUM distinct things that satisfy SENT, i.e. that render SENT true when taken as the value of VAR.  For example, the sentence\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$thereExistAtMost 7 ?X (#$isa ?X #$Sea))\n</pre>\n<p>\nmeans that there are at most seven seas.\n<p>\nIn the degenerate case where VAR does not appear free in SENT, then (#$thereExistAtMost NUM VAR SENT) is equivalent to SENT.\n<p>\nSee also #$thereExistAtLeast, #$thereExistExactly.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab54).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab54,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab54,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab54,24933).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab54,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab54,[]).
x_comment(x_geographicalSubRegions,"<code>(#$geographicalSubRegions SUPER SUB)</code> means that <code>SUPER</code> and <code>SUB</code> are both elements of #$GeographicalRegion, and the area <code>SUB</code> lies wholly within the region <code>SUPER</code> (see #$inRegion).  <code>SUPER</code> may or may not completely surround <code>SUB</code>, as they may share an outer boundary (e.g., the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica and  #$Texas-State in the #$WorldGeographyDualistMt).   Note that, if either argument to #$geographicalSubRegions is an instance of #$GeopoliticalEntity, the assertion must be made in #$WorldGeographyDualistMt or a more specialized microtheory.   To relate two instances of #$GeopoliticalEntity, use the predicate #$geopoliticalSubdivision, if it applies.  See also #$surroundsHorizontally and #$bordersOn.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab55).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab55,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab55,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab55,24944).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab55,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab55,[]).
x_comment(x_arg6Genl,"A binary #$ArgGenlPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$arg6Genl RELN COL)</code>  means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the sixth argument to <code>RELN</code> must be a subcollection of <code>COL</code>.  That is,  <code>(RELN ARG1 ARG2 ARG3 ARG4 ARG5 ARG6 ...)</code> is semantically well-formed only if <code>(#$genls ARG6 COL)</code> holds.  For an explanation of semantic well-formedness, see  #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  See #$argGenl for a  similar but more flexible ternary predicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab56).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab56,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab56,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab56,24945).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab56,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab56,[]).
x_comment(x_requiresMemory,"\n(#$requiresMemory PROGRAM RAM) means that PROGRAM\n  is an instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW and that PROGRAM will only run on\n  a computer that has #$ComputerMemoryCapacity RAM.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab57).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab57,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab57,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab57,24946).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab57,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab57,[]).
x_comment('x_ist-Asserted',"A #$MicrotheoryDesignatingPredicate that is used to relate CycL sentences to microtheories in which they are explicitly asserted. (#$ist-Asserted MT SENT) states that there is a #$CycLAssertion for SENT in the #$Microtheory MT.  This predicate is therefore a more specialized form of #$ist (q.v.) with the additional requirement that there be an actual assertion for SENT in MT.\n<p>\nNote that the above is also more precise than (#$ist MT (#$assertedSentence SENT)), which states that there is an assertion for SENT in some microtheory visible from (see #$genlMt) MT.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab58).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab58,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab58,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab58,24950).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab58,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab58,[]).
x_comment(x_integerRange,"A binary predicate that relates an #$IntegerTypeByRange (q.v.) to the #$IntegerExtent (q.v.) that corresponds to the range of the former.  <code>(#$integerRange INTEGER-TYPE EXTENT)</code> means that <code>EXTENT</code> spans exactly the range of the integers in the collection <code>INTEGER-TYPE</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$integerRange (#$IntegerFromToFn 1 10) (#$Unity 1 10)) holds.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab59).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab59,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab59,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab59,24953).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab59,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab59,[]).
x_comment(x_relationExtension,"A #$MetaRelation predicate that relates a given instance of #$Relation to its set-theoretic extension: the set containing all of and only the sequences of things among which the relation holds. (#$relationExtension RELATION EXTENT) means that the extension of RELATION is EXTENT.  More precisely (and following standard mathematical practice): EXTENT is the set of instances SEQ(i) of #$List such that RELATION holds among the members of SEQ(i) (taken in the same order as they appear in SEQ(i)).  If RELATION is a #$FixedArityRelation with #$relationalArity (q.v.) N then each of the SEQ(i) will have length N.  If RELATION is a #$VariableArityRelation (q.v.) then there will be no particular length shared by each of the SEQ(i).\n<p>\nNote also that since #$Relations are \"intensionally defined\" it follows that (i) a given relation can have different extensions with respect to different contexts (see #$Microtheory) and (ii) two distinct relations can have the same extension with respect to a given context.\n<p>\nSee also #$relationHoldsAmong, #$relationDomain, and #$relationRange.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab5a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab5a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab5a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab5a,24956).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab5a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab5a,[]).
x_comment(x_decreasesCausally,"<code>(#$decreasesCausally EVENT THING SLOT)</code> means that <code>EVENT</code> causes the value of the predicate <code>SLOT</code> applied to <code>THING</code> (in the first argument position) to decrease over some time period after the start of <code>EVENT</code>.  This is defined in terms of #$decreasesCausallyOverPeriod (see also).  Compared with its value immediately before <code>EVENT</code>, the value of <code>SLOT</code> applied to <code>THING</code> is guaranteed to be higher immediately following or some time after <code>EVENT</code>.  This net decrease need not be monotonic.  See #$NoteAboutQuantityChangePredicates for a map of related predicates.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab5b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab5b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab5b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab5b,24961).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab5b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab5b,[]).
x_comment('x_Slide-Photograph',"The collection of all instances of #$Photograph that are designed, not to be viewed directly, but for light to be projected through them onto a surface generating a #$StillImage to be viewed.\n<p>\nNote that a given #$Slide-Photograph might or might not be an instantiation (see #$instantiationOfWork) of some #$Slide-CW (q.v.), depending on whether or not the former is used in the performance of a #$Slideshow-CW (q.v.).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab5c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab5c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab5c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab5c,24962).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab5c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab5c,[]).
x_comment(x_informationDestination,"A specialization of both #$informationTerminal and #$to-Generic that is used to  indicate where information is transferred in a given #$InformationTransferEvent.   <code>(#$informationDestination TRANSFER DESTINATION)</code> means that in <code>TRANSFER</code>, the  information being transferred is sent to, given to, or impressed upon <code>DESTINATION</code>. <code>DESTINATION</code> is typically either an #$InformationBearingThing (at least upon  the completion of <code>TRANSFER</code>, if not before) or an agent; but note that  in the latter case one would normally use the more specific predicate  #$recipientOfInfo.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab5d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab5d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab5d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab5d,24965).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab5d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab5d,[]).
x_comment(x_spatiallyRelated,"A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate (q.v.) and an #$EquivalenceRelation.  This is indeed the most general of all #$SpatialPredicates (q.v.), in that it is by design a generalization (via either #$genlPreds or #$genlPredsWRTArgs) of every spatial predicate.  <code>(#$spatiallyRelated THING1 THING2)</code> means that <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code> are somehow spatially related to each other.  This implies that the two things are embedded within the same surrounding space, but does not specify any more particular spatial relation between them.  Thus, any two spatial objects located in the empirically-observable universe (see #$SpatialThing-Localized) are #$spatiallyRelated to each other.  Conversely, two spatial things that are embedded in different spaces (e.g. an object in the physical universe and a geometric figure in an abstract space defined in the context of a geometry problem) are not spatially related.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab5e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab5e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab5e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab5e,24966).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab5e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab5e,[]).
x_comment(x_ingredients,"The predicate #$ingredients is used to indicate a particular input used to make a particular artifact.  (#$ingredients ART INGR) means that the #$PartiallyTangible thing INGR is one of the #$inputs in the creation of the #$Artifact ART, and INGR (or matter derived from it) is one of ART's #$physicalParts.\n<p>\nThis is a general predicate that usually can be stated in a more specific manner.  For example: #$ingredients-Separable applies only to those inputs which retain their identity in the creation process and which are incorporated into the resulting #$Artifact; #$ingredients-Constituent applies to ingredients that permiate the resulting #$Artifact; and #$ingredients-Destroyed refer to ingredients that have been transformed in the creation process.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab5f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab5f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab5f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab5f,24967).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab5f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab5f,[]).
x_comment(x_thereExistAtLeast,"An #$ExistentialQuantifier (q.v.) and a specialized (albeit higher arity) version of #$thereExists (q.v.).  Compared to the latter, standard existential quantifier, #$thereExistAtLeast has an extra argument-place for specifying how many things (at the least) satisfy a given condition (specified by an #$ELSentence-Assertible, in which the also-specified #$ELVariable will typically occur free).  A closed sentence of the form (#$thereExistAtLeast NUM VAR SENT) means that there are at least NUM distinct things that satisfy SENT, i.e. that render SENT true when taken as the value of VAR.  For example, the sentence\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$thereExistAtLeast 7 ?X (#$isa ?X #$Sea))\n</pre>\n<p>\nmeans that there are at least seven seas.\n<p>\nIn the degenerate case where VAR does not appear free in SENT, then (#$thereExistAtLeast NUM VAR SENT) is equivalent to SENT.\n<p>\nSee also #$thereExistAtMost, #$thereExistExactly.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab60).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab60,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab60,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab60,24970).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab60,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab60,[]).
x_comment(x_EquivalenceRelation,"A #$BinaryPredicateTypeByLogicalFeature that is the \"intersection\"  (see #$collectionIntersection) of #$ReflexiveBinaryPredicate, #$SymmetricBinaryPredicate, and #$TransitiveBinaryPredicate (qq.v.).   A binary predicate is an instance of #$EquivalenceRelation if and only  if it is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n<p>\nAn equivalence relation <code>EQUIV</code> picks out one or more (pairwise disjoint and mutually  exhaustive) \"equivalence classes\" within its domain <code>DOM</code> (i.e. the class of all  things that stand in the <code>EQUIV</code> relation), where each member of a given  equivalence class is related by <code>EQUIV</code> to every other member of that class, and is  related by <code>EQUIV</code> to nothing else.\n<p>\nA simple example of an equivalence relation  is \"the-same-color-as\", applied to the squares on a particular chess board.  This relation partitions (see #$partitionedInto) the squares into two equivalence  classes: the class of white squares and the class of black squares.  Other examples of equivalence relations include #$equals, #$temporalBoundsIdentical and #$levelWith.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab61).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab61,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab61,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab61,24981).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab61,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab61,[]).
x_comment(x_threatConsequent,"The form of a #$Threat is: 'If you (the #$threatenedAgent) do <i>X</i>, I (the #$threateningAgent) will do <i>Y</i>'.  The consequent of this conditional (i.e. 'the threatening agent does <i>Y</i>') is the #$threatConsequent in an instance of #$MakingAThreat.  Thus <code>(#$threatConsequent THREATENING SENT)</code> means that the #$ELSentence-Assertible <code>SENT</code> is the consequent of the #$Threat made in the #$MakingAThreat <code>THREATENING</code>.  See also #$threatAntecedent.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab62).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab62,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab62,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab62,24986).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab62,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab62,[]).
x_comment(x_SubcollectionDenotingFunction,"A specialization of #$CollectionRelatingFunction (q.v.).  This is the type of function that always returns a subcollection of one of its arguments.  A function FUNC is an instance of #$SubcollectionDenotingFunction if and only if it has an argument-place (say the Nth) such that (#$resultGenlArg FUNC N) holds.\n<p>\nFor example, #$PartTypeFn always returns a subcollection of its second argument.  Thus (#$PartTypeFn #$Cat #$Paw), the collection of cat paws, is a subcollection of #$Paw.\n<p>\nAn important specialization of #$SubcollectionDenotingFunction is #$GenlsPreservingSubcollectionDenotingFunction (qq.v.).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab63).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab63,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab63,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab63,24987).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab63,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab63,[]).
x_comment(x_surroundsCompletely,"An instance of #$RelativeLocationalPredicate.  <code>(#$surroundsCompletely OUTSIDE INSIDE)</code> means that <code>OUTSIDE</code> completely surrounds <code>INSIDE</code>.  To be more precise, all rays with origins at <code>INSIDE</code> pass through <code>OUTSIDE</code> by default (with some exceptions; for example, a ray with an origin at <code>INSIDE</code> might pass through a small hole in <code>OUTSIDE)</code>. Note that <code>(#$surroundsCompletely OUTSIDE INSIDE)</code> implies that <code>OUTSIDE</code> and <code>INSIDE</code> are spatially disjoint (see the predicate #$spatiallyDisjoint).  For example, #$surroundsCompletely would hold between a a candy bar wrapper and a candy bar, or between a body of water and a fish in that body of water.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab64).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab64,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab64,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab64,24992).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab64,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab64,[]).
x_comment(x_physicallyContains,"(#$physicallyContains CONT OBJ) means that CONT physically contains (i.e., includes or at least partially surrounds) the object OBJ.  #$physicallyContains is agnostic as to whether or not OBJ is a part of CONT and as to whether OBJ is fully inside of CONT.  (#$physicallyContains CONT OBJ) it is true that whenever CONT moves, OBJ moves. (A container-predicate whose GAFs do not imply co-movement is #$spatiallyContains (q.v.).)  Note that a more specific predicate than #$physicallyContains is almost always applicable -- e.g., #$physicalDecompositions (q.v.) or a more restrictive predicate should be used if OBJ is definitely part of CONT, and #$surrounds-3D (q.v.) or a more restrictive predicate should be used when partonomy is to be ruled out.\n<p>\nFor example, although #$physicallyContains expresses the relationship between an instance of #$Brain and the head that it is in, #$internalParts (q.v.) is more precise.  #$in-ContClosed (q.v.) is more appropriate to specify the relationship of a #$Brain in a #$Skull.  The body of a pregnant woman #$physicallyContains a #$Placenta.  Since a portion of the placenta is a part of the woman's body, while other portions of it are part of the #$Fetus's body, no more restrictive predicate applies.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab65).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab65,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab65,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab65,24993).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab65,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab65,[]).
x_comment(x_transportees,"The predicate #$transportees relates a translational motion event to the object(s) transported in that event. <code>(#$transportees MOVE OBJ)</code> means that some conveyance (see the predicate #$conveyor-Generic) transports <code>OBJ</code> in <code>MOVE</code>.  The conveyance in question can either move along with <code>OBJ</code> in <code>MOVE</code> or remain stationary during <code>MOVE</code>.  The conveyance in question is separate from <code>OBJ</code> (that is, <code>OBJ</code> is not a part of the conveyance).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab66).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab66,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab66,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab66,24996).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab66,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab66,[]).
x_comment(x_requiresOperatingSystem,"(#$requiresOperatingSystem PROGRAM OS) means\n  that copies of the program PROGRAM will only run on the operating system OS.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab67).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab67,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab67,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
