xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab7d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab7d,[]).
x_comment(x_pretendsThat,"A #$BinaryRolePredicate that relates an instance of #$Pretending to an #$ELSentence-Assertible.  <code>(#$pretendsThat PRETENDING SENT)</code> means that the performers of <code>PRETENDING</code> are pretending that <code>SENT</code> is true.  <code>(#$pretendsThat PRETENDING SENT)</code> does not entail that <code>SENT</code> is false; however, it does entail that the performers of <code>PRETENDING</code> <i>believe</i> <code>SENT</code> to be false.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab7e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab7e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab7e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab7e,25052).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab7e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab7e,[]).
x_comment(x_FunctionToArg,"A binary #$FunctionDenotingFunction (q.v.) that takes as argument a #$FunctionalPredicate (and an integer indicating an argument-place in which that predicate is functional) and returns the corresponding function (whose value is always equal to the predicate's corresponding argument in the indicated place).\n<p>\nMore precisely: if <code>PRED</code> is an <code>N</code>-ary functional predicate that is functional in its <code>M</code>th argument (see #$functionalInArgs), then <code>(#$FunctionToArg M PRED)</code> is the (<code>N</code>-1)-ary function <code>FUNC-TO-ARGM</code> such that <code>(PRED ARG(1) ... ARG(N))</code> holds if and only if <code>(FUNC-TO-ARGM ARG(1) ... ARG(M-1) ARG(M+1) ... ARG(N)) = ARG(M)</code>.  If <code>PRED</code> is not functional in its <code>M</code>th argument, then <code>(#$FunctionToArg PRED M)</code> is #$undefined.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$FunctionToArg 2 #$age) is the function that returns the age of a given person.  Thus ((#$FunctionToArg 2 #$age) #$GeorgeWBush) = (#$YearsDuration 50) just in case (#$age #$GeorgeWBush (#$YearsDuration 50)) is true.\n<p>\nSee also #$Lambda and #$Kappa.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab7f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab7f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab7f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab7f,25053).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab7f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab7f,[]).
x_comment(x_BinaryTemporalRelationPredicate,"A specialization of both #$TemporalPredicate and #$BinaryPredicate.  Each instance of #$BinaryTemporalRelationPredicate is a (direct or indirect) specialization of #$temporallyRelated (q.v.) that is used to say something about the relative positions of two #$TemporalThings in time.\n<p>\n#$BinaryTemporalRelationPredicate is partitioned into #$PrimitiveTemporalPredicate and #$ComplexTemporalPredicate.  #$PrimitiveTemporalPredicates (such as #$after) relate #$TimePoints only, and #$ComplexTemporalPredicates (such as  #$postEvents and #$startingDate) also relate more complicated temporal objects such as events, tangible objects, and proper time intervals.\n<p>\nNote that _not_all_ binary #$TemporalPredicates are #$BinaryTemporalRelationPredicates; e.g. those that relate time-quantities (see #$Time-Quantity) are not. Note also that _not_all_ specializations of #$temporallyRelated are #$BinaryTemporalRelationPredicates, or even #$TemporalPredicates; e.g. while (#$relatives ORGANISM1 ORGANISM2) implies that ORGANISM1 and ORGANISM2 are somehow temporally related, it says nothing about their relative positions in time.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab80).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab80,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab80,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab80,25060).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab80,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab80,[]).
x_comment(x_skillCapableOf,"This predicate can be used to assert that a given animal agent has the ability to play a certain role in a certain type of #$SkilledActivityType (q.v.).  <code>(#$skillCapableOf ANIMAL ACTTYPE ROLE)</code> means that <code>ANIMAL</code> has the necessary skills to act in ROLE in normal instances of <code>ACTTYPE</code>.  For example, that #$Gumby can swim is expressed by (#$skillCapableOf #$Gumby #$Swimming-Generic #$performedBy).  #$skillCapableOf entails that <code>ANIMAL</code> satisfies any anatomical or intellectual prerequisites for possessing or exercising the skill; but it is neutral as to whether <code>ANIMAL</code> has the necessary resources of time, money, opportunity, etc., to use the skill in any particular instance of <code>ACTTYPE</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab81).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab81,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab81,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab81,25065).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab81,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab81,[]).
x_comment(x_ExpandSubLFn,"<code>(#$ExpandSubLFn PARAMETERS SUBL-TEMPLATE)</code> denotes the SubL expression resulting from expanding the SubL template <code>SUBL-TEMPLATE</code> once all <code>PARAMETERS</code> are known.  For example, <code>(#$ExpandSubLFn (?X) (+ 1 ?X))</code> will denote the SubL expression <code>(+ 1 2)</code> when the parameter <code>?X</code> is bound to 2.  See also #$EvaluateSubLFn, #$trueSubL and #$performSubL.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab82).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab82,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab82,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab82,25066).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab82,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab82,[]).
x_comment(x_scalarBounds,"A #$NonPhysicalPartPredicate and a specialization of #$quantitySubsumes (q.v.) that relates a given #$ScalarInterval (q.v.) to either of the #$ScalarPointValues that are its boundaries or limits.  <code>(#$scalarBounds SCALAR BOUND)</code> means that <code>BOUND</code> is either the upper bound (see #$maxQuantValue) or the lower bound (see #$minQuantValue) of <code>SCALAR</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, both of the following hold:\n<pre>\n  (#$scalarBounds (#$Mile 5 10) (#$Mile 5))\n\n  (#$scalarBounds (#$Mile 5 10) (#$Mile 10)).\n</pre>") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab83).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab83,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab83,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab83,25067).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab83,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab83,[]).
x_comment(x_RegistryKeyTemplateFn,"A variable-arity, reifiable function which  allows rule-writing using only partially instantiated MS Windows Registry Key (cf. #$RegistryKey) pathnames (cf. #$Pathname), into which variable strings may be substituted (generating full pathnames during inference).\n<p>\nFor instance, the result of using the function as follows: (#$RegistryKeyTemplateFn \"HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Internet Settings~s \"101\") is the pathname: \"HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Internet Settings\\101\".") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab84).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab84,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab84,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab84,25075).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab84,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab84,[]).
x_comment(x_BusinessEvent,"A specialization of #$SocialOccurrence and #$PurposefulAction (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$BusinessEvent is a purposeful activity that is closely associated with, and either helps or hinders, the business purpose of some agent.  Examples include the development of General Motors' business plan for 2003, an individual sales call on a particular customer, printing the 2003 Yellow Pages directory for the Los Angeles area, selling a newspaper, and designing and manufacturing a new memory chip.\n<p>\nThough there is a great deal of overlap between this collection and #$CommercialActivity, they are not the same. Firstly, #$BusinessEvent does not include any purely social or recreational events (whereas a weekend swap-meet, for example, would be an instance of #$CommercialActivity). Secondly, not all activities carried out in pursuit of business interests involve buying or selling (consider head-hunting, for example).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab85).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab85,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab85,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab85,25078).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab85,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab85,[]).
x_comment(x_sellingPerformer,"<code>(#$sellingPerformer COM AGENT)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> actively pursues making a sale in <code>COM</code>.  Note that <code>AGENT</code> may be either the seller of the goods in <code>COM</code> (see the predicate #$seller), or an agent representing the seller of the goods (see the predicate #$sellingAgent).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab86).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab86,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab86,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab86,25081).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab86,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab86,[]).
x_comment(x_PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType,"A collection of collections and a specialization of #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType (q.v.).  Each instance of #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType is both (i) a specialization of #$Quantity whose instances are totally ordered by some relation (see #$TotallyOrderedCollection) and (ii) a type of quantity that is not derived (via e.g. #$ProductFn or #$QuotientFn) from other quantities.  Instances of #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType include #$Time-Quantity, #$Mass, and #$Happiness.\n<p>\nSee also the specialization #$PrimitiveMeasurableQuantityType, whose instances are  specializations of #$OneDimensionalQuantity (q.v.).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab87).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab87,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab87,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab87,25082).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab87,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab87,[]).
x_comment(x_TotallyOrderedCollectionType,"A #$CollectionTypeType and the collection-intersection of #$TotallyOrderedCollection and #$CollectionType (qq.v.).  Thus, something is an instance of #$TotallyOrderedCollectionType if and only if it is a totally-ordered collection of collections.\n<p>\nAn example of a totally-ordered collection-type is #$DegreeOfCloudiness.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab88).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab88,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
