x_comment(x_populationDensity,"A #$MeasurableQuantitySlot and specialization of #$hasNonAspectualQuantity (q.v.) that relates a given #$GeographicalRegion to a #$DensityOfPopulation (q.v.).  <code>(#$populationDensity REGION DENSITY)</code> means that <code>REGION</code> has a population-density <code>DENSITY</code>.\n<p>\nFor example,\n<pre>\n  (#$populationDensity\n    Cucamonga\n    (#$LowAmountFn #$DensityOfPopulation))</code>\n</pre>\n<p>\nmeans that the population density of Cucamonga is low.\n<p>\nSee also #$populationOfRegion, #$populationGrowthRate, and #$populationRange.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab8b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab8b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab8b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab8b,25100).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab8b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab8b,[]).
x_comment(x_UnitOfMeasure,"A specialization of #$ScalarDenotingFunction (q.v.).  Each instance of #$UnitOfMeasure is a function that takes one or two numbers or other #$NumericIntervals as arguments, and returns as value a #$MeasurableQuantity (q.v.), such as a #$Distance or a #$Speed or a #$Volume.  If a unit of measure is applied to one number (see #$Number-General) the result is a precise quantity that is a #$ScalarPointValue; if applied to two (different) numbers -- or to one (or two) #$ProperIntervalOnNumberLine(s) -- the result is a closed-interval quantity that is a #$ScalarProperInterval.  For example, (#$Meter 5) is the distance five meters and (#$Meter 5 10) is the distance \"five to ten meters (inclusive)\".\n<p>\n(A partial exception to the above is the unit-of-measure #$Unity (q.v.), which always returns a #$NumericInterval rather than a #$MeasurableQuantity.)\n<p>\nSpecializations of #$UnitOfMeasure grouped by what they measure include #$UnitOfTime, #$UnitOfSpeed, and #$UnitOfVolume.  Other specializations are #$OneDimensionalUnitOfMeasure, #$MultiDimensionalUnitOfMeasure, #$UnitOfMeasureWithPrefix and #$UnitOfMeasureNoPrefix.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab8c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab8c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab8c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab8c,25112).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab8c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab8c,[]).
x_comment(x_inverseFunctions,"A #$MetaRelation predicate that relates unary one-to-one  functions.  <code>(#$inverseFunctions FUNC1 FUNC2)</code> means that <code>FUNC1</code> and <code>FUNC2</code> are the inverses of each other.  Thus, for  any things <code>X</code> and <code>Y</code>, <code>FUNC1</code> maps <code>X</code> to <code>Y</code> if and only if <code>FUNC2</code> maps <code>Y</code> to <code>X</code>. Note that the foregoing condition is necessary  but <i>not</i> sufficient for one function's being the inverse of  another.  This is because #$Function-Denotationals are  understood as being \"functions-in-intension\", making it  theoretically possible for numerically distinct (but  extensionally equivalent) functions to satisfy the above  condition with respect to a given function.  For example,  in a context restricted to monogamous heterosexual married  people, the inverse of a \"HusbandFn\" would presumably be  the \"WifeFn\" and <i>not</i> the \"BestFriendOfThisManFn\",  even if the latter two functions happened to be coextensional  in that context.  See also the corresponding function  #$InverseFunctionFn.  To relate a mathematical function  (i.e. a #$MathematicalFunctionOnScalars) to its inverse,  see #$inverseQuantFunctions-Nonsymmetric.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab8d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab8d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab8d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab8d,25117).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab8d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab8d,[]).
x_comment(x_SandwichFn,"<code>(#$SandwichFn SET)</code> denotes the collection of #$Sandwiches that have the food-types in <code>SET</code> as their main ingredients.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab8e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab8e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab8e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
