xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab8e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab8e,[]).
x_comment(x_SlightlyFn,"An instance of #$GenericValueFunction (q.v). #$SlightlyFn takes a collection-type COLTYPE that is instance of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder and returns the instance COL of COLTYPE that is the collection of individuals that are regarded as <i>slight</i> in the conventional ordering of COLTYPE.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$SlightlyFn #$RegionTypeByHilliness) is the collection of all geographical regions that are slightly hilly: those that are slight in the conventional ordering of regions by hilliness.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab8f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab8f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab8f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab8f,25119).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab8f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab8f,[]).
x_comment(x_HighAmountFn,"A #$GenericValueFunction (q.v.) that takes a given #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType to a #$GenericQuantity of that type.   In a given context, <code>(#$HighAmountFn QUANTTYPE)</code> is the properly interval-like quantity (and instance) of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> that is considered a <i>high amount</i> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> with respect to that context.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$HighAmountFn #$Happiness) is a high amount of happiness.  It is more (see #$followingValue) than (#$MediumAmountFn #$Happiness) and less than (#$VeryHighAmountFn #$Happiness).\n<p>\nSee also the corresponding predicate #$highAmountOf.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab90).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab90,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab90,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab90,25122).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab90,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab90,[]).
x_comment(x_MacroRelation,"A specialization of #$Relation whose instances are used to form shorthand expressions for more complex formulas.  Each instance of #$MacroRelation is correlated -- via an #$expansion assertion -- with a certain template for a class of (possibly quantified) CycL formulas.  A closed formula (i.e. a closed \"GAF\" or \"NAT\"; see #$CycLClosedFormula) built from a given macro-relation is treated by the Cyc system as semantically equivalent to the (usually more complex) corresponding formula fitting the template.  Thus suppose the macro-relation MACROREL had an expansion assertion of the form:\n<p>\n(#$expansion MACROREL\nCOMPLEX-RELATIONAL-STRUCTURE :ARG1 ... :ARGN) .\n<p>\nThen a closed formula of the form\n<p>\n(MACROREL TERM(1) ... TERM(N))\n<p>\nwould be treated by the system as shorthand for the more complex formula\n<p>\n(COMPLEX-RELATIONAL-STRUCTURE TERM(1) ... TERM(N)) .\n<p>\nSpecializations of #$MacroRelation include #$RuleMacroPredicate, #$ELRelation, and #$TermMacroFunction (qq.v.).  Instances of #$MacroRelation that are not in any of the latter three collections include #$implies, #$argFormat, and #$quotedIsa.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab91).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab91,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab91,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab91,25127).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab91,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab91,[]).
x_comment(x_simultaneousWith,"A #$PrimitiveTemporalPredicate (q.v.) that is a specialization of both #$cotemporal (q.v.) and #$equals. The predicate #$simultaneousWith is just the cotemporal relation restricted to #$TimePoints (q.v.), with respect to which (as with #$TimeIntervals generally) cotemporality is both a necessary and a sufficient condition for identity.  (#$simultaneousWith T1 T2) means that T1 and T2 both extend over (and thus _are_) the same single point in time.\n<p>\nNote that individual #$TimePoints are seldom mentioned in axioms in the Cyc Knowledge Base.  Most time-related axioms use #$ComplexTemporalPredicates (cf.), such as #$cotemporal, which can hold between #$TemporalThings of any sort.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab92).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab92,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab92,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab92,25141).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab92,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab92,[]).
x_comment(x_BiologicalProductionEvent,"A specialization of both #$PhysicalCreationEvent and #$BiologicalEvent.  In each instance of #$BiologicalProductionEvent, raw materials are combined to produce a product, and at least one instance of #$BiologicalLivingObject (q.v.) is the producer.  Specializations of #$BiologicalProductionEvent include #$SecretionEvent and #$BiologicalReproductionEvent.\n<p>\nNote that the distinction between this collection and #$BiologicalDevelopmentEvent is not always sharp, but instances of #$BiologicalProductionEvent are distinguished insofar as the event in question produces a reasonably distinct entity from start to finish. Thus, #$FlowerDevelopmentEvent is an instance of #$BiologicalDevelopmentEvent although some case could be made for it being an instance of #$BiologicalProductionEvent.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab93).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab93,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab93,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab93,25144).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab93,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab93,[]).
x_comment(x_interArgResultIsa,"A quaternary #$MetaRelation predicate for stating that a certain kind of complex, partly #$isa -based relationship holds between a given argument to a given function and the corresponding value that is returned.\n<p>\n<code>(#$interArgResultIsa FUNC N INDEP-ARG-COL DEP-ARG-COL)</code> means that, when an instance of <code>INDEP-ARG-COL</code> appears as the <code>N</code>th argument to <code>FUNC</code>, then (regardless of what the other arguments to <code>FUNC</code> might be) the value (if any) of that function for those arguments is an instance of <code>DEP-ARG-COL</code>.  That is: if <code>INST</code> is an instance of <code>INDEP-ARG-COL</code> and <code>(FUNC ARG1 ... ARGN-1 INST ...) = VALUE</code>, then <code>VALUE</code> is an instance of <code>DEP-ARG-COL</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, <code>(#$interArgResultIsa #$RoundUpFn 1 #$RealNumber #$Integer)</code> means that the rounding-up function #$RoundUpFn returns an integer whenever it is given a real number as its first (and only) argument.\n<p>\nNote that, unlike (e.g.) the #$InterArgTypePredicates, #$interArgResultIsa <i>cannot</i> be used to put semantic well-formedness constraints on functions.  See also the similar but less flexible ternary #$interArg1ResultIsa et al.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab94).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab94,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab94,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab94,25147).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab94,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab94,[]).
x_comment(x_subsetOf,"This predicate relates a set or collection <code>SUB</code> to a set or collection <code>SUPER</code> whenever the extent (see #$extent) of <code>SUB</code> is a  subset of the extent of <code>SUPER</code>.  That is, <code>(#$subsetOf SUB SUPER)</code> means that  every element of (see #$elementOf) <code>SUB</code> is an element of <code>SUPER</code>.  #$subsetOf is thus a generalization both of the subset relation in set theory and of #$genls (q.v.); and (unlike either of those other relations) #$subsetOf can hold between a set and a collection, or between a collection and a set.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab95).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab95,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab95,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab95,25154).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab95,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab95,[]).
x_comment(x_BiologicalSubspecies,"A specialization of #$BiologicalTaxon whose instances are more restrictive\nthan those of #$BiologicalSpecies.  Each instance of #$BiologicalSubspecies\nis a specialization of some instance of #$BiologicalSpecies.  Members of\ndifferent subspecies of the same species can produce fertile offspring by\ninterbreeding; but such offspring are not members of either of the parental\nsubspecies, although they are members of the common species.  All instances\nof a given biological subspecies have significant traits or collections of\ntraits in common that are not shared by all other members of the subsuming\nspecies.\n<p>\nA #$BiologicalSubspecies is formed by inbreeding of a restricted group of\nmembers of the same species.  This can happen naturally through geographic\nisolation or intentionally through controlled reproduction to create, for\nexample, dog breeds or crop strains.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab96).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab96,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab96,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab96,25155).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab96,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab96,[]).
x_comment(x_GenericValueFunction,"A specialization of #$ScalarDenotingFunction whose instances map #$TotallyOrderedQuantityTypes to certain #$GenericQuantity[ies] (qq.v.) of those types.  Each #$GenericValueFunction is a unary function which, when applied to a totally-ordered quantity-type <code>QUANTTYPE</code>, returns some \"generic amount\" or roughly-specified range (e.g. <i>high, medium, low</i>) of <code>QUANTTYPE</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, #$HighAmountFn is a generic-value function, and (#$HighAmountFn #$Elasticity) is a the generic-quantity \"high amount of elasticity\" (with respect to some contextually-determined, conventional elasticity scale).\n<p>\nOther instances of #$GenericValueFunction are #$LowAmountFn, #$MediumAmountFn, and #$MediumToVeryHighAmountFn.\n<p>\nSee also #$RelativeGenericValueFn and #$NoteAboutGivingGenericValueFunctionsNumericValues.  And cf. #$GenericValueTypeFunction.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab97).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab97,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab97,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab97,25169).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab97,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab97,[]).
x_comment(x_endingPoint,"A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate (q.v.) and specialization of both #$temporallyCoterminal and #$temporallySubsumes that relates a #$TemporalThing to the #$TimePoint at which it ceases to exist (if it is a #$SomethingExisting), happen (if an #$Event), or obtain (if a #$StaticSituation). <code>(#$endingPoint TEMP T)</code> means that <code>THING</code> ends at <code>T</code>, the latest moment of its #$temporalExtent (q.v.).\n<p>\nSee also the corresponding function #$EndFn; and see #$startingPoint.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab98).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab98,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab98,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab98,25176).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab98,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab98,[]).
x_comment('x_conveyor-Generic',"<code>(#$conveyor-Generic MOVE OBJ)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> enables or facilitates the conveyance of what is transported (the animals, goods, people, etc. that are the #$transportees) in the #$Conveying-Generic <code>MOVE</code>.  In the case where <code>OBJ</code> itself is an #$objectMoving in <code>MOVE</code>, use the more specific predicate #$transporter.  If <code>OBJ</code> is known not to be an #$objectMoving, use the more specific predicate #$conveyor-Stationary.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab99).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab99,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab99,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab99,25179).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab99,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab99,[]).
x_comment(x_TupleOfIntervals,"A specialization of #$Tuple (q.v.).  Each instance of #$TupleOfIntervals is a tuple whose members (see #$memberOfTuple) are all #$ScalarIntervals (q.v.).\n<p>\nNotable specializations of #$TupleOfIntervals include #$ScalarInterval itself (whose instances are one-tuples), #$VectorInterval (whose instances are two-tuples of scalars), and #$BloodPressureReading.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab9a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab9a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab9a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab9a,25180).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab9a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab9a,[]).
x_comment(x_rewriteOf,"A specialization of #$equals and a #$CycLReformulatorConstant that relates pairs of #$CycLClosedDenotationalTerms.   #$rewriteOf is, in a sense, a \"directional\" version of #$equals.  <code>(#$rewriteOf FAVORED-TERM DISFAVORED-TERM)</code> means that (1) <code>FAVORED-TERM</code> and <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code> denote the same thing, and (2) <code>FAVORED-TERM</code> is deemed worthy of being privileged over <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code> in the following senses: assertions made on <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code> will be propagated to <code>FAVORED-TERM</code>, and whenever possible attempts to reason about  <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code> will be transformed into attempts to reason about <code>FAVORED-TERM</code>.  The propagated assertions will differ from the original assertions only in that the propagated assertions will be the result of substituting <code>FAVORED-TERM</code> for <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code> wherever <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code> occurs in the original assertions.  Note that substitution does not happen in the opposite direction.  That is, assertions containing <code>FAVORED-TERM</code> will not propagate to <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code>.\n<p>\nOne common use of #$rewriteOf is that in which <code>FAVORED-TERM</code> is a syntactically simpler term than <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code>.  For example, <code>FAVORED-TERM</code> may contain fewer nested terms that denote #$Function-Denotationals than does <code>DISFAVORED-TERM</code>.  (#$rewriteOf #$UnitedStatesNavy (#$NavyFn #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica)) is a good example of this common sort of use.\n<p>\nNote that #$rewriteOf expresses a strictly conventional notion of \"directional\" equality. Pragmatic considerations, not ontology, will dictate which term is chosen to occur as the privileged (left-hand side) term in a #$rewriteOf assertion.  Thus, in the example the fact that simpler terms are generally easier to use (remember, manipulate, etc.) dictates that #$UnitedStatesNavy should be a rewrite of (#$NavyFn #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica), and not vice versa.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab9b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab9b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab9b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab9b,25183).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab9b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab9b,[]).
x_comment(x_MediumAmountFn,"A #$GenericValueFunction (q.v.) that takes a given #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType to a #$GenericQuantity of that type.   In a given context, <code>(#$MediumAmountFn QUANTTYPE)</code> is the properly interval-like quantity (and instance) of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> that is considered a <i>medium amount</i> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> with respect to that context.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$MediumAmountFn #$Happiness) is a medium amount of happiness.  It is more (see #$followingValue) than (#$LowAmountFn #$Happiness) and less than (#$HighAmountFn #$Happiness).\n<p>\nSee also the corresponding predicate #$mediumAmountOf.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab9c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab9c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab9c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab9c,25188).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab9c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab9c,[]).
x_comment(x_cultureBeliefs,"This predicate relates cultures to the beliefs   that are characteristic of and important to their participants.  <code>(#$cultureBeliefs CULTURE PROP)</code> means that participants in    <code>CULTURE</code> (but not participants in all other cultures) tend    to believe that <code>PROP</code> is true and would (at least upon   reflection) consider a belief in <code>PROP</code> to be important   to or distinctive of <code>CULTURE</code>.  Arguably, every human culture   has an associated set of beliefs that--taken as a whole--   serves to distinguish it, more or less, from all other    cultures.  This set might include religious beliefs,   socio-political beliefs, beliefs about the causes of    certain things, views on morality, views on how best to   accomplish certain tasks, etc.  (In using this predicate    it is important to avoid unjustified stereotypes of the    culture in question and to refrain from attributing to the    culture beliefs that do not accurately characterize it.)") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab9d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab9d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab9d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab9d,25193).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab9d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab9d,[]).
x_comment(x_EquipmentTypeForEventTypeFn,"A #$UnaryFunction that takes a #$FirstOrderCollection and returns a #$SecondOrderCollection.  More specifically, #$EquipmentTypeForEventTypeFn takes a specialization of #$Event and returns a collection of specializations of #$PhysicalDevice.  <code>(#$EquipmentTypeForEventTypeFn EVENT-TYPE)</code> denotes the collection of all device types <code>DEVICE-TYPE</code> such that in each instance of <code>EVENT-TYPE</code> some instance of <code>DEVICE-TYPE</code> is used.  For example, #$Microphone is an instance of (#$EquipmentTypeForEventTypeFn #$KaraokeEvent), but #$Bug-InformationGatheringDevice is not.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab9e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab9e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab9e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aab9e,25194).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab9e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab9e,[]).
x_comment(x_DerivedMeasurableQuantityType,"A collection of collections and a specialization of both #$MeasurableScalarIntervalType and #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$DerivedMeasurableQuantityType is a totally-ordered collection of instances of #$MultiDimensionalQuantity: measurable quantities that are derived from some more basic quantities (via e.g. #$ProductFn or #$QuotientFn).   Examples include #$Volume, #$Area, and #$Acceleration.\n<p>\nCf. #$PrimitiveMeasurableQuantityType.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aab9f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aab9f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aab9f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
