xc_kb_names(a65b16526aab9f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aab9f,[]).
x_comment(x_SingleDoerAction,"A specialization of #$Action.  Each instance of #$SingleDoerAction is an action which can have only one \"doer\" (see the predicate #$doneBy).  Such actions may be intentional, but they need not be.  Most bodily functions (e.g., the instances of #$Heartbeating or #$Bleeding-TheAction) belong to specializations of #$SingleDoerAction, because the only doer of a normal bodily function is the body of a single agent.  In order for a type of action (i.e., a specialization of #$Action) to be a specialization of #$SingleDoerAction, it must be inconsistent to assert, for any instance <code>ACT</code> of that type of action, both <code>(#$doneBy ACT X)</code> and <code>(#$doneBy ACT Y)</code>, where <code>X</code> and <code>Y</code> are different.  Notable specializations of #$SingleDoerAction include #$Sleeping, #$Dying, #$AnimalWalkingProcess, and #$WritingByHand.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaba0).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaba0,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaba0,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aaba0,25198).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aaba0,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aaba0,[]).
x_comment(x_pointQuantValue,"A #$NumericComparisonPredicate and a specialization of #$numericallyEquals, #$minQuantValue, and #$maxQuantValue (qq.v.).  #$pointQuantValue is essentially the relation #$numericallyEquals restricted to scalar point-values, with the additional proviso that its first argument must be given using a reifiable CycL term.  Thus a sentence of the form\n<p>\n    <code>(#$pointQuantValue REIFIABLE-TERM OTHER-TERM)</code>\n<p>\nis true if and only if <code>REIFIABLE-TERM</code> is a #$CycLReifiableDenotationalTerm (q.v.), and <code>REIFIABLE-TERM</code> and <code>OTHER-TERM</code> denote the same #$ScalarPointValue (q.v.).\n<p>\nThis predicate is primarily used to state the equivalence of a reified scalar-denoting term (e.g. '#$Zero', '#$AvogadrosNumber', '#$SpeedOfLight') with an appropriate unreifiable term.  For example, both of the following sentences hold:\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$pointQuantValue #$Zero 0)\n</pre>\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$pointQuantValue\n    #$SpeedOfLight\n    (#$PerFn (#$Mile 186000) (#$HoursDuration 1))'  .\n</pre>") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaba1).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaba1,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaba1,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aaba1,25199).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aaba1,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aaba1,[]).
x_comment(x_IncarnationPhysicalFn,"A function which returns the set of individuals that collectively comprise the physical incarnations of the stated divine being. Thus, in the #$ChristianityMt, #$JesusOfNazareth is the physical incarnation of both #$JesusChrist (the divine being representing the son of #$GodOfAbrahamIsaacAndJacob) and the physical incarnation of #$GodOfAbrahamIsaacAndJacob itself. Likewise, the resurrected Christ who appeared to the apostles is also a physical incarnation of #$JesusChrist. This introduces issues of the holy Trinity that should be investigated further in Cyc.\n<p>\nReligions which believe in reincarnation may also be axiomatized with this function, if one considers sentient animals as physical incarnations of a non-physical 'soul', where soul denotes a #$DivineBeing. Since some religions believe that people can be reincarnated as \"lower\" animals, the result of #$IncarnationPhysicalFn must be specified (hence it is a binary function).  Thus, we can tell Cyc that B is a reincarnation of A if there exists some divine-being (call it a soul) such that A and B are both physical incarnations of this being, and B is born after A dies (see #$Reincarnation)") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaba2).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaba2,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaba2,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aaba2,25216).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aaba2,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aaba2,[]).
x_comment(x_startingPoint,"A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate (q.v.) and specialization of both #$temporallyCooriginating and #$temporallySubsumes that relates a #$TemporalThing to the #$TimePoint at which it starts to exist (if it is a #$SomethingExisting), happen (if an #$Event), or obtain (if a #$StaticSituation). <code>(#$startingPoint TEMP T)</code> means that <code>THING</code> begins at <code>T</code>, the earliest moment of its #$temporalExtent (q.v.).\n<p>\nSee also the corresponding function #$StartFn; and see #$endingPoint.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaba3).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaba3,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaba3,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aaba3,25219).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aaba3,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aaba3,[]).
x_comment(x_spatiallySubsumes,"A #$SpatialPredicate that is a specialization of both #$spatiallyIncludes and #$spatiallyIntersects (qq.v.).  <code>(#$spatiallySubsumes SUPER SUB)</code> means that the spatial extent of <code>SUB</code> lies within the spatial extent of <code>SUPER</code>.  For example, a model ship in a bottle is <i>not</i> spatially subsumed by the bottle, but the lip of the bottle <i>is</i> spatially subsumed by the bottle.  (The bottle #$spatiallyContains the ship.)  Note that <code>SUB</code> might or might not be a part of <code>SUPER</code>.  For example, a shadow cast on a wall is spatially subsumed by the surface of the wall, but is not a part of it.  But note also that <code>SUPER</code> does have a part that is at least spatially coextensive (see #$cospatial) with <code>SUB</code>.  Specializations of #$spatiallySubsumes include #$physicalDecompositions and #$geographicalSubRegions.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaba4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaba4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaba4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aaba4,25222).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aaba4,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aaba4,[]).
x_comment(x_granuleOfSpatialStuff,"(#$granuleOfSpatialStuff STUFFTYPE OBJTYPE) means  that the stuff-like collection STUFFTYPE has as its spatial granules (or granularity level) instances of the object-like collection OBJTYPE. If some collection is spatially stuff-like, that means that the instances of that collection can be divided spatially, and the resulting physical portions will themselves be instances of that collection; e.g., a physical portion of some instance of #$SandMob is still sand (cf. #$ExistingStuffType). Such divisibility cannot go on indefinitely, however: eventually, division of something spatially stuff-like will result in the object-like 'granules' out of which the stuff-like thing is composed. For instances, division of sand would eventually result in individual grains of sand, division of water would eventually get down to individual molecules of water, etc.\n<p>\n_Below_ this level of division, the remaining physical portions do _not_ count as instances of the stuff-type from which they were divided. _At_ this level of division, things may go one way or another: a single molecule of water is #$Water (at least if we stick by the necessary identity of water and H2O), whereas a single grain of sand is not plausibly sand.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaba5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaba5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaba5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aaba5,25223).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aaba5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aaba5,[]).
x_comment(x_supervisedAction,"An instance of #$BinaryPredicate and a specialization (via #$genlInverse) of #$temporallySubsumes.  <code>(#$supervisedAction SUPERVISING ACTION)</code> means that <code>ACTION</code> is the #$PurposefulAction being supervised in the #$Supervising <code>SUPERVISING</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaba6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaba6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaba6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aaba6,25226).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aaba6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aaba6,[]).
x_comment(x_commonGenlPreds,"A #$RuleMacroPredicate that relates a given #$PredicateType to any #$Predicate that is a generalization (see #$genlPreds) of all instances of that type.  <code>(#$commonGenlPreds PREDTYPE PRED)</code> means that, for any instance <code>PREDINST</code> of <code>PREDTYPE</code>, <code>(#$genlPreds PREDINST PRED)</code> holds.  Thus the #$commonGenlPreds sentence is equivalent to <code>(#$relationAllInstance #$genlPreds PREDTYPE PRED)</code>.\n<p>\nNote that #$commonGenlPreds is not to be confused with #$typedGenlPreds (q.v.).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaba7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaba7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaba7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aaba7,25227).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aaba7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aaba7,[]).
x_comment(x_SexualReproductionEvent,"A specialization of #$BiologicalReproductionEvent.  In each instance of #$SexualReproductionEvent, two #$parentActors produce one or more offspring.  Sexual activity which does not result in offspring is not included in this concept.  On the other hand, if offspring begin to develop, but never finish (e.g., it just hasn't finished yet, or the mother dies, or the offspring is aborted, or the offspring doesn't survive to independence, etc.), then that is still considered an instance of #$SexualReproductionEvent but an incomplete one.\n<p>\nThe example of #$SexualReproductionEvent most familiar to most people is when two mammals, one male and one female, mate; sperm from the male combines with one or more eggs inside the female and the resulting offspring develop(s), inside the female parent and dependent upon her for nutrition, until it/they is/are born.  However, #$SexualReproductionEvent is more general than that, and would include these examples: Two birds mate; the female lays one or more eggs; both parents incubate the eggs until they hatch.  Two fish engage in courtship; the female expells her eggs; the male expells his sperm on top of them, and they swim away leaving many fertilized eggs to develop into juvenile fish.  One oak tree's flowers give off airborne pollen; some grains land on the stigma of another tree's flower and fertilize the ovary inside, which then develops into an acorn which is shed by the female parent at the end of summer.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaba8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaba8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaba8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aaba8,25230).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aaba8,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aaba8,[]).
x_comment(x_genls,"A instance of #$TaxonomicSlotForCollections (q.v.) and  a specialization of #$generalizations.  #$genls relates a given collection  (see #$Collection) to those collections that subsume it.  More precisely, <code>(#$genls SUBCOL SUPERCOL)</code> means that <code>SUPERCOL</code> is a supercollection of <code>SUBCOL</code>:  anything that is an instance of (see #$isa) <code>SUBCOL</code> is also an instance of <code>SUPERCOL</code>.  For example, <code>(#$genls #$Dog #$Mammal)</code> holds.  #$genls is one of the most commonly-occurring predicates in the Cyc Knowledge Base, and one of  the relations most fundamental to the Cyc ontology.  See also #$subsetOf.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaba9).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaba9,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaba9,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aaba9,25233).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aaba9,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aaba9,[]).
x_comment(x_exceedsCriterion,"<code>(#$exceedsCriterion PROP DESCRIP CRIT)</code> means that <code>PROP</code> is provable in the 'real world' description microtheory <code>DESCRIP</code>, but not in the #$Microtheory instance <code>CRIT</code>.  'Not provable' of course means unprovable to some set limit on the inference engine's checking.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabaa).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabaa,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabaa,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabaa,25234).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabaa,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabaa,[]).
x_comment(x_greaterThanOrEqualTo,"A #$NumericComparisonPredicate (q.v.) that is a generalization of the mathematical greater-than-or equal-to <code>(>=)</code> relation to #$ScalarIntervals (q.v.) of all sorts, including quantitative intervals (see #$NumericInterval and #$Quantity) as well as point values (see #$ScalarPointValue).  <code>(#$greaterThanOrEqualTo VALUE1 VALUE2)</code> means that <code>VALUE1</code> is greater than or equal to <code>VALUE2</code> with respect to some scale that they are both on.  More precisely, there is some #$TotallyOrderedScalarIntervalType <code>SCALE</code> that both <code>VALUE1</code> and <code>VALUE2</code> are instances of and either (i) <code>SCALE</code> is a specialization of #$NumericInterval (e.g. #$RealNumber) and the minimum (see #$minQuantValue) of <code>VALUE1</code> is greater than or equal to the maximum (see #$maxQuantValue) of <code>VALUE2</code>, (ii) <code>(#$followingValueOnScale VALUE2 VALUE1 SCALE)</code> holds, or (iii) <code>(#$equals VALUE1 VALUE2)</code> holds.\n<p>\nSee also #$greaterThan and #$lessThanOrEqualTo.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabab).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabab,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabab,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabab,25235).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabab,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabab,[]).
x_comment(x_PlusFn,"A variable-arity #$MathematicalFunctionOnScalars that is an extension of the arithmetical operation of addition to measurable #$ScalarIntervals (q.v.) generally.  Where  <code>ADDEND1</code>, ..., and <code>ADDENDn</code> are pairwise #$numericallyComparable (q.v.), <code>(#$PlusFn ADDEND1 ... ADDENDn)</code> is the arithmetic sum of <code>ADDEND1</code>, ..., and <code>ADDENDn</code>.  For example, (#$PlusFn 2 3 4) is 9, and (#$PlusFn (#$Meter 1.5) (#$Meter 0.7)) is (#$Meter 2.2).\n<p>\nNote that the term <code>(#$PlusFn ADDEND1 ... ADDENDn)</code> is #$undefined if there are <code>ADDENDi</code> and <code>ADDENDj</code> (1 <code><= i,j <= n)</code> that are not comparable.  For example, <code>(#$PlusFn (#$MinutesDuration 1) (#$Meter 3))</code> is undefined, since (#$MinutesDuration 1) is a #$Time-Quantity and (#$Meter 3) is a #$Distance, and time-quantities are not comparable with distances.\n<p>\nFor subtraction, see #$DifferenceFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabac).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabac,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabac,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabac,25238).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabac,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabac,[]).
x_comment(x_startingDate,"A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate that is used to  specify the date (see #$Date) on which a given temporal thing starts to  happen or begins to exist.  <code>(#$startingDate TEMP-THING DATE)</code> means that <code>TEMP-THING</code> starts (if <code>TEMP-THING</code> is an event or other  #$Situation) or begins to exist (if <code>TEMP-THING</code> is a physical object or other #$SomethingExisting) sometime during <code>DATE</code>.  That is, <code>(#$temporallySubsumes DATE (#$StartFn TEMP-THING))</code> holds.  Note that <code>DATE</code>, though defined in terms of a calendar, need not be a particular day or year (e.g. it might be a particular minute or month or decade).  See also #$startingPoint and  #$endingDate.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabad).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabad,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabad,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabad,25243).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabad,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabad,[]).
x_comment('x_Determiner-Indefinite',"The class of indefinite determiners, such as <i>a</i>, <i>an</i>.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabae).
