x_comment(x_likesAsFriend,"An instance of both #$PersonalAssociationPredicate and #$LikesPredicate that relates a given agent to another agent s/he feels friendliness toward.  <code>(#$likesAsFriend AGENT1 AGENT2)</code> means that <code>AGENT1</code> enjoys interacting socially with <code>AGENT2</code>.  See also #$Friendliness.  Note that this  does not preclude <code>AGENT1</code>'s \"liking\", or being fond of, <code>AGENT2</code> in other senses (e.g. a romantic sense) as well.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabaf).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabaf,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabaf,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabaf,25245).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabaf,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabaf,[]).
x_comment(x_compositionOfFunctions,"A #$MetaRelation predicate that relates two functions to the function that is their composition.  (#$compositionOfFunctions COMP FUNC1 FUNC2) means that COMP is the composition of FUNC1 and FUNC2.  That is, for any element DOM-EL #$inDomainOf FUNC1, the value of COMP applied to DOM-EL is the same as the result obtained by applying FUNC1 to DOM-EL, and then applying FUNC2 to that result.  Symbolically, (COMP DOM-EL) = (FUNC2 (FUNC1 DOM-EL)).\n<p>\nFor example, a function called \"MaternalGrandfatherFn\" could be construed as the composition of #$MotherFn with #$FatherFn: A is the maternal grandfather of B if and only if A is the father of the mother of B.\n<p>\nNote that in the general description above, the domain of COMP is identical to the domain of FUNC1, and the range (see #$RangeOfFunctionFn) of COMP is a (proper or otherwise) subset of the range of FUNC2.  (Strictly speaking, the range of COMP is the set of elements of the range of FUNC2 that are the values for some element or other of the range of FUNC1 taken as arguments for FUNC2.)  This means of course that COMP and FUNC1 must have the same #$arity (or both must be #$VariableArityFunctions); also the #$arity of FUNC2 must be 1.\n<p>\nSee also the corresponding #$FunctionCompositionFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabb0).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabb0,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabb0,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabb0,25246).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabb0,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabb0,[]).
x_comment(x_endTogetherInSituationType,"(#$endTogetherInSituationType <code>SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> end at the same time.\n<p>\nNote that <code>(#$endTogetherInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabb1).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabb1,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabb1,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabb1,25253).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabb1,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabb1,[]).
x_comment(x_thereExistRange,"An instance of #$ExistentialQuantifier-Bounded (q.v.) that can be used to make rough or inexact statements about the number of things that satisfy a given criterion.  <code>(#$thereExistRange RANGE VAR SENT)</code> is true if and only if the number of things in the universe of discourse that satisfy <code>SENT</code> with respect to <code>VAR</code> falls within <code>RANGE</code> (see #$NonNegativeIntegerRange).  (A given <code>THING</code> \"satisfies <code>SENT</code> with respect to <code>VAR</code>\" just in case any sentence obtained by substituting a CycL name of <code>THING</code> for each free occurrence of <code>VAR</code> within <code>SENT</code> is true.)  For example, the sentence '(#$thereExistRange #$Billions-Quant ?X (#$isa ?X #$Star))' means that there are billions of stars.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabb2).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabb2,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabb2,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabb2,25256).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabb2,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabb2,[]).
x_comment(x_MeaningInSystemFn,"A reifiable #$BinaryFunction that returns the meaningful entities indexed by a particular character string in a Cyc-external information source. More precisely, (#$MeaningInSystemFn INFOSOURCE STRING), applied to the #$CharacterString STRING and the #$IndexedInformationSource INFOSOURCE, returns whatever is indexed by STRING in that system.  For example, (#$MeaningInSystemFn #$WordNet-1997Version \"N03585958\") returns the WordNet `synset' (`synonym set') represented by (rampart|bulwark|wall), meaning \"an embankment built around a space for defensive purposes\".\n<p>\nSTRING can have any format chosen by the person who builds the representation of the external system.  In addition, STRING may contain arbitrary further characters or information, depending on how the external information is selected and processed.  This function makes it possible to relate a concept in an external system to an arbitrarily complicated expression composed of Cyc concepts.  If the external concept has a direct, exact mapping to a single Cyc constant, then use the predicate #$synonymousExternalConcept rather than this function.  If there is correspondence only to one Cyc constant, but it is only approximate, use #$overlappingExternalConcept.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabb3).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabb3,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabb3,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabb3,25257).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabb3,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabb3,[]).
x_comment(x_arg5Genl,"A binary #$ArgGenlPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$arg5Genl RELN COL)</code>  means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the fifth argument to <code>RELN</code> must be a subcollection of <code>COL</code>.  That is,  <code>(RELN ARG1 ARG2 ARG3 ARG4 ARG5 ...)</code> is semantically well-formed only if <code>(#$genls ARG5 COL)</code> holds.  For an explanation of semantic well-formedness, see  #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  See #$argGenl for a  similar but more flexible ternary predicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabb4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabb4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabb4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabb4,25260).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabb4,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabb4,[]).
x_comment(x_maleficiary,"An #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$objectHarmed.  <code>(#$maleficiary EVENT AGENT)</code> means that the #$Agent-Generic <code>AGENT</code> is harmed (in some sense) by the occurrence of the #$Event <code>EVENT</code>.  In some cases, this is consistent with <code>EVENT</code> being (in some sense) <i>beneficial</i> to <code>AGENT</code>.  For example, being punished might harm one in the short run, but benefit one in the long run.  Thus the fact that #$maleficiary is a required actor slot for #$Punishing does not entail that #$Punishing is always (completely) detrimental to the person punished.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabb5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabb5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabb5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabb5,25261).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabb5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabb5,[]).
x_comment(x_Anime,"The collection for anime, a style of animation. The word `anime' is a shortened form of the Japanese word `animeishon', derived from the English word `animation'.  #$Anime is also referred to as 'Japanimation'.  Anime often contains references to Japanese culture.\n<p>\nSome common characteristics of characters in anime (but are not limited to or restricted by) abnormally large eyes, heavily stylized, unique, and impossible hair styles, and/or an extraordinary amount supernatural powers.\n<p>\nThis collection is agnostic about medium; an instance of this collection could be an instance of #$MediaSeriesProduct, #$TVSeriesProduct, #$Movie-CW, or another relevant medium for animation.\n<p>\nSome examples of anime well known in the US are: #$Robotech-TheTVShow, #$Pokemon-TVShow, #$Akira-TheMovie, #$SailorMoon-TVShow, and #$DragonBallZ.\n<p>\nA borderline case is #$PowerpuffGirls-TheTVShow, which has anime-ish animation, but no references to Japanese culture.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabb6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabb6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabb6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabb6,25262).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabb6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabb6,[]).
x_comment(x_distributesOutOfArg,"(#$distributesOutOfArg <reln> <pred> <n>) is a directive to the canonicalizer that relation <reln> distrbutes out of a predicate <pred> when it appears as the top-most relation in the predicate's arg <n>.  Specifically, it denotes (#$distributesOutOfArg <reln> <pred> <n>) denotes that the canonicalizer will translate\n<p>\n(<pred> <arg-1> ... <arg-n-1> (<reln> <arg-n1> <arg-n2> ... <arg-nm>) <arg-n+1> ..)\n<p>\ninto\n<p>\n(<reln> (<pred> <arg-1> ... <arg-n-1> <arg-n1> <arg-n+1> ...)\n(<pred> <arg-1> ... <arg-n-1> <arg-n2> <arg-n+1> ...)\n...\n(<pred> <arg-1> ... <arg-n-1> <arg-nm> <arg-n+1> ...))") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabb7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabb7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabb7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
