x_comment('x_typePrimaryFunction-AgnosticIntentional',"A type-level version of #$primaryFunction. <code>(#$typePrimaryFunction-AgnosticIntentional AGTTYPE SITTYPE ROLE)</code> means that in ordinary or normal circumstances, instances of <code>AGTTYPE</code> have playing <code>ROLE</code> in situations of type <code>SITTYPE</code> as their most important function in some immediate functional context. Like #$primaryFunction, the relation is agnostic as to whether the functionality derives from the intent of some intentional designer: i.e., the relation is not a spec-pred of #$typeIntendedBehaviorCapable.  This is not to say that usage of #$typePrimaryFunction-AgnosticIntentional implies that no design exists: the relation is simply agnostic as to whether a design exists or not.  For this reason #$typePrimaryFunction is deemed to be a specialization.  In cases where <code>SITTYPE</code> is itself a #$FunctionalSystem (say, a kind of #$Cell or a kind of #$Organization in the #$FunctionalRoleAnalysisMt), <code>SITTYPE</code> itself specifies the functional context: otherwise, this context is taken to be specified by the #$Microtheory in which the assertion is made.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabe8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabe8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabe8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabe8,25463).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabe8,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabe8,[]).
x_comment(x_preActors,"An #$ActorSlot (q.v.) that relates an event to  those of its participants that pre-exist it. <code>(#$preActors EVENT OBJECT)</code>  means that <code>OBJECT</code> is a participant (see #$actors) in <code>EVENT</code> and that <code>OBJECT</code>  exists before <code>EVENT</code> begins.  Note that <code>OBJECT</code> also exists during at least part of <code>EVENT</code> (as #$actors generalizes to #$temporallyIntersects).  #$preActors is a very general actor-slot, subsuming #$doneBy, #$objectActedOn, #$objectMoving,  and many others.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabe9).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabe9,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabe9,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabe9,25464).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabe9,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabe9,[]).
x_comment(x_movesWith,"<code>(#$movesWith MOVED MOVER)</code> means that if <code>MOVER</code> undergoes translation (see #$Movement-TranslationEvent) then <code>MOVED</code> by default also undergoes a translation in the same direction and same speed.  However, this default is defeated if <code>MOVED</code> is undergoing motion relative to <code>MOVER</code>. For example, a person would #$movesWith the Titanic if they were #$on-Physical it.  However, if <code>MOVED</code> is moving relative to <code>MOVER</code>, then its 'absolute' motion is computed by subtracting its motion relative to <code>MOVER</code> from the 'absolute' motion vector of <code>MOVER</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabea).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabea,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabea,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabea,25465).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabea,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabea,[]).
x_comment(x_StrictlyMentalEvent,"A specialization of #$AtLeastPartiallyMentalEvent. Each instance of this collection is an event which is 'strictly mental' in the sense that if the event is instantiated by some physical situation, that instantiating situation is not essential to the event being an event of that type.  For instance, perceiving an apple is not an instance of #$StrictlyMentalEvent insofar as it essentially requires sense organs and an apple (and thus #$Perceiving is not a specialization of #$StrictlyMentalEvent). However, the phenomenological aspects of this event, e.g., the experience of redness or roundness, do not require the sense organs or the apple, and it is conceivable that a disembodied mind could have such experiences. (Thus, #$ExperiencingPerception is a specialization of #$StrictlyMentalEvent.)\n<p>\nNote that the property defining this collection is primitive and notoriously difficult to define, except via examples. If an event is not clearly an instance of #$ExperiencingPerception, entertaining a thought (#$Thinking), or #$ExperiencingEmotion, or fully decomposable into a collection of such events, then one should be very careful about making it in an instance of #$StrictlyMentalEvent.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabeb).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabeb,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabeb,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabeb,25466).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabeb,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabeb,[]).
x_comment(x_superTaxons,"A specialization of #$genls used to relate instances of #$BiologicalTaxon.  <code>(#$superTaxons TAXON SUPERTAXON)</code> means that <code>SUPERTAXON</code> is above the more specific <code>TAXON</code> in the biological taxon hierarchy; i.e. the later taxon is more general and inclusive than the former.  Every instance of <code>TAXON</code> must therefore also be an instance of <code>SUPERTAXON</code>.  For example, (#$superTaxons #$DomesticCat #$FelisGenus) holds.  See also #$taxonMembers.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabec).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabec,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabec,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabec,25469).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabec,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabec,[]).
x_comment(x_holdsSometimeDuring,"An instance of both #$TemporalPredicate and #$ModalPredicate that is used to state (in a somewhat indirect way) that a given sentence is true at some point within a certain time period.  <code>(#$holdsSometimeDuring TEMP SENT)</code> means that <code>SENT</code> is true at some  moment in the #$temporalExtent (q.v.) of the #$TemporalThing <code>TEMP</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, the sentence <code>(#$holdsSometimeDuring (#$MonthFn #$July (#$YearFn 2003)) (#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat))</code> means that at some moment in the  month of July 2003 Curtis had Patches as a pet.  This entails (e.g.) that Curtis had Patches at some moment in 2003:\n<pre>\n  (#$holdsSometimeDuring\n    (#$YearFn 2003)\n    (#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat)).\n</pre>\n<p>\nSimilarly, <code>(#$holdsSometimeDuring #$Patches-Cat (#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat))</code> means that Curtis has Patches as a pet at some point during Patches's lifetime.\n<p>\nSee also the specialization #$holdsIn.\n<p>\nNote that a sentence of the form <code>(#$holdsSometimeDuring TEMP (PRED ... ARGi ...))</code>, where <code>ARGi</code> is a #$TemporalThing, does <i>not</i> in general imply that <code>ARGi</code> temporally intersects <code>TEMP</code>.  For example, <code>(#$holdsSometimeDuring (#$YearFn 2003) (#$awareOf #$Curtis #$GeorgeWashington))</code> obviously doesn't imply that #$GeorgeWashington was alive at some point in 2003.  <code>ARGi</code>'s temporally intersecting (indeed, its temporally <i>subsuming</i>) <code>TEMP</code> <i>does</i> follow, however, if <code>PRED</code> is a #$CotemporalPredicate that is #$contemporaryInArg (q.v.) in its <code>i</code>th argument-place (as #$hasPets is, in both argument-places).\n<p>\nNote also that there are two other ways to temporally-qualify sentences in CycL, which do not involve using #$holdsSometimeDuring (or #$holdsIn).  Both involve temporally qualifying the #$Microtheory in which the (otherwise unqualified) sentence is asserted, and either can be used to state something roughly equivalent to a #$holdsSometimeDuring sentence.  Consider the second (displayed)  Curtis/Patches example above.  First, one could assert <code>(#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat)</code> in some microtheory <code>MT</code> such that <code>(#$holdsInTime-Sometime MT (#$YearFn 2003))</code>.  Second, one could assert <code>(#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat)</code> in a microtheory <code>MT'</code> such that both <code>(#$mtTimeIndex MT' (#$YearFn 2003))</code> and <code>(#$mtTimeParameter MT' #$Null-TimeParameter)</code> hold.  This later method, being the most developed and well-supported, is generally preferred over the others.\n<p>\nNote finally that it would be incorrect to assert a sentence like <code>(#$hasPets #$Curtis #$Patches-Cat)</code> in a non-temporally-qualified microtheory like the #$BaseKB, since Curtis didn't have Patches when (e.g.) he was a young boy, let alone in 3500 BCE.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabed).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabed,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
