x_comment(x_transitiveInArgs,"The expression `(#$transitiveInArgs PRED N M)' means that the #$Predicate PRED is transitive in its Nth and Mth arguments, if  its other arguments are held fixed.  Specifically, if the Mth argument in one assertion using PRED is the same as the Nth argument in another assertion using PRED, and all arguments other than the Nth and Mth are the same in the two assertions, then the sentence obtained by replacing the Mth argument in the first assertion with the Mth argument in the second sentence holds.\n<p>\nThus, for example, if #$greaterThanOrEqualWRTScalar is ternary and (#$transitiveInArgs #$greaterThanOrEqualWRTScalar 1 2), (#$greaterThanOrEqualWRTScalar VAL1 VAL2 PRED), and (#$greaterThanOrEqualWRTScalar VAL2 VAL3 PRED) all hold, then (#$greaterThanOrEqualWRTScalar VAL1 VAL3 PRED) also holds.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabfc).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabfc,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabfc,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabfc,25512).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabfc,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabfc,[]).
x_comment(x_CommercialActivity,"A collection of events and a specialization of both #$EconomicAction and #$Transaction.  Instances of this collection include all types of buying, selling, offering to sell, offering to buy, marketing, paying for bought items, requesting bids, performing services for hire, advertising, etc. Its many specializations include #$LoanRepaying, #$Renting and #$ProductRecall.\n<p>\nThough there is a great deal of overlap between this collection and #$BusinessEvent, they are not the same. Firstly, #$BusinessEvent does not include any purely social or recreational events (whereas a weekend swap-meet, for example, would be an instance of #$CommercialActivity). Secondly, not all activities carried out in pursuit of business interests fall under the definition of commercial activity above (consider head-hunting, for example).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabfd).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabfd,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabfd,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabfd,25515).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabfd,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabfd,[]).
x_comment(x_surroundsHorizontally,"<code>(#$surroundsHorizontally OUTSIDE INSIDE)</code> means that <code>OUTSIDE</code> surrounds a horizontal cross-section of <code>INSIDE</code>.  That is, there is some horizontal cross-section of <code>INSIDE</code> such that all rays drawn horizontally from points in that cross-section pass through <code>OUTSIDE</code>, and along each of those rays there are points which are in <code>OUTSIDE</code> but not in <code>INSIDE</code>.  Note that <code>(#$surroundsHorizontally OUTSIDE INSIDE)</code> is <i>not</i> true if <code>INSIDE</code> shares a boundary with <code>OUTSIDE</code>; for example, Texas is not surrounded by the USA.  Positive cases: water surrounds islands, and foothills may surround a mountain range.  See also #$surroundsCompletely.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabfe).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabfe,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabfe,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabfe,25518).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabfe,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabfe,[]).
x_comment(x_arg6Isa,"A binary #$ArgIsaPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$arg6Isa RELN COL)</code>  means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the sixth argument to <code>RELN</code> must be an instance of <code>COL</code>.  That is,  <code>(RELN ARG1 ARG2 ARG3 ARG4 ARG5 ARG6 ...)</code> is semantically well-formed only if  <code>(#$isa ARG6 COL)</code> holds.  For an explanation of semantic well-formedness, see  #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  See #$argIsa for a similar but more flexible ternary predicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aabff).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aabff,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aabff,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aabff,25519).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aabff,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aabff,[]).
x_comment(x_relationAllInstance,"A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate that is used to state that a given binary predicate holds between all instances of a given collection and a given thing. <code>(#$relationAllInstance PRED COL THING)</code> means that every instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL</code> bears the relation <code>PRED</code> to <code>THING</code> such that <code>(PRED INST THING)</code> holds.  It is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:\n<p>\n<pre>\n     (#$implies\n          (#$isa ?INST COL)\n          (PRED ?INST THING)) .\n</pre>\n<p>\nFor example, '(#$relationAllInstance #$maxSpeed #$GolfCart (#$MilesPerHour 15))' means that the maximum speed of every golf cart is 15 miles per hour. Cf. #$relationInstanceAll, and see #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac00).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac00,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac00,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac00,25520).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac00,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac00,[]).
x_comment(x_outputsCreated,"An #$ActorSlot that relates #$CreationEvents to things -- tangible objects or other \"enduring\" things (see #$SomethingExisting) -- created by those events. <code>(#$outputsCreated EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> doesn't exist before <code>EVENT</code> but comes into existence sometime during <code>EVENT</code>, as a result of <code>EVENT</code>.  #$outputsCreated is a specialization of #$nonDeliberateActors, since it would in general be impossible for <code>OBJECT</code> to act deliberately in its own creation.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac01).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac01,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac01,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac01,25529).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac01,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac01,[]).
x_comment(x_backchainForbidden,"#$backchainForbidden is a predicate that allows users to cut down on the number of rules used to prove certain literals, and thus to increase the speed of inferences that involve proving those literals.  <code>(#$backchainForbidden PREDICATE)</code> means that no rules containing a positive literal with <code>PREDICATE</code> as the arg0 (in other words, no #$implies assertions in which a literal with <code>PREDICATE</code> as the arg0 appears in the consequent) will be used when attempting to prove (non-negated) literals with <code>PREDICATE</code> as the arg0.  Asserting <code>(#$backchainForbidden PREDICATE)</code> is useful when all (non-negated) literals with <code>PREDICATE</code> as the arg0 are expected to be solvable by lookup of ground atomic formulas (GAF's) already in the KB, and so rules won't be needed to prove them.  However, asserting <code>(#$backchainForbidden PREDICATE)</code> is usually counter-productive in contexts where some literals with <code>PREDICATE</code> as the arg0 are expected to be proven via backchaining on rules containing a positive literal with <code>PREDICATE</code> as the arg0.  See also the related predicates #$backchainRequired, #$backchainEncouraged, and #$backchainDiscouraged.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac02).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac02,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac02,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac02,25530).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac02,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac02,[]).
x_comment(x_deviceUsed,"The predicate #$deviceUsed relates an event to a device used in that event. <code>(#$deviceUsed EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that the #$PhysicalDevice <code>OBJECT</code> plays an instrumental role in the #$Event <code>EVENT</code> (see the more generalized predicate #$instrument-Generic), <code>OBJECT</code> is intentionally used in <code>EVENT</code>, and standardly (for example, in the #$HumanActivitiesMt) <code>OBJECT</code>'s role in <code>EVENT</code> is consistent with the object's #$primaryFunction.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac03).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac03,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac03,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac03,25531).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac03,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac03,[]).
x_comment(x_eventTypeOccursAtLocationType,"A #$BinaryPredicate that relates a specialization of #$Event-Localized to a specialization of #$SpatialThing-Localized.  <code>(#$eventTypeOccursAtLocationType EVENT-TYPE LOC-TYPE)</code> means that every instance of <code>EVENT-TYPE</code> occurs at an instance of <code>LOC-TYPE</code>. For example (#$eventTypeOccursAtLocationType #$HavingAPicnic #$OutdoorLocation) means that all picnics occur outdoors.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac04).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac04,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac04,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac04,25532).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac04,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac04,[]).
x_comment(x_extent,"A #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot predicate that relates a given set or collection (see #$SetOrCollection) to its extension: the set consisting of all of (and only) its elements (see #$elementOf).  <code>(#$extent SETORCOL SET)</code> means that every element of <code>SETORCOL</code> is an element of <code>SET</code> and every element of <code>SET</code> is an element of <code>SETORCOL</code>. If <code>SETORCOL</code> happens to be a set (see #$Set-Mathematical) itself, then <code>SET</code> and <code>SETORCOL</code> are identical (see #$equals), as sets are identified and distinguished strictly in terms of their elements.  Conversely, if <code>SETORCOL</code> happens to be a collection (see #$Collection), then <code>SET</code> and <code>SETORCOL</code> are distinct (see #$different), as no collection is a set.  See also #$coextensionalSetOrCollections.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac05).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac05,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac05,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac05,25533).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac05,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac05,[]).
x_comment(x_inDomainOf,"A #$MetaRelation predicate that relates instances of #$Relation\nto things in their domains.  (#$inDomainOf ELEMENT RELATION)\nmeans that ELEMENT is an element of the #$relationDomain (q.v.)\nof RELATION.  That is, RELATION relates ELEMENT to at least\none thing in RELATION'S range (see #$relationRange and\n#$inRangeOf).  If the #$relationalArity (q.v.) of RELATION\nis N (for N > 1), then ELEMENT is an (N-1)-length instance of\n#$List such that RELATION holds among ELEMENT's N-1\nmembers (taken in the order of their occurrence in the list)\nand some THING (taken as filling RELATION's Nth argument-\nposition).  In other words, ELEMENT consists of the first\nN-1 members (taken in order) of some N-length list in the\n#$relationExtension of RELATION.  If RELATION is unary it\nhas no domain, and #$inDomainOf is undefined for such a\nRELATION.  If RELATION is a #$VariableArityRelation (q.v.)\nthere will be no particular length N-1 shared by each of\nthe lists in its domain.\n<p>\nThough this predicate can be applied to polyadic relations\ngenerally, asserting that something is in RELATION's domain\nwill normally be of particular interest only when RELATION\nis a function (i.e. an instance of #$Function-Denotational),\nin which case RELATION relates each element of its domain to\nexactly one element of its range.  See also #$DomainOfFunctionFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac06).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac06,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac06,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac06,25534).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac06,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac06,[]).
x_comment(x_inputsDestroyed,"The predicate #$inputsDestroyed is used to relate a particular event to the item or items destroyed in that event.  <code>(#$inputsDestroyed EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> exists before <code>EVENT</code>, is affected by <code>EVENT</code>, and due to that involvement, ends its existence at some time during <code>EVENT</code>.  Note that <code>OBJECT</code> may or may not be acting deliberately (see #$deliberateActors) in <code>EVENT</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac07).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac07,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac07,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac07,25558).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac07,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac07,[]).
x_comment(x_subSituations,"This predicate relates situations to further situations which are their parts.  <code>(#$subSituations SIT SUBSIT)</code> means that the #$Situation <code>SUBSIT</code> is a part of the #$Situation <code>SIT</code> such that a complete description of <code>SIT</code> would have to involve a description of <code>SUBSIT</code>.  For example, a situation in which a car engine is non-functioning might have as a sub-situation the situation of a carburetor being misconfigured.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac08).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac08,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac08,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac08,25559).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac08,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac08,[]).
x_comment(x_quantitySubsumes,"A specialization of both #$quantityIntersects and #$intangibleParts, this predicate relates a given numerically measurable #$ScalarInterval to those scalar intervals that it subsumes or that \"fall completely inside\" it.    <code>(#$quantitySubsumes SUPER SUB)</code> means that both (i) the maximum (see #$maxQuantValue) of <code>SUPER</code> is #$greaterThanOrEqualTo the maximum of <code>SUB</code> and (ii) the minimum (see #$minQuantValue) of <code>SUPER</code> is #$lessThanOrEqualTo the minimum of <code>SUB</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$Meter 2 5) -- the #$Distance of between 2 and 5 meters inclusive -- subsumes both (#$Meter 3 4) and (#$Meter 3 5), but not (#$Meter 3 6).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac09).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac09,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac09,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac09,25560).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac09,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac09,[]).
x_comment(x_IncarnationPhysicalFn,"A function which returns the set of individuals that collectively comprise the physical incarnations of the stated divine being. Thus, in the #$ChristianityMt, #$JesusOfNazareth is the physical incarnation of both #$JesusChrist (the divine being representing the son of #$GodOfAbrahamIsaacAndJacob) and the physical incarnation of #$GodOfAbrahamIsaacAndJacob itself. Likewise, the resurrected Christ who appeared to the apostles is also a physical incarnation of #$JesusChrist. This introduces issues of the holy Trinity that should be investigated further in Cyc.\n<p>\nReligions which believe in reincarnation may also be axiomatized with this function, if one considers sentient animals as physical incarnations of a non-physical 'soul', where soul denotes a #$DivineBeing. Since some religions believe that people can be reincarnated as lower animals, the result of #$IncarnationPhysicalFn must be specified (hence it is a binary function).  Thus, we can tell Cyc that B is a reincarnation of A if there exists some divine-being (call it a soul) such that A and B are both physical incarnations of this being, and B is born after A dies (see #$Reincarnation)") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac0a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac0a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac0a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac0a,25563).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac0a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac0a,[]).
x_comment(x_PerFn,"A binary #$UnitOfMeasureDenotingFunction (q.v.) that takes two #$UnitOfMeasure (q.v.) functions and returns a #$UnitOfRatio (q.v.) composed of them.  <code>(#$PerFn UNIT1 UNIT2)</code> is a unit <code>RATIO-FN</code> for measuring the ratio of <code>UNIT1</code> units to <code>UNIT2</code> units.   That is, for any number or other #$NumericInterval <code>NUM</code>, <code>(RATIO-FN NUM)</code> = <code>(#$QuotientFn (UNIT1 NUM) (UNIT2 1))</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$PerFn #$Meter #$SecondsDuration) is a #$UnitOfSpeed function that measures meters-per-second.  When applied to (e.g.) the integer 20, this unit-of-speed returns the particular #$Speed equal to\n<pre>\n  (#$QuotientFn (#$Meter 20) (#$SecondsDuration 1))\n</pre>\nor 20 meters-per-second.\n<p>\nNote that <code>UNIT1</code> and <code>UNIT2</code> must <i>not</i> be #$interconvertibleUnits (q.v.), or <code>(#$PerFn UNIT1 UNIT2)</code> is #$undefined.  Otherwise, <code>UNIT1</code> and <code>UNIT2</code> would effectively \"cancel each other out\", and the value returned by #$PerFn for those arguments would not be a #$UnitOfRatio.\n<p>\nSee also #$UnitProductFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac0b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac0b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac0b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac0b,25566).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac0b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac0b,[]).
x_comment(x_dateOfEvent,"A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate that is a specialization of both #$startingDate and #$endingDate (qq.v.).  #$dateOfEvent relates an event to a #$Date on or in which it transpired.  <code>(#$dateOfEvent EVENT DATE)</code> means that <code>EVENT</code> both starts and ends during <code>DATE</code>.  For example, the sentence\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$dateOfEvent\n        #$GettysburgAddress-Speech\n        (#$DayFn 19 (#$MonthFn #$November (#$YearFn 1863))))\n</pre>\n<p>\nis true because Lincoln both started and finished that address on the date mentioned.\n<p>\nSee also #$occursDuring.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac0c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac0c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac0c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac0c,25577).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac0c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac0c,[]).
x_comment(x_startTogetherInSituationType,"<code>(#$startTogetherInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> start at the same time.\n<p>\nNote that <code>(#$startTogetherInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac0d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac0d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac0d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac0d,25588).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac0d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac0d,[]).
x_comment(x_transitiveViaArg,"A #$MetaPredicate used for stating that a given predicate behaves transitively, in a specified argument-place, with respect to a given binary predicate. <code>(#$transitiveViaArg PRED BINPRED N)</code> means that the <code>N</code>th argument position of <code>PRED</code> is \"transitive\" with respect to <code>BINPRED</code>.  That is, if <code>(PRED ... ARG-N ...)</code> and <code>(BINPRED ARG-N ARG-N-PRIME)</code> hold, then so does <code>(PRED ... ARG-N-PRIME ...)</code>.  For example, (#$transitiveViaArg #$relationAllExists #$genlPreds 1) holds; thus from (#$relationAllExists #$anatomicalParts #$Mammal #$Head-AnimalBodyPart) and (#$genlPreds #$anatomicalParts #$physicalParts) it follows that (#$relationAllExists #$physicalParts #$Mammal #$Head-AnimalBodyPart). See also #$transitiveViaArgInverse.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac0e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac0e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac0e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac0e,25591).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac0e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac0e,[]).
x_comment(x_perpetrator,"An #$AgentiveRole and a specialization of #$performedBy. <code>(#$perpetrator ACTION PERP)</code> means that the #$SocialBeing <code>PERP</code> is a perpetrator of the arguably criminal <code>ACTION</code>.  More  precisely: <code>PERP</code> performs <code>ACTION</code>, where <code>ACTION</code> is likely to  be considered criminal by some observers (or at least would  be so considered in some contexts).  Note that the first argument of #$perpetrator is not constrained (see #$argIsa) to #$CriminalActs, since an act that counts as criminal in one context might not count as criminal in another context, and yet we might still want to talk about the \"perpetrator\" of that act in the latter context.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac0f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac0f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac0f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac0f,25592).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac0f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac0f,[]).
x_comment(x_hasBeenIn,"A predicate linking a thing to its prior history; <code>(#$hasBeenIn OBJECT PLACE)</code> means that the thing <code>OBJECT</code> has at one time during its lifetime been in (in the general sense of #$inRegion) the thing <code>PLACE</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac10).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac10,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac10,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
