x_comment(x_purposeOfEvent,"When <code>(#$purposeOfEvent EVENT GOAL)</code> is asserted within the context of a           particular planning context, this means that the purpose of <code>EVENT</code> within the           planning context is <code>GOAL</code>. What this implies will vary from context to context:           however there will often be the entailment that rational agents in the context who are           tasked with performing the <code>EVENT</code> in question will themselves have <code>GOAL</code> as a           goal (see #$goals). For the present, we remain agnostic as to how an event may inherit a           'purpose', apart from stipulating that <code>(#$purposeInEvent AGENT EVENT GOAL)</code>           implies <code>(#$purposeOfEvent EVENT GOAL)</code>. However, it seems fairly certain that           <code>(#$purposeOfEvent EVENT GOAL)</code> implies that there is an Agent performing <code>EVENT</code>           who has <code>GOAL</code> as a purpose: for this reason the first argument is constrained to be a           #$PurposefulAction.  Also, see  #$purposeOfEvent-GoalType.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac14).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac14,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac14,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac14,25599).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac14,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac14,[]).
x_comment(x_FlowRate,"A specialization of #$Rate and #$PhysicalQuantity (qq.v.).\nEach instance of #$FlowRate is a #$Volume taken with respect to (i.e. divided by; see #$QuotientFn) an amount of time (see #$Time-Quantity).  For example, (#$CubicFootPerSecond 10) -- the flow rate of 10 cubic feet per second -- might be the  rate at which the water in a given stream is flowing.\n<p>\nSee also #$UnitOfFlowRate.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac15).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac15,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac15,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac15,25600).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac15,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac15,[]).
x_comment(x_children,"A #$FamilyRelationSlot and specialization of #$coreRelatives that relates  a given person or other animal to each of his or her children.   <code>(#$children PARENT CHILD)</code> means that <code>PARENT</code> fulfills the role of parent  with respect to <code>CHILD</code>.  Note that, in the case of humans, <code>PARENT</code>'s  being one of <code>CHILD</code>'s biological parents (see #$biologicalOffspring) is  neither necessary nor sufficient for this.  Thus, <code>CHILD</code> might be an adopted child (see #$adoptedChildren), stepchild, or foster child of  <code>PARENT</code>'s.  Conversely, a biological parent who has given a child up for  adoption would <i>not</i> normally be related to that child by the predicate  #$children.  In the case of nonhuman animals (see #$NonPersonAnimal),  however, #$children holds between two given creatures if and only if  #$biologicalOffspring does.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac16).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac16,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac16,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac16,25604).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac16,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac16,[]).
x_comment(x_VeryHighAmountFn,"A #$GenericValueFunction (q.v.) that takes a given #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType to a #$GenericQuantity of that type.  In a given context, <code>(#$VeryHighAmountFn QUANTTYPE)</code> is the properly interval-like quantity (and instance) of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> that is considered a <i>very high amount</i> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> with respect to that context.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$VeryHighAmountFn #$Happiness) is a high amount of happiness.  It is more (see #$followingValue) than (#$HighAmountFn #$Happiness).\n<p>\nSee also the corresponding predicate #$veryHighAmountOf.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac17).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac17,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac17,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac17,25605).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac17,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac17,[]).
x_comment(x_unitMultiplicationFactor,"A ternary #$MetaFunction predicate that relates two interconvertible #$UnitOfMeasures (q.v.) to the real number by which the larger unit is a multiple of the smaller.  <code>(#$unitMultiplicationFactor SMALLUNIT BIGUNIT N)</code> means that there are <code>N SMALLUNIT</code>s in one <code>BIGUNIT</code>.  In other words, to convert from a measure <code>BIGUNIT</code>s to a measure in <code>SMALLUNIT</code>s, multiply by <code>N</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$unitMultiplicationFactor #$Inch #$Foot-UnitOfMeasure 12) entails that, when converting from feet to inches, one multiplies the number of feet by twelve.\n<p>\nNote that <code>SMALLUNIT</code> and <code>BIGUNIT</code> must be instances of the same #$InterconvertibleUnitType (q.v.), and that <code>N</code> must be greater than or equal to 1.\n<p>\nSee also #$QuantityConversionFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac18).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac18,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac18,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac18,25610).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac18,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac18,[]).
x_comment(x_propositionalInfoAbout,"A binary #$IntangibleObjectPredicate that relates intangible \"information stores\" (see #$AspatialInformationStore) to the things they are \"about\".  (#$propositionalInfoAbout AIS THING)  means that the AIS is or contains propositional information about THING.  For instance, AIS might be a #$Microtheory with assertions concerning THING or a #$TextualPCW containing information about THING.  This relation constitutes a kind of \"intentionality\" in the philosophers' sense (see e.g. #$IntentionalMentalSituation), though some would argue that it is only an indirect or derived sort of intentionality in that it always presupposes an underlying intention on the part of some agent(s).\n<p>\nAlthough the notion of a written text or other sort of conceptual work being (propositionally) \"about\" something is a completely familiar one -- it is regularly appealed to in everyday discourse as well as in academic studies  pertaining to meaning or interpretation -- giving a precise analysis of the notion (in the form of necessary and sufficient conditions, e.g.) is a  notoriously difficult task, shot-through with controversy.  Still, a couple of non-contentious points can be made in this direction.  First, it is clear that the information in question must be sufficiently specific to the thing it is \"about\".  That is, in order for AIS to \"contain propositional information about\" THING it is _not_ sufficient that AIS merely involve general information about the elements of some collection (or set or class) to which THING belongs, but must involve some way of singling-out THING, e.g. by mentioning THING by name or containing a description that uniquely applies to THING.  For example, the sentence 'Dogs are carnivores' is too general to  be \"about\" Lassie (though it _is_ arguably about the collections #$Dog and  #$Carnivore); 'Television's best-loved canine was a collie', on the other hand, is certainly about her.  Second, since the information must be _propositional_ (see e.g. #$Proposition), it is also _not_ sufficient that AIS merely contain a term or symbol that specifies or denotes or represents THING; AIS must \"say something about\" THING.  Thus, a mere unannotated list of the names of famous show-biz animals would _not_ be propositionally about Lassie, even though her name would undoubtedly appear on it.  Finally, note that #$propositionalInfoAbout does _not_ insist that THING is what AIS is mainly or focally about; thus the selfsame AIS might easily be about several (even many) different things at the same time.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac19).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac19,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac19,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac19,25617).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac19,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac19,[]).
x_comment(x_actors,"The most general instance of #$ActorSlot (q.v.): all other actor slots are specializations of this predicate.  Thus #$actors is a broad relation that holds between a given event and any contemporary existing thing (see #$SomethingExisting) that is meaningfully involved in the event. <code>(#$actors EVENT ACTOR)</code> means that <code>ACTOR</code> is somehow saliently (directly or indirectly) involved in <code>EVENT</code> during <code>EVENT</code>.  An object's merely being cotemporal (see #$cotemporal) with an event is obviously not sufficient for being an actor in it, nor is participating (however centrally) in a representation or re-enactment of the event.  But note that being an \"actor\" in the relevant sense is <i>not</i> restricted to things that play \"active\" (as opposed to passive or instrumental) roles in a given event.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac1a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac1a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac1a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac1a,25620).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac1a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac1a,[]).
x_comment(x_relationInstanceExistsRange,"<code>(#$relationInstanceExistsRange PRED INS1 COL2 EXTENT)</code> means that there exist <code>EXTENT</code> many instances (e.g., <code>INS2)</code> of <code>COL2</code> that have the relation <code>PRED</code> with <code>INS1</code>, i.e. <code>(PRED INS1 INS2)</code> holds. Thus, (#$relationInstanceExistsRange #$legalOwnerOf #$Blake #$RaceCar #$AFew-Quant) means simply \"Blake owns a few race cars.\".") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac1b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac1b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac1b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac1b,25621).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac1b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac1b,[]).
x_comment(x_ReciprocalFn,"An instance of both #$UnaryFunction and #$EvaluatableFunction.  When applied to an instance SCALAR of #$ScalarInterval, #$ReciprocalFn returns an instance RECIPROCAL of #$ScalarInterval that is the reciprocal of SCALAR.  If SCALAR is an instance of #$ScalarPointValue, then RECIPROCAL is an instance of #$ScalarPointValue whose product (see #$TimesFn) with SCALAR is 1.  For instance, (#$ReciprocalFn 2) is 0.5 and (#$ReciprocalFn (#$Meter 2)) is ((#$PerFn #$Unity #$Meter) 0.5), a quantity whose product with (#$Meter 2) is 1.  If SCALAR is a range of values (rather than an instance of #$ScalarPointValue), then RECIPROCAL is also a range of values, with the reciprocal of the maximum value of SCALAR serving as RECIPROCAL's minimum value, and the reciprocal of the minimum value of SCALAR serving as RECIPROCAL's maximum value.  For example, (#$ReciprocalFn (#$Unity 1 2)) (i.e., the reciprocal of the range of numbers from 1 to 2) is (#$Unity 0.5 1).  Note that when SCALAR and RECIPROCAL are ranges of values, the product of SCALAR and RECIPROCAL may not be 1; for example, the product of (#$ReciprocalFn (#$Unity 1 2)) and (#$Unity 1 2) is (#$Unity 0.5 2).  Nevertheless, even when the product of SCALAR and RECIPROCAL is not 1, 1 will lie within the range (see #$valueInRange) of the product of SCALAR and RECIPROCAL.  Finally, note that (#$ReciprocalFn 0) is undefined.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac1c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac1c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac1c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac1c,25622).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac1c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac1c,[]).
x_comment(x_NonVerbalCommunicating,"A collection of information transfer events; a specialization of #$Communicating. Each instance of #$NonVerbalCommunicating is a transmission of information that is not in the form of language. Thus spoken, written, and sign-language communications are excluded.  Specializations of #$NonVerbalCommunicating include #$ShakingHands and #$Cuddling.\n<p>\nNote that #$MakingAGesture is <i>not</i> a subcollection of #$NonVerbalCommunicating, since instances of #$MakingAGesture are merely information-encodings, upon which communication (even <i>verbal</i> communication, in the case sign-language) might or might not occur).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac1d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac1d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac1d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac1d,25623).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac1d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac1d,[]).
x_comment(x_homepage,"A #$FunctionalSlot that relates anything to the URL of its homepage on the World Wide Web. <code>(#$homepage THING STRING)</code> means that <code>THING</code> has a World Wide Web home page with the #$UniformResourceLocator <code>STRING</code>.  For example, (#$homepage #$NationalFootballLeague \"http://www.nfl.com\") means that if a browser is directed to the URL \"http://www.nfl.com\", it will display the homepage for the NFL.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac1e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac1e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac1e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac1e,25626).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac1e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac1e,[]).
x_comment(x_PartTypeFn(x_RightObjectOfPairFn(x_SixthRib),x_BoneShaft),"<p>") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac1f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac1f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac1f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
