x_comment(x_TimeElapsedFn,"A commutative binary #$ScalarDenotingFunction that returns the duration (see #$Time-Quantity) of the time interval between the starting points of two #$Dates.  Thus, sentences of the following form hold:\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$duration\n    (#$TimeIntervalBetweenFn (#$StartFn DATE1) (#$StartFn DATE2))\n    (#$TimeElapsedFn DATE1 DATE2)\n</pre>\n<p>\nFor example, (#$TimeElapsedFn (#$YearFn 1990) (#$YearFn 1992)) is equal to (#$YearsDuration 2), the duration of 2 years.  An example of an axiom using this function would be one saying that a female animal generally won't give birth more than once per gestation-period-of-time (for that type of animal).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac45).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac45,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac45,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac45,25716).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac45,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac45,[]).
x_comment(x_culturePractices,"This predicate relates human cultures to types of    practices that are characteristic of and important to their participants.  (#$culturePractices CULTURE ACTTYPE ROLE) means that most or many participants in CULTURE play ROLE in instances of ACTTYPE from time to time, and would (at least upon reflection) consider such activities important to or distinctive of CULTURE.  These activities might be ones commonly engaged in on a regular basis (like praying thrice daily), or might be ones normally engaged in only in particular situations or on special occasions (like joining in group singing at parties); all that's essential is that many or most participants in the culture do them sometimes.\n<p>\nFor a similar predicate that applies to practices distinctive of a culture but participated in by only a relatively small, specialized subcollection of its participants, see #$cultureSpecialPractices.\n<p>\nNote that when using this predicate it is important to avoid unjustified    stereotypes of the culture in question and to refrain from relating the culture to practices that do not accurately characterize it.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac46).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac46,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac46,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac46,25725).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac46,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac46,[]).
x_comment(x_SquareRootFn,"A unary #$MathematicalFunctionOnScalars that is the arithmetical operation that takes a (non-negative) number to its non-negative square root, extended to measurable #$ScalarIntervals generally.  <code>(#$SquareRootFn SCALAR)</code> is by definition equal to <code>ROOT</code>: the #$NonNegativeScalarInterval such that <code>(#$TimesFn ROOT ROOT)</code> is equal to <code>SCALAR</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$SquareRootFn 81) is equal to 9 and (#$SquareRootFn (#$SquareMile 9)) is equal to (#$Mile 3).\n<p>\nNote that while this holds:\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$inverseQuantFunctions-Nonsymmetric #$SquaredFn #$SquareRootFn) ,\n</pre>\n<p>\nthis does <i>not</i>:\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$inverseQuantFunctions-Nonsymmetric #$SquareRootFn #$SquaredFn) .\n</pre>\n<p>\nFor the square of the non-negative square root of <code>X</code> is always <code>X</code>, but the non-negative square root of the square of <code>X</code> is not always <code>X</code> (e.g. where <code>X</code>  is -2).  Thus, #$SquareRootFn and #$SquaredFn might be considered \"quasi-inverses\".") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac47).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac47,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac47,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac47,25730).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac47,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac47,[]).
x_comment(x_thereExists,"An #$ExistentialQuantifier (q.v.) that corresponds to the standard existential quantifier of predicate calculus.  #$thereExists takes as its arguments an #$ELVariable and an #$ELSentence-Assertible (such that, typically, the former occurs free in the latter).  (#$thereExists VAR SENT) means that there is at least one thing THING such that SENT is true of it.  That is, if some given CycL term that denotes THING were substituted for each free occurrence of VAR in SENT, the result would be a true sentence.  For example, `(#$thereExists ?X (#$mother ?X #$GeorgeWBush))' means that George W. Bush has a (i.e. at least one) mother; and\n<p>\n(#$forAll ?PER\n(#$implies\n(#$isa ?PER #$Person)\n(#$thereExists ?MOM\n(#$mother ?MOM ?PER))))\n<p>\nmeans that every person has a mother.  Note that some existential statements can be represented more tersely in CycL using other instances of #$ExistentialQuantifier, such as #$thereExistAtMost or #$thereExistExactly.  Also see the #$cyclistNotes for #$thereExists.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac48).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac48,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac48,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac48,25747).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac48,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac48,[]).
x_comment(x_mtCultureBeliefs,"<code>(#$mtCultureBeliefs MT CULTURE)</code> means that <code>MT</code> contains assertions having the propositional content of the beliefs that  characterize <code>CULTURE</code> (see #$cultureBeliefs).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac49).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac49,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac49,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac49,25756).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac49,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac49,[]).
x_comment(x_forAll,"A binary #$Quantifier that corresponds to the standard universal quantifier of predicate calculus.  The relation #$forAll takes as its arguments a variable (#$ELVariable) and a sentence (#$ELSentence-Assertible) in which, typically, that variable occurs free.  A closed formula (see #$CycLClosedFormula) of the form (#$forAll VAR SENT) is a \"universally quantified\" sentence that states (roughly) that every thing in the universe of discourse satisfies SENT.  For example,\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$forAll ?THING (#$isa ?THING #$Individual))\n</pre>\n<p>\nmeans that everything is an individual.  And the sentence\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$forAll ?X\n    (#$implies\n      (#$isa ?X #$Cat)\n      (#$eatsWillingly ?X #$Grass-Plant)))\n</pre>\n<p>\nmeans that all cats eat grass.\n<p>\nA more precise definition of #$forAll is as follows.  Any occurence of VAR that is free in SENT is bound in (#$forAll VAR SENT).  If VAR is the only variable free in SENT, then (#$forAll VAR SENT) is a closed sentence that is true if and only if every thing that meets all of the argument-type constraints (see #$ArgTypePredicate) to which the position(s) in SENT occupied by VAR is (are) subject to satisfies SENT. If the variable VAR does _not_ occur free in SENT, then (#$forAll VAR SENT) is equivalent to SENT (and the '#$forAll' is said to be \"vacuous\").  If variables other than VAR occur free in SENT, then (#$forAll VAR SENT) is an open sentence in which those other variables are still free.\n<p>\nNote that, for the sake of convenience and readability, universally quantified assertions (i.e. \"rules\") in the Cyc Knowledge Base appear in the KB browser -- and may be asserted to the KB -- without (any of) their initial '#$forAll's.  Such assertions are considered to be \"implicitly quantified\": they are interpreted by the system just as if their initial quantifiers were explicitly present.  Thus the above sentence about cats would actually appear in the KB as:\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$implies\n    (#$isa ?X #$Cat)\n    (#$eatsWillingly ?X #$Grass-Plant)) .\n</pre>\n<p>\nNote also that certain #$RuleMacroPredicates (e.g. those with 'All' in their names) can be used to abbreviate universally quantified sentences.  Thus the same statement about cats can be even more tersely expressed with the atomic sentence\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$relationAllInstance #$eatsWillingly #$Cat #$Grass-Plant) .\n</pre>\n<p>\nCf. the existential quantifier #$thereExists.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac4a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac4a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac4a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac4a,25757).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac4a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac4a,[]).
