x_comment(x_informationOrigin,"A specialization of #$informationTerminal  (q.v.) that is used to indicate the source of information for a particular  #$InformationTransferEvent (q.v.).  <code>(#$informationOrigin TRANSFER ORIGIN)</code>  means that the information being transferred in <code>TRANSFER</code> comes from <code>ORIGIN</code>. <code>ORIGIN</code> is either an instance of #$InformationBearingThing or an agent.   Note that if <code>ORIGIN</code> still exists after the ending of <code>TRANSFER</code>, it  presumably also still contains the information that was transferred.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac4f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac4f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac4f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac4f,25797).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac4f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac4f,[]).
x_comment(x_constructActionType,"(#$constructActionType NAME SCRIPTTYPE (#$TheList ACTORSLOT ...)) means construct a\nfunction that is a #$CollectionDenotingFunction, whose name is NAME and whose arguments must be appropriate for\nassertions of the form (ACTORSLOT ?x@SCRIPTTYPE <argument>).  By having this predicate actions in plans\ncan be stated more teresely in an active form, and we should be able to do plan and plan-recognition level reasoning\nover such GAFs. What happens as a result of asserting this is a bit of SubL gets called to actually add a new\naction-type constructor named NAME.\n<p>\nThe effect of the #$afterAdding is to assert (for the following sample)\nAdd: (#$constructActionType #$HitsFn #$HittingAnObject (#$TheList #$doneBy #$objectActedOn))\n<p>\nResults in:\n(#$isa #$HitsFn #$BinaryFunction); from number of items in #$TheList\n(#$isa #$HitsFn #$CollectionDenotingFunction) ; because it denotes a type\n(#$resultGenl #$HitsFn #$HittingAnObject)\n(#$arg1Isa #$HitsFn #$SomethingExisting) ; arg2Isa from the #$doneBy\n(#$arg2Isa #$HitsFn #$SomethingExisting) ; arg2Isa from the #$objectActedOn\n(#$expansion #$HitsFn\n(#$CollectionSubsetFn #$HittingAnObject\n(#$TheSetOf ?hitting (#$and (#$doneBy ?hitting :arg1)\n(#$objectActedOn ?hitting :arg2)))))") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac50).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac50,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac50,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac50,25798).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac50,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac50,[]).
x_comment(x_EventPredicate,"#$EventPredicate is a collection of predicates that are used to make assertions about events. The representation of events in terms of #$EventPredicates is an alternative to the Davidsonian representation of events (so-called after the philosopher Donald Davidson) in which the \"participants\" in  events are related to the reified individual events in which they participate (for the representational approach, see #$Role, #$ActorSlot, and #$SubEventPredicate). The first argument to an event predicate will always be the \"agentive force\" in the event. For instance, in the event described by the formula\n<p>\n<code>(#$transports LOTUSBLOSSUM099 OBJECT056 FROM TO VIA)</code> ,\n<p>\nLOTUSBLOSSUM099 is the agentive force -- it is what transports OBJECT056.\n<p>\nNote that the agentive force need not be a volitional or intelligent agent; it may just be a causal agent.  For example, a bacterium (or a mob of bacteria) can be the agentive force in an infection.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac51).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac51,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac51,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac51,25818).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac51,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac51,[]).
x_comment(x_MediaSeriesByDateFn,"(#$MediaSeriesByDateFn MEDIA_SERIES_PRODUCT DATE), given MEDIA_SERIES_PRODUCT (a #$MediaSeriesProduct) and DATE (a #$Date), returns the member of this series that was distributed via the mass media on that date.\n<p>\nEach regularly distributed #$MediaSeriesProduct, such as a #$PeriodicalSeries or a #$TVSeriesProduct, has a series of #$MediaProducts as #$subWorks.  These #$subWorks can be ordered by date of media distribution of instantiations of the works.  Instances of #$BroadcastMediaProduct often have different instantiations distributed on different date.  Thus for a  #$TVSeriesProduct that has reruns, the #$MediaSeriesByDateFn of that series for several dates would be the same.\n<p>\n<p>\nFor instance, (#$MediaSeriesByDateFn #$TheNewYorkTimes-TheNewspaper (#$DayFn 15 (#$MonthFn #$January (#$YearFn 1998)))) would represent the #$NewspaperIssue comprising the 1/15/98 edition of The New York Times. Compare #$MediaSeriesNthFn, which specifies the nth #$MediaSeriesProduct of a series published by the date of ORIGINAL publication -- i.e., the date when the first instantiation was published.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac52).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac52,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac52,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac52,25825).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac52,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac52,[]).
x_comment(x_lastProperSubSituationTypes,"<code>(#$lastProperSubSituationTypes SITTYPE SUBSITTYPE)</code> means that one of the last sub-situation types in <code>SITTYPE</code> is of type <code>SUBSITTYPE</code>.  Note that a situation type may have more than one final sub-situation type because the sub-situation types are only partially ordered by type-level quasi-temporal relations.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac53).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac53,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac53,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac53,25831).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac53,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac53,[]).
x_comment(x_employees,"A specialization of #$hasWorkers (q.v.) that relates employers to their  paid employees.  <code>(#$employees EMPLOYER WORKER)</code> means <code>WORKER</code> regularly  performs work for <code>EMPLOYER</code>, and <code>EMPLOYER</code> pays <code>WORKER</code> for that work (often  by paycheck).  <code>EMPLOYER</code> directs the manner in which <code>WORKER</code> performs the  work and might provide the workplace, tools, materials, capital, or other  assistance for the work.  <code>EMPLOYER</code> is commonly an organization but may be  a person; e.g. (#$employees PerryMason PaulDrake).  Note that in many contexts the use of this predicate require proper temporal qualification;  e.g. (#$ist (#$MtSpace #$PeopleDataMt (#$MtTimeWithGranularityDimFn (#$YearFn 1999) #$TimePoint))) (#$employees #$NBC-TVNetwork #$KatieCouric)).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac54).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac54,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac54,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac54,25832).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac54,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac54,[]).
x_comment(x_StudyOfFn,"<code>(#$StudyOfFn THING)</code> denotes the collection of activities in which <code>THING</code> is studied.  The instances in these collections are described in common English as instances of 'the study of <code>THING</code>'. <code>(#$StudyOfFn THING)</code> may well include direct encounters with <code>THING</code>, and need have nothing to do with #$Education. #$StudyOfFn is similar to, but more general than, #$ResearchInFn.  For example, reading extant material about <code>THING</code> would generally be included in <code>(#$StudyOfFn THING)</code>, while it may well not be in <code>(#$ResearchInFn THING)</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac55).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac55,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac55,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac55,25833).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac55,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac55,[]).
x_comment(x_parts,"This predicate relates individuals to their (individual) \"parts\", where this is understood in a very broad sense that includes spatial parts, temporal parts, \"conceptual\" parts, members of groups, and so forth.  <code>(#$parts WHOLE PART)</code> means that <code>PART</code> is in some sense a part of <code>WHOLE</code>.  Note that <code>PART</code> need not be a <i>proper</i> part of <code>WHOLE</code>: #$parts is a #$ReflexiveBinaryPredicate. (For representing proper parthood - see #$properParts.) Important specializations of #$parts include the predicates #$physicalParts, #$subEvents, #$timeSlices, #$subInformation, and #$groupMembers.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac56).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac56,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac56,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac56,25834).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac56,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac56,[]).
x_comment(x_Unity,"An instance of #$ZeroDimensionalUnitOfMeasure (q.v.) that takes one or two numbers or other #$NumericIntervals (q.v.) as argument(s) and returns the continuous numeric-interval that spans between and includes those arguments.  Given one argument, #$Unity behaves like an identity function.\n<p>\nMore precisely: for any numeric-interval <code>NUM</code>, <code>(#$Unity NUM)</code> = <code>NUM</code>; and for any two numeric-intervals <code>LO</code> and <code>HI</code> such that <code>(#$greaterThanOrEqualTo HI LO)</code>, <code>(#$Unity LO HI)</code> is the numeric-interval that ranges from the least point-value (see #$Number-General) subsumed by (see #$quantitySubsumes) <code>LO</code> to the greatest point-value subsumed by <code>HI</code>.\n<p>\nIn practice, <code>LO</code> and <code>HI</code> will typically be two distinct #$RealNumbers, and <code>(#$Unity LO HI)</code> will be the continuous #$ProperIntervalOnNumberLine that ranges from <code>LO</code> to <code>HI</code>.  For example, (#$Unity 1 10) is the interval from 1 to 10 inclusive.  But note that <code>LO</code> and/or <code>HI</code> might themselves be proper intervals: (#$Unity (#$Unity 7 9) 11) is thus the interval from 7 to 11 inclusive.\n<p>\n#$Unity is classified as a #$UnitOfMeasure mainly in order to simplify the application of quasi-mathematical operations (e.g. #$PerFn, #$UnitProductFn) to units-of-measure generally.  For example, #$TimesPerDay is defined as the result of \"dividing\" (via #$PerFn) #$Unity by #$DaysDuration.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac57).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac57,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac57,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac57,25835).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac57,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac57,[]).
x_comment(x_transitiveViaArgInverse,"A #$MetaPredicate used for stating that a given predicate behaves transitively, in a specified argument-place, with respect to the inverse of a given binary predicate.  <code>(#$transitiveViaArgInverse PRED BINPRED N)</code> means that the <code>N</code>th argument position of <code>PRED</code> is \"transitive\" with respect to the inverse of <code>BINPRED</code>.  That is, if <code>(PRED</code> ... <code>ARGN</code> ...) and <code>(BINPRED ARGN-PRIME ARGN)</code> hold, then so does <code>(PRED</code> ... <code>ARGN-PRIME</code> ...).  For example,(#$transitiveViaArgInverse #$relationAllExists #$genls 2) holds; thus from (#$relationAllExists #$anatomicalParts #$Mammal #$Head-AnimalBodyPart) and (#$genls #$Horse #$Mammal) it follows that (#$relationAllExists #$anatomicalParts #$Horse #$Head-AnimalBodyPart).  See also #$transitiveViaArg.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac58).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac58,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac58,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac58,25842).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac58,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac58,[]).
x_comment('x_temporallySubsumedBy-TypeType',"A #$TemporalPredicate and a specialization of #$temporallyIntersects-TypeType (q.v.) that is a type-level inverse correlate of #$temporallySubsumes (q.v.).  This predicate is used to state that things of one given type are always temporally subsumed by things of another given type.  (#$temporallySubsumedBy-TypeType SHORTTYPE LONGTYPE) means that for every instance SHORTINST of SHORTTYPE, there is an instance LONGINST of LONGTYPE such that (#$temporallySubsumes LONGINST SHORTINST).\n<p>\nFor example, (#$temporallySubsumedBy-TypeType #$AIDS #$HIVPositive) means that every case of having AIDS is temporally subsumed by a case of being HIV+.\n<p>\nSee also #$temporallySubsumes-TypeType; and note that (#$temporallySubsumes-TypeType LONGTYPE SHORTTYPE) is neither necessary nor sufficient for (#$temporallySubsumedBy-TypeType SHORTTYPE LONGTYPE).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac59).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac59,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac59,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
