x_comment(x_DistributingMetaKnowledgePredicate,"A subcollection of #$MetaKnowledgePredicate (q.v.).  Each instance of #$DistributingMetaKnowledgePredicate is a meta-knowledge predicate that can be used to make meta-assertions (i.e it can take a #$CycLAssertion as an argument) and is such that, when applied to an EL-level assertion that polycanonicalizes (i.e. gets transformed by the Cyc canonicalizer into multiple HL-level assertions), it is automatically \"distributed\" over each of the multiple assertions resulting from the polycanonicalization.\n<p>\nLet METAPRED be an instance of #$DistributingMetaKnowledgePredicate. Suppose that one asserts <code>(METAPRED ASSERTION FOO)</code> and that <code>ASSERTION</code> polycanonicalizes into <code>ASSERTION1</code> and <code>ASSERTION2</code>.  Then <code>(METAPRED ASSERTION1 FOO)</code> and <code>(METAPRED ASSERTION2 FOO)</code> will both be asserted to the Knowledge Base automatically.\n<p>\nFor example, if I assert this at the EL-level:\n<pre>\n  (#$comment\n    (#$and (#$isa #$Muffet #$Dog) (#$likesAsFriend #$Muffet #$Patches-Cat))\n    \"Muffet digs Patches.\"),\n</pre>\n<p>\nboth of these will automatically be asserted a the HL-level:\n<p>\n    <code>(#$comment (#$isa #$Muffet #$Dog) \"Muffet digs Patches.\")</code>\n<p>\n    <code>(#$comment (#$likesAsFriend #$Muffet #$Patches-Cat) \"Muffet digs Patches.\")</code> .\n<p>\nSee also the shared note #$MetaAssertionsForPolycanonicalizingAssertions.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac5b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac5b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac5b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac5b,25849).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac5b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac5b,[]).
x_comment('x_interArgIsa1-2',"A ternary instance of #$InterArgIsaPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$interArgIsa1-2 RELN INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL DEPENDENT-ARG-COL)</code> means that, when an instance of <code>INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL</code> appears as the first argument to <code>RELN</code>, a necessary condition for semantic well-formedness is that the second argument must be an instance of <code>DEPENDENT-ARG-COL</code>.  That is, if <code>INST</code> is an instance of <code>INDEPENDENT-ARG-COL</code>, then <code>(RELN INST ARG2 ... ARGN)</code> is semantically well-formed only if <code>ARG2</code> is an instance of <code>DEPENDENT-ARG-COL</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$interArgIsa1-2 #$performedBy #$PurposefulAction #$IntelligentAgent) means that, if <code>ACT</code> is a purposeful action, then (#$performedBy <code>ACT AGENT</code>) is semantically well-formed only if <code>AGENT</code> is an intelligent agent.  For an explanation of semantic well-formedness, see  #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its direct specializations.  See #$interArgIsa for a similar but more general predicate that is quintary, allowing the user to specify the positions of the independent and dependent arguments.\n<p>\nUse #$interArgCondIsa1-2 to conclude that arg2 is an instance of <code>DEPENDENT-ARG-COL</code> instead of having that as a constraint.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac5c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac5c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac5c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac5c,25867).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac5c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac5c,[]).
x_comment(x_relationInstanceExistsMany,"A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain relationship with respect to a given thing and a given collection.  (#$relationInstanceExistsMany BINPRED THING COL) means that there are many instances INST(i) of COL such that (BINPRED THING INST(i)) holds.  It is equivalent to (#$relationInstanceExistsRange BINPRED THING COL #$Many-Quant), and thus to the more complex form (for which it can in effect be used as shorthand):\n<p>\n(#$thereExistRange #$Many-Quant ?INST\n(#$and\n(#$isa ?INST COL2\n(BINPRED THING ?INST))) .\n<p>\nFor example, `(#$relationInstanceExistsMany #$anatomicalParts #$Patches-Cat #$Hair-Strand)' means that Patches has many hairs.  See also #$relationInstanceExistsMin, #$relationInstanceExistsMax, #$relationInstanceExistsCount, and #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac5d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac5d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac5d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac5d,25872).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac5d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac5d,[]).
x_comment(x_relationExistsMaxInstance,"A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain numerical relation with respect to a given collection and a given thing.  (#$relationExistsMaxInstance BINPRED COL THING N) means that there exists at most N instances (INST(1), ..., INST(N)) of COL that bear the relation PRED to THING such that (PRED INST(i) THING) holds (for 1 <= i <= N).  This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:\n<p>\n(#$thereExistAtMost N ?INST\n(#$and\n(#$isa ?INST COL)\n(BINPRED ?INST THING)))) .\n<p>\nFor example, `(#$relationExistsMaxInstance #$physicalParts #$Hand MyLeftThumb 1)' means that my left thumb is part of at most one hand.  Cf. #$relationInstanceExistsMax, and see the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac5e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac5e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac5e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac5e,25880).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac5e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac5e,[]).
x_comment(x_movieActors,"A binary predicate that relates a given #$Movie-CW to each agent who acted in that movie.  <code>(#$movieActors MOVIE ACTOR)</code> means that <code>ACTOR</code> played a role in <code>MOVIE</code>.  For example, <code>(#$movieActors #$CitizenKane-TheMovie #$OrsonWelles)</code> holds.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac5f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac5f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac5f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac5f,25888).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac5f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac5f,[]).
x_comment(x_Rate,"A specialization of #$PhysicalQuantity and #$RatioQuantity (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Rate is a multi-dimensional quantity made up of some quantity (e.g. distance traveled or money spent), taken with respect to -- i.e. divided by (see #$QuotientFn) -- a quantity of time (see #$Time-Quantity).\n<p>\nSpecializations of #$Rate include #$Speed (e.g. 55 mph), #$Frequency (e.g. 55 kHz), and #$MonetaryRate (e.g. 55 cents per minute).\n<p>\nSee also #$UnitOfRate, the collection of functions used to measure rates (e.g. #$DollarsPerYear, #$MetersPerSecond).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac60).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac60,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac60,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac60,25889).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac60,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac60,[]).
x_comment(x_relationAllExists,"A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain relation to two collections. <code>(#$relationAllExists BINPRED COL1 COL2)</code> means that for any instance <code>INST1</code> of <code>COL1</code>, there exists some instance <code>INST2</code> of <code>COL2</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST1 INST2)</code> holds.  It is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$implies\n    (#$isa ?INST1 COL1)\n    (#$thereExists ?INST2\n      (#$and\n        (#$isa ?INST2 COL2)\n        (BINPRED ?INST1 ?INST2)))).\n</pre>\n<p>\nFor example, <code>(#$relationAllExists #$temporalBoundsContain #$CalendarWeek #$Wednesday)</code> means that every calendar week contains a Wednesday.  See also #$relationExistsAll and #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac61).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac61,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac61,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac61,25894).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac61,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac61,[]).
x_comment(x_fromLocation,"An #$ActorSlot that is a specialization of #$preActors (q.v.).  This predicate is used to indicate the starting point of a particular movement from one place to another.  <code>(#$fromLocation MOVE LOC)</code> means that <code>LOC</code> is where some salient moving-object (see #$objectMoving) in the #$Translocation <code>MOVE</code> is found at the beginning of <code>MOVE</code> and is where it begins this motion.  This moving-object might or might not be #$Stationary at <code>LOC</code> at the beginning of <code>MOVE</code>.  If <code>MOVE</code> is a single-pathway translation (see #$Translation-SingleTrajectory), then each of its salient moving-objects is found at <code>LOC</code> when <code>MOVE</code> starts.  If <code>MOVE</code> has multiple movers and multiple pathways (see #$Translation-MultiTrajectory), then at least some of the moving-objects can be found at <code>LOC</code> when <code>MOVE</code> starts.  If <code>MOVE</code> is a flow (such as a river flowing, a wind blowing, a tornado, a typhoon, some clouds moving, or air filling your lungs when you take a breath; see #$FluidFlow-Translation), then at least some portion of the fluid moving-object can be found at <code>LOC</code> when <code>MOVE</code> starts.  See also #$motionPathway-Complete and #$pathConnects.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac62).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac62,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac62,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac62,25906).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac62,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac62,[]).
x_comment(x_negationInverse,"A #$MetaPredicate for stating that each of two binary predicates is a logical \"contrary\" of the other's inverse. <code>(#$negationInverse BINPRED1 BINPRED2)</code> means that if <code>BINPRED1</code> holds between a pair <code><THING1, THING2></code>, then <code>BINPRED2</code> does <i>not</i> hold between the inverse pair <code><THING2, THING1></code> (and vice versa).  In other words, <code>(#$negationInverse BINPRED1 BINPRED2)</code> is equivalent to <code>(#$not (#$and (BINPRED1 ARG1 ARG2) (BINPRED2 ARG2 ARG1)))</code>.\n<p>\nNote that any #$AsymmetricBinaryPredicate is related to itself by #$negationInverse.  For example, (#$negationInverse #$subordinates #$subordinates) holds, since if one person is a subordinate of another, the latter person cannot at the same time also be a subordinate of the former.  See also #$negationPreds and #$genlInverse.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac63).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac63,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac63,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac63,25907).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac63,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac63,[]).
x_comment(x_MonetaryValue,"A specialization of #$ScalarInterval. Each instance of this collection is a value by means of which payments, wealth, capital, assets, etc., are measured.  Each instance of this collection is thus an abstract object, not to be confused with the physical objects used as legal tender. For those, see #$Currency, though it is worth noting that each coin, bill, etc. that is an instance of #$Currency, is typically _stably associated with_ some particular instance of #$MonetaryValue.\n<p>\nNor is #$MonetaryValue to be confused with the many and varied social practices (such as a backed claim for a certain amount of physical wealth payable by the government on demand), which create money qua social institution and underpin all financial transactions. For that, see #$Money-DevisedPractice.\n<p>\nSince instances of #$MonetaryValue are not pure numbers but quantities of some particular currency, they are denoted by means of the collection #$UnitOfMoney. Each instance of this collection is a function which (like many Cyc functions which are instances of #$UnitOfMeasure) either takes one argument and returns a fixed amount of money, e.g. (#$Dollar-Australia 4) or takes two arguments and returns a range, e.g. (#$Shilling-Uganda 2 2000). Since currencies may appreciate or depreciate in value over time, and this information is sometimes relevant, it is possible to index instances of #$UnitOfMoney temporally by means of further functions. For example, ((#$USDollarFn 1997) 2) denotes the monetary value of 2 US dollars in the year 1997. See also #$MoneyTransfer, #$monetaryValueOfThing, #$cost, #$basicPrice.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac64).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac64,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac64,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac64,25910).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac64,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac64,[]).
x_comment(x_relationAllExistsCount,"A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate that is a specialization of both #$relationAllExistsMin and #$relationAllExistsMax (qq.v.).  <code>(#$relationAllExistsCount BINPRED COL1 COL2 N)</code> means that for any instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL1</code> there are exactly <code>N</code> instances <code>INST1</code>, ..., <code>INSTn</code> of <code>COL2</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST INSTi)</code> holds (for 1 <code><= i <= N)</code>. It is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:\n<pre>\n  (#$implies\n    (#$isa ?INST COL1)\n    (#$thereExistExactly N ?INST-I\n      (#$and\n        (#$isa ?INST-I COL2)\n        (BINPRED ?INST ?INST-I))))\n</pre>\n<p>\n(wherein the variable <code>?INST</code> is interpreted as if it were bound by an initial universal quantifier).\n<p>\nFor example, <code>(#$relationAllExistsCount #$anatomicalParts #$Dog #$Leg 4)</code> means that all dogs have four legs.\n<p>\nCf. #$relationExistsCountAll, and see the #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac65).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac65,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac65,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac65,25915).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac65,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac65,[]).
x_comment(x_Relation,"A specialization of #$MathematicalObject and the collection of all relations.  Each instance of #$Relation is a relation that can hold among one or more things, depending on whether the relation is unary, binary, ternary, or whatever (see #$arity and #$relationalArity).  A unary relation (such as #$unknownSentence) is a sort of degenerate case that holds of  certain individual things (in this case, all sentences that are unknown to Cyc).  A binary relation (such as #$likesAsFriend) relates one thing to another (in this case, it relates one sentient animal to another just in case the first likes the second).  A ternary relation relates certain triples of things.  And so on.  There are also relations of no particular fixed arity; see #$VariableArityRelation.\n<p>\nNames of relations can be used to construct sentences and other formulas.  More precisely, CycL terms that denote #$Relations can appear in the \"0th\" argument (or \"arg0\") position of a #$CycLFormula (q.v.), i.e. as the term immediately following the formula's opening parenthesis.\n<p>\nAn important subcollection of #$Relation is #$TruthFunction (q.v.), whose instances are intimately related to truth-values, as reflected in the fact that the CycL expressions that denote truth-functions can appear in the arg0 position of a #$CycLSentence; and a sentence (if quantificationally closed; see #$CycLClosedSentence), will generally be either true or false (with respect to a given context or interpretation).  The major subcollections of #$TruthFunction are #$Predicate, #$LogicalConnective, and #$Quantifier (qq.v.).\n<p>\nAnother important subcollection of #$Relation is #$Function-Denotational (q.v.), the collection of all functions.  A CycL term that denotes a function can appear in the arg0 position of a #$CycLNonAtomicTerm (q.v.).\n<p>\nSee also #$relationExtension and #$relationHoldsAmong.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac66).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac66,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac66,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac66,25930).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac66,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac66,[]).
x_comment(x_IntelligentAgent,"A specialization of #$Agent-Generic and #$InformationStore (qq.v.).  An agent is an #$IntelligentAgent if and only if it is capable of knowing and acting, and capable of employing its knowledge in its actions.  An intelligent agent typically #$knowsAbout certain things, and its #$beliefs concerning those things influences its actions.  As with agents generally, an intelligent agent might either be a single individual, such as a person, or a group consisting of two or more individual agents, such as a business or government organization.  Specializations of #$IntelligentAgent include #$SocialBeing and #$MultiIndividualAgent-Intelligent.\n<p>\nNote that non-human #$Vertebrates that are sentient and somewhat intelligent, such as (arguably) cats and horses, can be considered intelligent agents in some contexts.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac67).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac67,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac67,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac67,25939).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac67,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac67,[]).
x_comment(x_arg4Genl,"A binary #$ArgGenlPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$arg4Genl RELN COL)</code>  means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the fourth argument to <code>RELN</code> must be a subcollection of <code>COL</code>.  That is,  <code>(RELN ARG1 ARG2 ARG3 ARG4 ...)</code> is semantically well-formed only if  <code>(#$genls ARG4 COL)</code> holds.  For an explanation of semantic well-formedness, see  #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  See #$argGenl for a  similar but more flexible ternary predicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac68).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac68,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac68,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac68,25942).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac68,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac68,[]).
x_comment(x_eMailAddressText,"<code>(#$eMailAddressText OBJ ADDR)</code> means that an e-mail address of <code>OBJ</code> is <code>ADDR</code>.  This predicate can be used to associate multiple e-mail addresses with one instance of #$PartiallyIntangible.  See also the more specializaed predicate, #$preferredEMailAddressText, which associates a unique e-mail address with an instance of #$PartiallyIntangible.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac69).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac69,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac69,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac69,25943).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac69,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac69,[]).
x_comment(x_FunctionCompositionFn,"A binary #$FunctionDenotingFunction (q.v.) that in effect combines two functions into one.  (#$FunctionCompositionFn FUNC1 FUNC2)  is the function COMP (if there is such) that is the composition of FUNC1 and FUNC2.  That is, for any element D #$inDomainOf FUNC1, the value of COMP applied to D is the same as the result obtained by applying FUNC1 to D, and then applying FUNC2 to that result.  Symbolically, (COMP D) = (FUNC2 (FUNC1 D)).\n<p>\nFor example, (#$FunctionCompositionFn #$MotherFn #$FatherFn) is a function that takes an animal to its maternal grandfather, since A is the maternal grandfather of B if and only if A is the father of the mother of B.\n<p>\nNote that in the general description above, the domain of COMP is identical to the domain of FUNC1, and the range of COMP is a (proper or otherwise) subset of the range of FUNC2. (Strictly speaking, the range of COMP is the set of elements of the range of FUNC2 that are the values for some element or other of the range of FUNC1 taken as argument for FUNC2.)  This entails that COMP and FUNC1 must have the same #$arity (q.v.), and that the arity of FUNC2 must be 1.  (#$FunctionCompositionFn FUNC1 FUNC2) is defined (cf. #$undefined) whenever the range of FUNC1 is a (proper or otherwise) subset of the domain of FUNC2 (see  #$DomainOfFunctionFn and #$RangeOfFunctionFn).\n<p>\nSee #$compositionOfFunctions for the predicate corresponding to this function.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac6a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac6a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac6a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac6a,25944).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac6a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac6a,[]).
x_comment(x_identificationStrings,"A #$BinaryPredicate that relates instances of #$Thing to instances of #$IDString.  <code>(#$identificationStrings THING STRING)</code> means that <code>STRING</code> is used somehow to identify <code>THING</code>. Its many notable specializations include #$constantID, #$taxIDNumber and #$computerIP. <code>STRING</code> may be a unique identifier for <code>THING</code> or it may be possible that more than one thing is identified by <code>STRING</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac6b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac6b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac6b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac6b,25951).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac6b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac6b,[]).
x_comment(x_ModeratelyToHighlyFn,"An instance of #$GenericValueFunction (q.v). #$ModeratelyFn takes a collection-type COLTYPE that is instance of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder and returns the instance COL of COLTYPE that is the collection of individuals that are regarded as <i>moderate-to-high</i> in the conventional ordering of COLTYPE.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$ModeratelyToHighlyFn #$RegionTypeByHilliness) is the collection of all geographical regions that are moderately to highly hilly: those that are moderate-to-high in the conventional ordering of regions by hilliness.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac6c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac6c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac6c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac6c,25952).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac6c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac6c,[]).
x_comment(x_startsAfterEndingOf,"A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate (q.v.) that relates any pair of temporal things such that the first starts after the  second ends.  <code>(#$startsAfterEndingOf AFTER BEFORE)</code> means that the time  point (see #$TimePoint) at which <code>AFTER</code> first starts to exist or occur  is temporally later than (see #$after) the time point at which <code>BEFORE</code> finally ceases to exist or occur.  Thus it is equivalent to the form <code>(#$after (#$StartFn AFTER) (#$EndFn BEFORE))</code>.  That is, the  #$startingPoint of <code>AFTER</code> is later than the #$endingPoint of <code>BEFORE</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac6d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac6d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac6d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac6d,25955).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac6d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac6d,[]).
x_comment(x_nextSubSituationTypeToStart,"<code>(#$nextSubSituationTypeToStart SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then <code>SIT1</code> is the next situation to start after <code>SIT2</code>.\n<p>\nNote that <code>(#$nextSubSituationTypeToStart SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac6e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac6e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac6e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac6e,25956).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac6e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac6e,[]).
x_comment(x_InformationBearingThing,"A collection of spatially-localized  individuals, including various actions and events as well as physical  objects.  Each instance of #$InformationBearingThing (or \"IBT\") is an  item that contains information (for an agent who knows how to interpret it).   Examples: a copy of the novel <i>Moby Dick</i>; a signal buoy; a photograph; an  elevator sign in Braille; a map; a US dollar bill; a resume; a musical score;  copies of the #$CycProgram.\n<p>\nFor representations of the propositional content  of information bearing things, see #$PropositionalInformationThing; but note  that not all IBTs have a propositional content (cf. #$ArtObject).  An important specialization of #$InformationBearingThing is #$InformationBearingObject,  which comprises all of those IBTs that are also physical objects (i.e.  #$PartiallyTangibles).  Though often a subtle task in particular contexts,  it is important to distinguish the various specializations of  #$InformationBearingThing from those of #$AspatialInformationStore (whose  instances are the chunks of information instantiated in particular IBTs;  see #$instantiationOfAIT) and from those of #$ConceptualWork (whose  instances are the conceptual or artistic creations that are instantiated  in particular IBTs; see #$instantiationOfWork).\n<p>\nFor instance, #$TextString  is a specialization of #$AspatialInformationStore, #$TextualMaterial is  a specialization of #$InformationBearingThing, and #$TextualPCW is a  specialization of #$ConceptualWork; to conflate any of these with  another would be to make a \"category error\".  Also note that events  in which information is transferred (see #$InformationTransferEvent) are  not considered instances of #$InformationBearingThing.  Rather, such  transfer events have as one of their participants (see #$actors) some  instance of #$InformationBearingThing; though in cases where IBTs are  themselves events this will require distinguishing very finely between  the event that encodes the information and the event that is the information transfer.  See also the sense-modality-based specializations, #$SoundInformationBearingThing and #$VisualInformationBearingThing.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac6f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac6f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac6f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac6f,25959).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac6f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac6f,[]).
x_comment(x_relationAllExistsRange,"A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain numerical relationship with respect to two collections.  <code>(#$relationAllExistsRange BINPRED COL1 COL2 RANGE)</code> means that for any instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL1</code> there are between the minimum and maximum of <code>RANGE</code> (see #$NonNegativeIntegerRange) many instances <code>INST</code>(i) of <code>COL2</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST INST</code>(i)) holds.  This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:\n<pre>\n(#$implies\n  (#$isa ?INST COL1)\n  (#$thereExistRange RANGE ?INST-I\n    (#$and\n      (#$isa ?INST-I COL2)\n      (PRED ?INST ?INST-I)))) .\n</pre>\nFor example, '(#$relationAllExistsRange #$anatomicalParts #$Dog #$Hair-Strand #$Many-Quant)' means that dogs have many hairs.  Cf. #$relationExistsRangeAll, and see #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac70).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac70,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac70,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac70,25964).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac70,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac70,[]).
x_comment(x_relationExistsAll,"A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain relation to two collections.  <code>(#$relationExistsAll BINPRED COL1 COL2)</code> means that for any instance <code>INST2</code> of <code>COL2</code>, there exists some instance <code>INST1</code> of <code>COL1</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST1 INST2)</code> holds.  It is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form\n<pre>\n  (#$implies\n    (#$isa ?INST2 COL2)\n    (#$thereExists ?INST1\n      (#$and\n        (#$isa ?INST1 COL1)\n        (BINPRED ?INST1 ?INST2)))).\n</pre>\nFor example, <code>(#$relationExistsAll #$adjacentTo #$Harbor #$PortCity)</code> means that for every port city there exists a harbor to which the city is adjacent.  See also #$relationAllExists and #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac71).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac71,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac71,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac71,25974).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac71,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac71,[]).
x_comment(x_subEventTypes,"A #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate that is a specialization of #$subSituationTypes (q.v.) and a type-level correlate of #$subEvents (q.v.).  This predicate is used to state that events of one given type always have events of another given type as subevents. <code>(#$subEventTypes SUPERTYPE SUBTYPE)</code> means that for every instance <code>SUPERINST</code> of <code>SUPERTYPE</code>, there is an instance <code>SUBINST</code> of <code>SUBTYPE</code> such that <code>(#$subEvents SUPERINST SUBINST)</code>.  For example, (#$subEventTypes #$Pedaling #$LiftingALeg) means that every pedaling involves a leg-lifting.  See also #$properSubEventTypes and #$superEventTypes; and note that <code>(#$subEventTypes SUPERTYPE SUBTYPE)</code> is neither necessary nor sufficient for <code>(#$superEventTypes SUBTYPE SUPERTYPE)</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac72).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac72,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac72,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac72,25984).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac72,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac72,[]).
x_comment(x_temporallyIntersects,"A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate that relates things whose temporal extentions overlap, i.e. temporal things that exist concurrently for at least part of their lifetimes.  More precisely: <code>(#$temporallyIntersects OBJ1 OBJ2)</code> means that there is some #$TimeInterval (possibly as small as a #$TimePoint) temporally subsumed by (see #$temporallySubsumes) both <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac73).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac73,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac73,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac73,25985).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac73,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac73,[]).
x_comment(x_anatomicalParts,"<code>(#$anatomicalParts ORGANISM PART)</code> means that the #$OrganismPart <code>PART</code> is an anatomical part of the #$Organism-Whole <code>ORGANISM</code>.  One should use the predicate #$physicalParts (q.v.), rather than #$anatomicalParts, to relate an instance of #$OrganismPart (for example, a hand) to other #$OrganismParts (for example, the fingers of the hand) that are part of it.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac74).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac74,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac74,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac74,25986).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac74,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac74,[]).
x_comment(x_relationalArity,"A #$MetaRelation predicate that is used to state how many things a given #$FixedArityRelation (q.v.) holds among or relates to one another.  <code>(#$relationalArity RELATION N)</code> means that <code>RELATION</code> only holds among the members of <code>N</code>-item sequences or #$Lists (see #$relationHoldsAmong and #$relationExtension).  If <code>RELATION</code> is any fixed-arity relation other than a function its #$relationalArity is the same as its ordinary #$arity (q.v.).  But if <code>RELATION</code> is a #$FixedArityFunction its #$relationalArity is <i>1 greater</i> than its #$arity.  This reflects the fact that, qua relation, a function <code>FUNC</code> of #$arity <code>N</code> actually holds among <code>N</code><i>+1</i> things: <code>FUNC</code>'s <code>N</code> arguments together with the corresponding value <code>FUNC</code> has for those arguments (see #$valueOfFunctionForArgs).  For example, the function #$MotherFn has #$arity 1 while the corresponding predicate (see #$functionCorrespondingPredicate) #$biologicalMother has #$arity 2; but both of these relations have #$relationalArity 2.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac75).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac75,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac75,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac75,25987).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac75,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac75,[]).
x_comment(x_FirstOrderCollectionPredicate,"A #$PredicateType and a specialization of #$SetOrCollectionPredicate (q.v.).  First-order collection predicates are used to make statements about first-order collections or types, primarily in order to say something about their instances.  They generally provide a simpler way of stating what could be stated (albeit less tersely) without the use of collection-predicates (viz. by referring more directly to the instances themselves).  More precisely: each instance of #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate is a predicate at least one of whose relata is always a #$FirstOrderCollection (q.v.).  Such predicates are typically used to make ground-atomic sentences (see #$CycLClosedAtomicSentence) that are in principle equivalent to certain quantified sentences that involve no set-or-collection-predicates.\n<p>\nFor example, #$argIsaForType is a \"type-level\" correlate of #$argIsa that enables one to place a particular argument-type constraint on every instance of a #$RelationshipType in one fell-swoop.  Thus, a ground-atomic sentence <code>(#$argIsaForType RELNTYPE ARGNUM COL)</code> is equivalent to the quantified sentence\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$forAll ?RELN\n    (#$implies\n      (#$isa ?RELN RELNTYPE)\n      (#$argIsa ?RELN ARGNUM COL))) .\n</pre>") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac76).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac76,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac76,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac76,25988).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac76,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac76,[]).
x_comment(x_immediatelyAfterInSituationType,"<code>(#$immediatelyAfterInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then <code>SIT1</code> starts immediately after <code>SIT2</code> ends.\n<p>\nNote that <code>(#$immediatelyAfterInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac77).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac77,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
