x_comment(x_DurationFn,"A unary #$ScalarDenotingFunction that maps a given #$TemporalThing to the time-quantity that measures the (possibly discontinuous) time-interval during which the thing existed or transpired.  (#$DurationFn TEMP) is the #$Time-Quantity that the predicate #$duration (q.v.) relates to TEMP.  For example, (#$DurationFn #$TheTwentiethCenturyCE) is equal to (#$YearsDuration 100).\n<p>\n#$DurationFn is the function that corresponds to the predicate #$duration. (Cf. #$timeSpan.)") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac78).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac78,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac78,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac78,26001).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac78,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac78,[]).
x_comment(x_divisorTypeForQuantityType,"A binary predicate that relates a #$DerivedMeasurableQuantityType (q.v.) (that is a specialization of #$RatioQuantity) to a #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType (q.v.) from which the former's instances are derived via division.  <code>(#$divisorTypeForQuantityType DERIVED-TYPE PRIMITIVE-TYPE)</code> means that each instance of <code>DERIVED-TYPE</code> is the result of dividing (see #$QuotientFn ) some quantity by an instance of <code>PRIMITIVE-TYPE</code>.\n\nFor example, <code>(#$divisorTypeForQuantityType #$Speed #$Time-Quantity)</code> means that each speed is the result of dividing some quantity by a time-quantity.  Thus the speed (#$MilesPerHour 35) is equal to the result of dividing (#$Mile 35) by (#$HoursDuration 1).\n\nSee also #$multiplierTypeForQuantityType.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac79).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac79,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac79,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac79,26004).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac79,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac79,[]).
x_comment(x_assertionMt,"A predicate which relates Cyc assertions to the microtheories in which they reside. (#$assertionMt ASSERTION MT) states that the #$CycLAssertion ASSERTION resides in the #$Microtheory MT.\n<p>\nNote that the first argument to this predicate is #$CycLAssertion, rather than #$ELSentence-Assertible. This renders the predicate #$functionalInArgs 2, since any given #$CycLAssertion only appears in one microtheory (though another assertion with an identical #$ELSentence-Assertible may well appear in a different microtheory - cf. #$assertionSentence).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac7a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac7a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac7a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac7a,26009).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac7a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac7a,[]).
x_comment(x_publicationTimeOfMTAssertions,"(#$publicationTimeOfMTAssertions MICROTHEORY TIME-INTERVAL) means that the assertions in the #$Microtheory MICROTHEORY were published (or at least publically made) during the #$TimeInterval TIME-INTERVAL.  For example, the 'publication time' of the #$CIAWorldFactbook1997Mt is the year 1997.\n<p>\nNote that the time interval in question is not necessarily that referenced in assertions within MICROTHEORY; for example, the #$CIAWorldFactbook1997Mt contains assertions about the 1996 gross domestic product of various countries.\n<p>\nNor should it be interpreted as the time-interval at which the assertions within MICROTHEORY are true; for an expression of that idea, see #$mtTime (and #$MtTimeFn).\n<p>\nNor is it the date on which assertions in MICROTHEORY were entered in Cyc. Again, the #$CIAWorldFactbook1997Mt is a good example, since it was created in 1998.  Generally, when the source of the assertions in a microtheory is a publication, the 'publication time' will be the date of publication.  (Specifically, the date of first publication, in the case of unrevised subsequent editions.  However, if the material in the source is made publically available prior to the formal publication date, the 'publication time' will be the earlier date.)  Note that it will only make sense to assign an 'publication time' to a microtheory when it represents a set of propositions that were all asserted (or hypothesized, or...etc.) by some specific source or agent -- many microtheories do not fall into this category.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac7b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac7b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac7b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac7b,26012).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac7b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac7b,[]).
x_comment(x_ModeratelyToVeryHighlyFn,"An instance of #$GenericValueFunction (q.v). #$ModeratelyToVeryHighlyFn takes a collection-type COLTYPE that is instance of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder and returns the instance COL of COLTYPE that is the collection of individuals that are regarded as <i>moderate-to-very-high</i> in the conventional ordering of COLTYPE.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$ModeratelyToVeryHighlyFn #$RegionTypeByHilliness) is the collection of all geographical regions that are moderately to very-highly hilly: those that are moderate-to-very-high in the conventional ordering of regions by hilliness.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac7c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac7c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac7c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac7c,26019).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac7c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac7c,[]).
x_comment(x_relationExistsRangeAll,"A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain numerical relationship with respect to two collections.  <code>(#$relationExistsRangeAll BINPRED COL1 COL2 RANGE)</code> means that for any instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL2</code> there are between the minimum and maximum of <code>RANGE</code> (see #$NonNegativeIntegerRange) many instances <code>INST(i)</code> of <code>COL1</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST(i) INST)</code> holds.  This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$implies\n     (#$isa ?INST COL2)\n     (#$thereExistRange RANGE ?INST-I\n        (#$and\n           (#$isa ?INST-I COL1)\n           (PRED ?INST-I ?INST)))).\n</pre>\n<p>\nFor example, '(#$relationExistsRangeAll #$anatomicalParts #$Mammal #$Foot-AnimalBodyPart #$AFew-Quant)' means every mammal has a few feet.  Cf. #$relationAllExistsRange, and see #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac7d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac7d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac7d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac7d,26022).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac7d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac7d,[]).
x_comment(x_Informing,"A #$FirstOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$Informing is a #$CommunicationAct-Single in which the #$senderOfInfo conveys some piece of <i>propositional</i> information--i.e. information of the sort that can be true or false.\n<p>\nThe information conveyed in an #$Informing is presented by the sender as being true, though it may in fact be false.  Thus, passing on misinformation counts as #$Informing, as do acts of #$Lying.\n<p>\nNote that the propositional nature of the information conveyed in an #$Informing may be implicit; for example, if Alice asks 'What's your favorite color?' and Bob simply responds 'Red', Bob has <i>implicitly</i> conveyed the proposition <i>Bob's favorite color is red</i>.\n<p>\nSpecializations of #$Informing include #$Reporting, #$Testifying, and #$AnnouncingSomething.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac7e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac7e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac7e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac7e,26034).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac7e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac7e,[]).
x_comment(x_transporter,"This predicate relates objects to the transportation events in which they facilitate movement.  <code>(#$transporter MOVE OBJ)</code> means that #$SolidTangibleThing <code>OBJ</code> enables or facilitates the conveyance of the #$transportees in the #$TransportationEvent <code>MOVE</code>.  <code>OBJ</code> is an #$objectMoving in <code>MOVE</code> that moves along with the #$transportees.  <code>OBJ</code> will generally hold, support, contain, pull, or push the #$transportees throughout the <code>MOVE</code> #$Event.  <code>OBJ</code> may or may not be the #$providerOfMotiveForce in the event <code>MOVE</code>.  If <code>OBJ</code> stays with each #$primaryObjectMoving from the #$fromLocation to the #$toLocation, moving along the same trajectory, then it is also a #$primaryObjectMoving.\n<p>\nIf <code>OBJ</code> facilitates the motion of the #$primaryObjectMoving but does not itself engage in translational motion it is merely the conveyor of the action, and the role #$conveyor-Stationary should be asserted.  If it is unclear whether the conveyor is stationary or not, the role #$conveyor-Generic is used.  Specializations of the role #$transporter should be used when possible: although automobiles are #$transporters in many events, they should normally have the more specific role designation of #$vehicle because they are also instances of #$TransportationDevice-Vehicle.   When a car is being towed by a tow truck, the car is just a #$transporter of any of its contents while the tow truck is the #$vehicle of that event.  An additional role designation for some #$transporters -- those which are #$PhysicalDevices -- is #$deviceUsed; e.g., the use of crutches in hobbling or ice skates in skating.  See the #$comment on #$TransportationEvent.  Note that an organism may be a #$transportees in a #$Bicycle riding or #$Skating event as well as being the #$providerOfMotiveForce in such cases.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac7f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac7f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac7f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac7f,26041).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac7f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac7f,[]).
x_comment(x_startsAfterEndOfInSituationType,"<code>(#$startsAfterEndOfInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then <code>SIT1</code> starts after <code>SIT2</code> ends.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$startsAfterEndOfInSituationType #$GoingOutToEat #$Paying #$HavingAMeal) means that when going out to eat, people pay after they finish eating.\n<p>\nNote that <code>(#$startsAfterEndOfInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac80).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac80,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac80,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac80,26044).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac80,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac80,[]).
x_comment(x_genlFuncs,"A binary #$MetaRelation and a specialization of #$generalizations.  #$genlFuncs can be used to relate an instance of #$CollectionDenotingFunction to other instances that are correlatively more general with respect to the collections they return as values for the same arguments.  <code>(#$genlFuncs SPECFN GENLFN)</code> means that the collection that is the value of <code>GENLFN</code> for any given sequence of arguments is a generalization (see #$genls) of the value (if any) of <code>SPECFN</code> for that same sequence of arguments.  That is, whenever <code>SPECFN</code> is defined for (i.e. associates a value with) a sequence <<code>X1, ..., Xn</code>>, the following holds:\n<p>\n    <code>(#$genls (SPECFN X1 ... Xn) (GENLFN X1 ... Xn))</code> .\n<p>\nFor example, the sentence <code>(#$genlFuncs #$MobFn #$GroupFn)</code> entails that (e.g.) the collection (#$MobFn #$Person) is a specialization of (#$GroupFn #$Person).\n<p>\nCf. #$genlFunctions.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac81).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac81,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac81,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac81,26049).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac81,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac81,[]).
x_comment(x_Integer,"A specialization of #$RationalNumber.  Each instance of #$Integer is a whole number with no fractional remainder.  An integer may be positive (e.g. 42), zero, or negative (e.g. -42).\n<p>\nNote that, in the KB browser, a fixed- or floating-point number representation such as `42.00' should not be assumed to refer to the integer 42. For it might only appear to correspond to that integer within the tolerance of its representation, and might in fact be an approximation to a non-integer number such as 42.0003.\n<p>\nAlso note that, unlike #$Integer, #$SubLInteger (q.v.) is a collection of integer-denoting expressions in the SubL programming language rather than a collection of integers themselves. Still, (#$genls #$SubLInteger #$Integer) holds, but only under a special interpretation of #$genls forced by the fact that #$SubLInteger is a #$quotedCollection (q.v).)") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac82).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac82,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac82,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac82,26056).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac82,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac82,[]).
x_comment(x_middleName,"A specialization of #$nameString (q.v.).  <code>(#$middleName PERSON STRING)</code> means that <code>PERSON</code> is known by <code>STRING</code> as his or her middle name.   Persons usually  have no more than one middle name.  <code>STRING</code> is typically a #$HumanGivenNameString, though in some cultures certain people have #$HumanFamilyNameStrings for their middle names.  See also #$middleNameInitial.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac83).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac83,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac83,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac83,26061).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac83,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac83,[]).
x_comment(x_quantityRange,"A binary predicate that relates a #$QuantityType to a collection to which anything possessing a #$Quantity of that type belongs. <code>(#$quantityRange QUANTTYPE COL)</code> means that anything that is related to any instance of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> via some #$QuantitySlot (q.v.) is itself an instance of <code>COL</code>.  That is, for any thing <code>THING</code>, any instance <code>QUANT</code> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code>, and any quantity-slot <code>SLOT</code>, <code>(SLOT THING QUANT)</code> entails <code>(#$isa THING COL)</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, <code>(#$quantityRange #$Viscosity #$PartiallyTangible)</code> means that only tangible things have viscosities.  But note that it does <i>not</i> imply that all tangible things have viscosities.\n<p>\nWhen making an assertion of the form <code>(#$quantityRange QUANTTYPE COL)</code> to the Cyc Knowledge Base it is normally advisable to select the most restricted collection possible (among those that are reified in the Cyc ontology) for <code>COL</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac84).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac84,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac84,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac84,26062).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac84,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac84,[]).
x_comment(x_objectActedOn,"The predicate #$objectActedOn is used to relate an event to any object significantly affected in that event.  The entity or entities in question must exist before the event, but may be either destroyed in the event (see the more specific predicate #$inputsDestroyed), or merely affected by it (for example, see the more specific predicates #$damages and #$objectOfStateChange). <code>(#$objectActedOn EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> is altered or affected in <code>EVENT</code>, and the change that <code>OBJECT</code> undergoes is central or focal to understanding <code>EVENT</code>.  Thus, scissors are <i>not</i> an #$objectActedOn in a #$HairCuttingEvent.  The focal change in a haircut is hair getting shorter; thus, hair is a legitimate #$objectActedOn in a #$HairCuttingEvent.  The almost microscopic dulling that scissors undergo in a single haircut is a comparatively insignificant change with respect to a single haircut, considered as a #$HairCuttingEvent.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac85).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac85,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac85,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac85,26067).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac85,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac85,[]).
x_comment(x_computerMACAddress,"A predicate relating a instance of #$Computer with the instance of #$MACAddress associated with one of its network cards. <code>(#$computerMACAddress COMP MAC-ADDR)</code> means that <code>COMP</code> is a computer with a network card installed whose MAC address is <code>MAC-ADDR</code>. Note that a single computer can have more than one network card and each <code>MAC-ADDR</code> address is unique to a network card, hence one computer can be associated with more than one MAC address.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac86).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac86,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac86,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac86,26068).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac86,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac86,[]).
x_comment(x_fieldsOfActivity,"A specialization of #$fieldsOfCompetence.  <code>(#$fieldsOfActivity PERSON FIELD)</code>  means that <code>PERSON</code> is or was active in the #$FieldOfStudy <code>FIELD</code>.  Usually,  <code>PERSON</code> has made, is making, or intends to make some contribution to  <code>FIELD</code>.  E.g. (#$fieldsOfActivity #$Thales #$MilesianSchool) holds.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac87).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac87,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac87,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac87,26069).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac87,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac87,[]).
x_comment(x_NumericComparisonPredicate,"A specialization of #$ComparisonPredicate.  The collection of binary predicates used to compare instances of #$ScalarInterval (q.v.) with respect to their  numeric properties or magnitudes.  Instances include #$greaterThan,  #$greaterThanOrEqualTo, and #$numericallyEquals.\n<p>\nThe numeric-comparison predicates are effectively the mathematical relations of greater-than et al., generalized to  hold between scalars of all sorts, including quantitative intervals as  well as point values (see #$ScalarPointValue).  Generally speaking, a given numeric comparison predicate holds between two scalars only  if they are both instances of the same #$TotallyOrderedScalarIntervalType (q.v.).\n<p>\nSee also #$quantitiesShareScale and #$colinearQuantities.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac88).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac88,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac88,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac88,26070).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac88,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac88,[]).
x_comment(x_conservativeViaArg,"A #$MetaPredicate that is a generalization of #$transitiveViaArg (q.v.) in that it can take <i>any</i> binary predicate (transitive or not) as its second argument.  <code>(#$conservativeViaArg PRED RELN N)</code> means that if both of these hold:\n<pre>\n  (PRED ... ARGN ... )\n\n  (RELN ARGN NEW-ARGN),\n</pre>\n<p>\nthen so does\n<pre>\n  (PRED ... NEW-ARGN ...)\n</pre>\n(where the argument-sequence <code>... NEW-ARGN ...</code> is just like <code>... ARGN ...</code> except for having <code>NEW-ARGN</code> in place of <code>ARGN)</code>.\n<p>\nNote that if <code>RELN</code> is a #$TransitiveBinaryPredicate, the more specialized #$transitiveViaArg can be used instead.  See also #$conservativeViaArgInverse.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac89).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac89,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac89,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac89,26075).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac89,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac89,[]).
x_comment('x_temporallySubsumedBy-InsType',"A binary #$TemporalPredicate that relates a #$TemporalThing to a type of #$TimeInterval or #$QualitativeTimeOfDay (q.v.) an instance of which the thing is temporally subsumed by.  (#$temporallySubsumedBy-InsType TEMP INTERVAL-TYPE) means that some instance of INTERVAL-TYPE #$temporallySubsumes (q.v.) TEMP.\n<p>\nFor example, '(#$temporallySubsumedBy-InsType FredsBirth #$Wednesday)' means that Fred was born on a Wednesday.\n<p>\nSee also #$temporallySubsumes-InsType and #$temporallySubsumedBy-TypeType.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac8a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac8a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac8a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac8a,26089).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac8a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac8a,[]).
x_comment(x_RelativeGenericValueFn,"A ternary #$ScalarDenotingFunction that can be used for specifying \"relativized generic quantities\" of various types and amounts.  #$RelativeGenericValueFn takes as arguments a #$QuantitySlot, a #$Collection, and a #$GenericValuePredicate, and returns a #$GenericQuantity (qq.v.).  <code>(#$RelativeGenericValueFn SLOT COL GENVALPRED)</code> is the <code>GENVALPRED</code> amount (e.g. <i>very-low</i>, <i>low</i>, <i>medium</i>, <i>high</i>) of generic quantity of the type to which <code>SLOT</code>'s second argument-place is constrained (via #$arg2Isa), relativized to instances of <code>COL</code> as occupants of it's first argument-place.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$RelativeGenericValueFn #$heightOfObject #$BasketballPlayer #$mediumAmountOf) is the range (see #$ScalarProperInterval) of #$Distance that is roughly a medium height for a basketball player.\n<p>\nSee also the collection #$GenericValueFunction.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac8b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac8b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac8b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac8b,26094).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac8b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac8b,[]).
x_comment(x_domainAssumptions,"A #$MicrotheoryPredicate that relates a given #$Microtheory to an #$ELSentence-Assertible that expresses one of its 'domain assumptions'. (#$domainAssumptions MT PROP) means that the microtheory MT has the proposition PROP as a domain assumption, which means that all assertions explicitly made in MT assume that PROP is true.  For example, (#$domainAssumptions #$ChristianTrinityMt (#$equals #$JesusChrist #$GodTheSon)).\n<p>\nAnother way of thinking of this is that the various `content' assertions of MT only apply to objects that satisfy all its #$domainAssumptions (i.e. that the quantifiers #$forAll and #$thereExists will be restricted in MT in this way).  So if it's true in MT that:\n<p>\n(#$forAll ?x Q)\n<p>\nand if we lift this axiom to another microtheory (MT2) that does not make the domain assumption P, then the axiom becomes:\n<p>\n(#$forAll ?x (#$implies P Q))\n<p>\n(Or, of course ((#$forAll ?x (#$implies (#$and P1 P2 P3...) Q)), where there is more than one relevant domain assumption).  See also #$mtAdditionConstraint.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac8c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac8c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac8c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac8c,26099).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac8c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac8c,[]).
x_comment(x_toLocation,"The predicate #$toLocation is used to indicate the ending point of a particular movement.  <code>(#$toLocation MOVE LOC)</code> means that <code>LOC</code> is where the #$objectMoving in <code>MOVE</code> (a #$Movement-TranslationEvent) is found when the event <code>MOVE</code> ends; the final location of the #$objectMoving in that event is <code>LOC</code>.  The #$objectMoving may or may not be #$Stationary at <code>LOC</code>.  If <code>MOVE</code> is a single-pathway translation (see #$Translation-SingleTrajectory), then every #$objectMoving in it is found at <code>LOC</code> when <code>MOVE</code> ends.  If <code>MOVE</code> has multiple movers and multiple pathways (see #$Translation-MultiTrajectory), then at least some of the #$objectMoving(s) can be found at <code>LOC</code> at the end of <code>MOVE</code>.  If <code>MOVE</code> is a fluid flow (see #$FluidFlow-Translation), then at least some portion of the fluid #$objectMoving can be found at location <code>LOC</code> at the end of <code>MOVE</code>.  See also #$motionPathway-Complete, #$pathConnects.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac8d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac8d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac8d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac8d,26110).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac8d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac8d,[]).
x_comment(x_hasWorkers,"The predicate #$hasWorkers relates two instances of #$Agent-Generic.  <code>(#$hasWorkers AGENT1 AGENT2)</code> means that <code>AGENT2</code> regularly works for <code>AGENT1</code>.  This predicate can be used to relate a business to its employees, a master craftsman to his apprentices, or an organization to its subcontractors.  Note that <code>(#$hasWorkers AGENT1 AGENT2)</code> does not necessarily imply that <code>AGENT2</code> is an employee of <code>AGENT1</code>, since <code>AGENT2</code> may be working voluntarily for <code>AGENT1</code>.  Note also that assertions using #$hasWorkers should usually be temporally qualified, since when one agent regularly works for another, that agent usually does so only for a portion of  his, her, or its life, rather than for all of it.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac8e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac8e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac8e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac8e,26111).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac8e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac8e,[]).
x_comment(x_isaInstanceOfType,"(#$isaInstanceOfType INS COL-TYPE) means that the #$Thing INS is an instance of a #$Collection which is itself an instance of COL-TYPE, a #$CollectionType. This predicate can be used when referring to instances, say INS1, of instances, say COL1 with (isa INS1 COL1) of a given type, TYPEA, such that (isa COL1 TYPEA) when the narrower collection COL that would stand in a #$typeGenls relationship with TYPEA and such that (#$isa INS1 COLA) and (#$genls COL1 COLA) is not reified.\n<p>\nExample of use: #$BacteriumIdentificationTestType is the collection of collections of tests conducted to identify bacteria. All these tests are such that objects on which tests are conducted are instances of #$Bacterium. This would be expressed as follows:\n<p>\n(#$implies\n(#$and\n(#$isaInstanceOfType TEST #$BacteriumIdentificationTestType)\n(#$objectTested TEST OBJ))\n(#$isa OBJ #$Bacterium)).\n<p>\nTo specify that during each of these tests there is at least one bacterium tested, one would assert the following GAF:\n<p>\n(#$relationAllExists #$objectTested\n(#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToFn #$TrueOrFalseTest\n#$isaInstanceOfType\n#$BacteriumIdentificationTestType)\n#$Bacterium).\n<p>\nThis predicate may be used when one wants to avoid reification of the union of all instances of a collection type, but note that extensive use of this predicate may amount to a good justification for reification of such a collection.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac8f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac8f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac8f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac8f,26112).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac8f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac8f,[]).
x_comment(x_payer,"<code>(#$payer MONEY-TRANSFER PAYER)</code> means that <code>MONEY-TRANSFER</code> is an instance of #$MoneyTransfer in which <code>PAYER</code> is an agent which provides (some of) the money.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac90).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac90,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac90,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac90,26131).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac90,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac90,[]).
x_comment(x_succeedingIntervalType,"A binary #$TemporalPredicate and specialization of #$followingIntervalTypes (q.v.) that relates types of #$TimeIntervals.  (#$succeedingIntervalType PRECEDING-TYPE SUCCEEDING-TYPE) means that every instance of PRECEDING-TYPE is immediately followed by (see #$contiguousAfter) some instance of SUCCEEDING-TYPE, and every instance of SUCCEEDING-TYPE is immediately preceded by some instance of PRECEDING-TYPE.\n<p>\nFor example,  (#$succeedingIntervalType #$Saturday #$Sunday) holds.\n<p>\nSee also #$followingIntervalType-Cumulated.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac91).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac91,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac91,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac91,26132).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac91,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac91,[]).
x_comment(x_RuleMacroPredicate,"A specialization of both #$MacroRelation and #$SetOrCollectionPredicate (qq.v.) whose instances are used to form shorthand expressions for more complex sentences.  Each instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate is correlated -- via an #$expansion (q.v.) assertion -- with a certain template for a class of (possibly quantified) CycL sentences.  A closed sentence (see #$CycLClosedAtomicSentence) built from (i.e. whose \"0<sup>th</sup>\" argument is) a given rule-macro-predicate is treated by the Cyc system as fully semantically equivalent to the (usually more complex) corresponding sentence fitting the template.  For example, #$genls is a rule-macro-predicate whose expansion assertion is\n<pre>\n  (#$expansion #$genls\n    (#$implies\n      (#$isa ?OBJ :ARG1)\n      (#$isa ?OBJ :ARG2))\n</pre>\nThus <code>(#$genls #$Poodle #$Dog)</code> is shorthand for the rule\n<pre>\n  (#$implies\n    (#$isa ?OBJ #$Poodle)\n    (#$isa ?OBJ #$Dog))\n</pre>\nNote that virtually any #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate (q.v.) could in principle be made into a #$RuleMacroPredicate and given a similar treatment.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac92).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac92,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac92,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac92,26137).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac92,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac92,[]).
x_comment(x_SkolemFunction,"A specialization of #$IndeterminateTermDenotingFunction and #$ReifiableFunction (qq.v.).  #$SkolemFunction is a collection of system-generated functions that implement existential quantification in Cyc.  Whenever someone asserts to the Knowledge Base a sentence that contains '#$thereExists' (in an arg0 position), Cyc automatically creates a new instance of #$SkolemFunction and rewrites the assertion using that Skolem function.\n<p>\nFor example, suppose we want Cyc to know that every animal has a birth date.   So we assert the following sentence:\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$implies\n    (#$isa ?X #$Animal)\n    (#$thereExists ?Y\n      (#$birthDate ?X ?Y))) .\n</pre>\n<p>\nIn response, Cyc automatically reifies a new unary instance of #$SkolemFunction (call it 'BirthDateFn') that takes any given animal to its date of birth, and then rewrites our rule using BirthDateFn instead of #$thereExists, as\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$implies\n    (#$and\n      (#$isa ?X #$Animal)\n      (#$termOfUnit ?Y (BirthDateFn ?X)))\n    (#$birthDate ?X ?Y)) .\n</pre>\n<p>\nNote that actual Cyc-generated Skolem function names currently (02/04) consist of the prefix 'SKF-' follow by a numeral.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac93).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac93,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac93,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac93,26151).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac93,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac93,[]).
x_comment(x_paAppropriateQuestion,"A predicate that states a given question is appropriate for\nthe #$CycProductAdvisor to ask.  (paAppropriateQuestion ID FORMULA)\nmeans that the question specified by the string id ID is\nappropriate to ask at the current point in the user's\ninteraction with the Cyc Product Advisor.  FORMULA is\na template for a formula that should be asserted\nwhen the user answers the question.  This allows\nOEers working on the Product Advisor to 'special-case' certain questions.\n<p>\nIt is assumed that ID refers to a question which has already\nbeen written for such a situation, and the Cyc-PA code will be able\nto retrieve the question given its id.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac94).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac94,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac94,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac94,26173).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac94,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac94,[]).
x_comment(x_vehicle,"A binary predicate that relates instances of #$TransportationEvent to instances of #$TransportationDevice-Vehicle.  <code>(#$vehicle EVENT VEHICLE)</code> means that <code>VEHICLE</code> is a #$TransportationDevice-Vehicle which is both the #$providerOfMotiveForce and the #$transporter in EVENT.  If an object is a #$TransportationDevice-Vehicle and plays the role of #$transporter in some moving event, then it generally  will play the role of #$vehicle in that event.  Examples: a car plays the role of #$vehicle in driving.  Note, however, that a bicycle does not play the role of #$vehicle in bike riding since it is not a provider of motive force.  A borderline non-example is someone sitting in a car while it's being pulled by a tow truck; the car is not playing the role of #$vehicle in that event (because it is not the provider of motive force - the tow truck is).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac95).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac95,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac95,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac95,26185).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac95,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac95,[]).
x_comment(x_startsDuringInSituationType,"<code>(#$startsDuringInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> means that in any instance <code>SIT</code> of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Situation), if instances <code>SIT1</code> and <code>SIT2</code> of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations, then <code>SIT1</code> starts during <code>SIT2</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$startsDuringInSituationType #$UnwindingOfDNAHelix #$Transcription-DNAToRNA #$GeneExpression) means that when DNA unwinds, DNA to RNA transcription starts during gene expression.\n<p>\nNote that <code>(#$startsDuringInSituationType SIT-TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2)</code> does not imply that instances of <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> or <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> occur as subsituations of every instance of <code>SIT-TYPE</code>, since <code>SUBSIT-TYPE1</code> and <code>SUBSIT-TYPE2</code> may be merely candidate subsituation types of <code>SIT-TYPE</code> (see #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac96).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac96,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac96,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac96,26186).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac96,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac96,[]).
x_comment(x_IntervalOnNumberLine,"A specialization of #$NumericInterval (q.v.).  Each instance of #$IntervalOnNumberLine is either a particular #$RealNumber or a proper interval on the real number line (see #$ProperIntervalOnNumberLine).  For example, the phrase \"numbers greater than zero and less than or equal to 10\" describes such an interval.\n<p>\nNote that an interval on the real number line need not be continuous.  E.g. the phrase \"even integers between Pi and the square root of 1000\" describes a noncontinuous instance of #$IntervalOnNumberLine.  Cf. the specialization #$ContinuousIntervalOnNumberLine.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac97).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac97,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac97,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac97,26191).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac97,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac97,[]).
x_comment(x_colinearQuantities,"A #$CoEquivalenceBinaryPredicate and a specialization of #$comparableScalars (q.v.) that holds only between #$Quantity[ies] (q.v.).  <code>(#$colinearQuantities QUANT1 QUANT2)</code> means that there exists some linear ordering <code>SCALE</code> (see #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType) such that <code>QUANT1</code> and <code>QUANT2</code> are distinct instances of <code>SCALE</code>.  This implies both (i) <code>(#$quantitiesShareScale QUANT1 QUANT2 SCALE)</code> holds and (ii) either <code>(#$followingValue QUANT1 QUANT2)</code> or <code>(#$followingValue QUANT2 QUANT1)</code> holds.\n<p>\n#$followingValue is in turn a specialization of #$colinearQuantities.  The latter can be thought of as a version of former that is unconcerned with the order of its arguments.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac98).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac98,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac98,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac98,26194).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac98,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac98,[]).
x_comment(x_partTypes,"A #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate that is a type-level correlate of #$parts (q.v.).  This predicate relates types of individuals to the types of parts they have.  <code>(#$partTypes WHOLETYPE PARTTYPE)</code> means that for every instance <code>WHOLEINST</code> of <code>WHOLETYPE</code> there exists at least one instance <code>PARTINST</code> of <code>PARTTYPE</code> such that (#$parts <code>WHOLEINST PARTINST)</code> holds.  Important specializations of this predicate include #$physicalPartTypes, #$subEventTypes and #$properSubEventTypes (qq.v.).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac99).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac99,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac99,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac99,26197).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac99,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac99,[]).
x_comment(x_PrimitiveMeasurableQuantityType,"A collection of collections and a specialization of both #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType and #$MeasurableScalarIntervalType (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$PrimitiveMeasurableQuantityType is a totally-ordered collection of instances of #$OneDimensionalQuantity (q.v.).  Examples include #$Mass, #$Distance, #$Temperature, and #$ElectricalCharge.\n<p>\nCf. #$DerivedMeasurableQuantityType.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac9a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac9a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac9a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac9a,26198).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac9a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac9a,[]).
x_comment(x_arg3Genl,"A binary #$ArgGenlPredicate (q.v.).  <code>(#$arg3Genl RELN COL)</code>  means that semantic well-formedness requires that anything given as the third argument to <code>RELN</code> must be a subcollection of <code>COL</code>.  That is, <code>(RELN ARG1 ARG2 ARG3 ...)</code> is semantically well-formed only if <code>(#$genls ARG3 COL)</code> holds.  For an  explanation of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible and its specializations.  See #$argGenl for a similar but more flexible ternary  predicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac9b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac9b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac9b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac9b,26201).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac9b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac9b,[]).
x_comment(x_TotallyOrderedQuantityType,"A collection of collections and a specialization of both #$TotallyOrderedScalarIntervalType and #$QuantityType (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType is a totally-ordered collection of #$Quantity[ies], and thus an appropriate argument for a #$GenericValueFunction (q.v.), such as #$LowAmountFn or #$MediumToHighAmountFn.\n<p>\nInstances of #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType include #$Toxicity, #$DiplomaticSupport, and #$LevelOfDifficulty.  Specializations include #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType and #$MeasurableQuantityType.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac9c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aac9c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aac9c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aac9c,26202).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aac9c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aac9c,[]).
x_comment(x_AntiSymmetricBinaryPredicate,"A #$BinaryPredicateTypeByLogicalFeature and thus a specialization of #$BinaryPredicate.  A binary predicate <code>PRED</code> is an #$AntiSymmetricBinaryPredicate if and only if it has the following two properties.  (i) For any <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code> such that both <code>(PRED THING1 THING2)</code> and <code>(PRED THING2 THING1)</code> hold, <code>THING1</code>  = <code>THING2</code>.  (ii) <code>PRED</code>'s two argument-places must be \"co-satisfiable\" with respect to their (single-argument) type constraints (see #$cosatisfiableInArgs and #$NoteOnArgumentCosatisfiabilityRequirement).\n<p>\nInstances of #$AntiSymmetricBinaryPredicate include #$greaterThanOrEqualTo, #$parts, and #$subEvents.  See also the specialization #$AsymmetricBinaryPredicate; and cf. #$SymmetricBinaryPredicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aac9d).
