xc_kb_names(a65b16526aaca6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aaca6,[]).
x_comment(x_MonetaryRate,"A specialization of #$Rate and #$MoneyRatio (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$MonetaryRate is an amount of money (see #$MonetaryValue) taken with respect to an amount of time (see #$Time-Quantity).  Instances of #$MonetaryRate include rates at which money is earned, spent, transferred, lost, and invested.\n<p>\nAn example of #$MonetaryRate is (#$DollarsPerHour 10), the rate of ten dollars per hour.\n<p>\nSee also #$UnitOfMonetaryRate.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaca7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaca7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaca7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aaca7,26245).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aaca7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aaca7,[]).
x_comment(x_FunctionalPredicate,"A specialization of #$Predicate whose instances are those predicates that are \"functional\" in at least one argument-place (see #$functionalInArgs and #$singleEntryFormatInArgs).  That is, given any sequence of legal arguments placed in such a predicate's other argument positions, there is at most one argument such that, when it is placed in the predicate's functional position, the resulting formula is true.  More precisely: Suppose PRED is an N-ary instance of #$FunctionalPredicate that is functional in its Kth place.  Given any (N-1)-tuple <O(1),.., O(K-1), O(K+1),.., O(N)> consisting of things that are, respectively, legal arguments for (i.e. satisfy all argument-constraints on) the N-1 argument-places in PRED other than  its Kth-place, there is at most one thing -- call it O(K) -- such that  (PRED O(1)..O(N)) is true.  For example, #$biologicalMother is functional in its second argument-place, since every animal has one and only one biological mother.\n<p>\nThe one exception to the above is when the entry-format for PRED's functional argument-place is #$intervalEntryFormatInArgs (q.v.) instead of #$singleEntryFormatInArgs.  In that case there might be multiple instances INT-1..INT-M of either #$ScalarInterval or #$TimeInterval that, when put in this argument-place, each yield a true formula; but only if all of the INT-i overlap one another and thus have a common sub-interval; see #$quantityIntersects and #$temporallyIntersects.  Cf. #$StrictlyFunctionalPredicate.\n<p>\nNote that it might be the case that, for some ways of fixing PRED's other arguments, there will be _nothing_ that would yield a true formula when put into PRED's functional argument-place; for example, while a spatial thing has at most one length (see #$lengthOfObject), if it is (say) a sphere it has none.\n<p>\nA binary #$FunctionalPredicate that is functional in its second argument is an instance of #$FunctionalSlot (q.v.).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaca8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaca8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaca8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aaca8,26250).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aaca8,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aaca8,[]).
x_comment(x_objectOfPossessionTransfer,"#$objectOfPossessionTransfer relates an event (more specifically, an instance of #$ChangeInUserRights) to an object (an instance of #$SomethingExisting) the user rights to which are transferred during that event.  <code>(#$objectOfPossessionTransfer EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that in <code>EVENT</code>, all or some rights to use <code>OBJECT</code> are transferred from one agent (the #$fromPossessor) to another (the #$toPossessor).  Since <code>EVENT</code> is an instance of #$ChangeInUserRights, it could be (among other things) a buying, renting, lending, or repossessing.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaca9).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaca9,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaca9,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aaca9,26257).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aaca9,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aaca9,[]).
x_comment(x_SymmetricBinaryPredicate,"A specialization of both #$BinaryPredicate and #$CommutativeRelation (qq.v.).  A binary predicate <code>PRED</code> is an instance of #$SymmetricBinaryPredicate if and only if (i) for any things <code>X</code> and <code>Y</code>, if <code>(PRED X Y)</code> then <code>(PRED Y X)</code> and (ii) the single-argument argument type constraints (see #$argIsa and #$argGenls) on <code>PRED</code>'s two argument-places are co-satisfiable (see #$cosatisfiableInArgs and #$NoteOnArgumentCosatisfiabilityRequirement).  Note that if <code>PRED</code> satisfies condition (i), then it also satisfies condition (ii) as long as <code>(PRED A B)</code> holds for some <code>A</code> and <code>B</code>.\n<p>\nInstances of #$SymmetricBinaryPredicate include #$siblings, #$teammates, #$connectedTo, and #$bordersOn.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacaa).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacaa,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacaa,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacaa,26258).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacaa,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacaa,[]).
x_comment(x_valueOfFunctionForArgs,"A ternary #$MetaRelation that relates a given function and a sequence of arguments on which it is defined to the value the function has for those arguments. <code>(#$valueOfFunctionForArgs FUNCTION ARGLIST VALUE)</code> means that <code>FUNCTION</code> maps <code>ARGLIST</code> to <code>VALUE</code>.  More precisely: when <code>FUNCTION</code> is applied to the (say) <code>N</code> items in <code>ARGLIST</code> (with the <code>i</code>-th item of <code>ARGLIST</code> serving as the <code>i</code>-th  argument to <code>FUNCTION</code>, for 1 <= <code>i</code> <= <code>N)</code>, it yields <code>VALUE</code>.\n<p>\nA necessary condition for the above is that <code>ARGLIST</code> is #$inDomainOf (q.v.) <code>FUNCTION</code> and <code>VALUE</code> is #$inRangeOf (q.v.) <code>FUNCTION</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacab).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacab,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacab,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacab,26261).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacab,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacab,[]).
x_comment(x_SetOrCollectionPredicate,"The #$PredicateType consisting of all predicates that (always) hold of #$SetOrCollections (q.v.).  Each instance of #$SetOrCollectionPredicate has at least one argument-place constrained to instances (see #$argIsa) of #$SetOrCollection or some specialization thereof.  Some set-or-collection predicates are used to make statements that are more or less directly about a given set-or-collection itself (e.g. #$cardinality relates a set-or-collection to its size); but most are used to make more general statements about some or all of the elements of a given set-or-collection (e.g. #$inhabitantTypes relates a geographical region to a collection of persons such that at least one person in the collection resides in that region).\n<p>\nSpecializations of #$SetOrCollectionPredicate include #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate and #$RuleMacroPredicate.\n<p>\nCf. #$ObjectPredicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacac).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacac,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacac,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacac,26264).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacac,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacac,[]).
x_comment(x_VeryHighlyFn,"An instance of #$GenericValueFunction (q.v). #$VeryHighlyFn takes a collection-type COLTYPE that is instance of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder and returns the instance COL of COLTYPE that is the collection of individuals that are regarded as <i>very-high</i> in the conventional ordering of COLTYPE.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$VeryHighlyFn #$RegionTypeByHilliness) is the collection of all geographical regions that are very highly hilly: those that are very-high in the conventional ordering of regions by hilliness.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacad).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacad,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacad,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacad,26269).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacad,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacad,[]).
x_comment(x_ScalarIntervalSlot,"A specialization of #$BinaryPropertyPredicate (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ScalarIntervalSlot is a binary predicate that is used to attribute a property to a thing, by relating it to a #$ScalarInterval (i.e. to a #$Quantity or #$NumericInterval of some sort).\n<p>\nExamples of #$ScalarIntervalSlot include #$lengthOfObject, #$levelOfMorale, and #$iQ.\n<p>\nSee also the specializations #$QuantitySlot and #$NumericIntervalSlot.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacae).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacae,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
