x_comment(x_ProductTypeByMarketCategory,"A collection of collections.  Each element of #$ProductTypeByMarketCategory is a collection of items that are commonly offered for sale in the same (type of) store, (type of) section of a store, or (type of) catalog.  A specialty store might be devoted to a single element of #$ProductTypeByMarketCategory.  The basis for a #$ProductTypeByMarketCategory includes both (1) different brands or variations of a certain kind of product (e.g., hosiery or hammers); and (2) products that are often used together (e.g., kitchen pans and cooking utensils).  Thus, members of a single market category collection need not have any intrinsic similarity; they may simply be things that are often used or purchased together.  For example, work gloves, a soft clothing item, are usually found with hardware or gardening tools.  Commonly, a single element of #$ProductTypeByMarketCategory will include elements of several instances of #$ProductType as members; e.g., many brief cases, suit cases, garment bags, trunks, etc., will all be marketed as instances of #$LuggageMarketCategory  Elements of #$ProductType are related to their #$ProductTypeByMarketCategory by #$insMarketedAs.\n<p>\nNote that #$Briefcase et al. are not specs of #$LuggageMarketCategory; instances of them are merely marketed as such.\n<p>\nExamples of #$ProductTypeByMarketCategory: #$BuildingMaterial, #$HealthAndBeautyMarketCategory, #$BooksAndMagazinesMarketCategory, #$ElectricalSupplyMarketCategory, #$AutoSoundMarketCategory, #$HouseholdFurnishingsMC, #$OfficeProductMarketCategory.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacb3).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacb3,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacb3,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacb3,26287).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacb3,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacb3,[]).
x_comment(x_genlFunctions,"A #$MetaRelation predicate that relates a given function to another function that \"agrees\" with the first but might be more general in the sense of being defined for a broader class of arguments (i.e. having a #$relationDomain that is a superset of that of the first function). <code>(#$genlFunctions SPECFN GENLFN)</code> means that for any sequence of arguments that <code>SPECFN</code> maps to some particular <code>VALUE</code>, <code>GENLFN</code> also maps that sequence to <code>VALUE</code>.  <code>GENLFN</code> might also be defined for certain argument-sequences for which  <code>SPECFN</code> is not.\n<p>\nFor example, since the integers are a subset of the real numbers, the square-function defined on the integers is related by #$genlFunctions to the square-function defined on the reals.\n<p>\nCf. #$genlFuncs for a different predicate not to be confused with this one.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacb4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacb4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacb4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacb4,26292).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacb4,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacb4,[]).
x_comment(x_SquaredFn,"A unary #$MathematicalFunctionOnScalars that is an extension of the arithmetical operation of squaring to measurable #$ScalarIntervals generally.  <code>(#$SquaredFn SCALAR)</code> is by definition equal to <code>SCALAR</code> multiplied by itself: <code>(#$TimesFn SCALAR SCALAR)</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$SquaredFn 9) is equal to 81 and (#$SquaredFn (#$Mile 3)) is equal to (#$SquareMile 9).\n<p>\nSee also #$SquaredFn's quasi-inverse, #$SquareRootFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacb5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacb5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacb5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacb5,26297).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacb5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacb5,[]).
x_comment(x_ModeratelyFn,"An instance of #$GenericValueFunction (q.v). #$ModeratelyFn takes a collection-type COLTYPE that is instance of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder and returns the instance COL of COLTYPE that is the collection of individuals that are regarded as <i>moderate</i> in the conventional ordering of COLTYPE.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$ModeratelyFn #$RegionTypeByHilliness) is the collection of all geographical regions that are moderately hilly: those that are moderate in the conventional ordering of regions by hilliness.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacb6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacb6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacb6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacb6,26302).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacb6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacb6,[]).
x_comment(x_startsAfterStartingOf,"<code>(#$startsAfterStartingOf LATER-START EARLIER-START)</code> means that <code>LATER-START</code> begins to exists or occur after <code>EARLIER-START</code> begins to exist or occur.  That is, the #$startingPoint of <code>LATER-START</code> is later than the #$startingPoint of <code>EARLIER-START</code> or, equivalently, <code>(#$after (#$StartFn LATER-START) (#$StartFn EARLIER-START))</code>.  This implies nothing about whether, or by how much, <code>LATER-START</code> and <code>EARLIER-START</code> overlap, except that they can't be fully #$cotemporal.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacb7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacb7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacb7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacb7,26305).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacb7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacb7,[]).
x_comment(x_providerOfMotiveForce,"<code>(#$providerOfMotiveForce MOVE OBJ)</code> means that the #$PartiallyTangible <code>OBJ</code> provides, through its expenditure of energy, a significant fraction of the physical force required to make the #$MovementEvent <code>MOVE</code> happen. For example, if <code>MOVE</code> is an instance of #$PedalingABicycle, <code>OBJ</code> will be the person who is operating the bike. Another example: if <code>MOVE</code> is a use of a belt sanding tool, then #$providerOfMotiveForce will relate <code>MOVE</code> to both the electric sander and the person operating the sander, since both the sander and the operator supply a significant fraction of the force required to make <code>MOVE</code> happen.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacb8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacb8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacb8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacb8,26306).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacb8,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacb8,[]).
x_comment(x_timeSlices,"A #$TemporalPartPredicate and a specialization of both #$temporallySubsumes and #$parts (qq.v.) that relates a temporal thing to any of its \"pure\" temporal parts.  (#$timeSlices TEMP SLICE) means that SLICE is a part of TEMP that is distinguished from TEMP purely in terms of its temporal extent, which is subsumed by the temporal extent of TEMP itself.  In other words, SLICE is \"everything there is to\" TEMP during the particular #$TimeInterval that SLICE occupies.  For example, today's noon hour has the interval from 12:00 noon to 12:30 p.m. today as one of its time-slices; the first act of a particular performance of \"Hamlet\" is a time-slice of that performance of the whole play.\n<p>\nThere is no general agreement on exactly what types of temporal things have time-slices.  Still, a few generalizations can be made.  All and only time-slices of #$TimeIntervals are themselves #$TimeIntervals.  Indeed, one time interval temporally subsumes another if and only if the latter is a time-slice of the former.  All and only time-slices of #$Situations are themselves #$Situations.  It is controversial whether any temporal things besides pure time intervals and temporal situations -- such as ordinary tangible objects -- have time-slices.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacb9).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacb9,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacb9,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacb9,26307).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacb9,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacb9,[]).
x_comment(x_relationExistsExists,"A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that is used to state that a given binary predicate holds between some two instances of two respective collections. <code>(#$relationExistsExists BINPRED COL1 COL2)</code> means that there is some instance <code>INST1</code> of <code>COL1</code> and some instance <code>INST2</code> of <code>COL2</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST1 INST2)</code> holds.  This is equivalent to (and so can be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:\n<pre>\n  (#$thereExists ?INST1\n    (#$thereExists ?INST2\n      (#$and\n        (#$isa ?INST1 COL1)\n        (#$isa ?INST2 COL2)\n        (BINPRED ?INST1 ?INST2)))).\n</pre>\n<p>\nFor example, <code>(#$relationExistsExists #$possesses #$Dog #$Bone-BodyPart)</code> means that some dog has a bone.  See the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacba).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacba,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacba,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacba,26310).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacba,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacba,[]).
x_comment(x_arityMin,"A #$MetaRelation for specifying the minimum number\nof arguments a given #$VariableArityRelation must take if the resulting formula is to be semantically well-formed.  <code>(#$arityMin RELN N)</code> means that semantic well-formedness requires that <code>RELN</code> take at least <code>N</code> arguments at a time.  More precisely: a formula <code>(RELN ARG<sub>1</sub> ... ARG<sub>M</sub>)</code> is semantically well-formed only if <code>M</code> is greater than or equal to <code>N</code>.  For example, the minimum-arity of any instance of #$UnitOfMeasure is 1.  Note that full semantic well-formedness requires obeying argument-type constraints (see #$ArgTypePredicate) as well as arity constraints. For a general explanation of semantic well-formedness, see #$CycLExpression-Assertible.  See also #$arityMax and #$arity.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacbb).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacbb,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacbb,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacbb,26321).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacbb,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacbb,[]).
x_comment(x_Date,"A specialization of #$ContinuousTimeInterval (q.v.).  A continuous time-interval is an instance of #$Date if and only if it has a standardly-defined location on particular calendar.  Here, having a \"standardly-defined location\" means that the interval is a standard unit of time (with respect to some calendar) whose start and end times can be specified in whole-number multiples of that and larger units.  Thus the midnight hour GMT on 1 January 2000 CE is a date; but neither the five-hour-long interval between midnight and 5:00 AM GMT nor the hour-long interval between 12:15 and 1:15 AM GMT on that same day are dates.\n<p>\nSpecializations of #$Date include #$CalendarMilliSecond, #$CalendarMinute, #$CalendarHour, #$PolarDay, #$CalendarWeekend, #$FiscalYear, and  #$CalendarMillennium.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacbc).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacbc,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacbc,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacbc,26323).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacbc,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacbc,[]).
x_comment(x_relationInstanceExists,"A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate holds between a specified thing and some instance of a given collection.  <code>(#$relationInstanceExists PRED THING COL)</code> means that there exists an instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL</code> such that <code>(PRED THING INST)</code> holds.  It is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:\n<p>\n<pre>\n     (#$thereExists ?INST\n          (#$and\n               (#$isa ?INST COL)\n               (PRED THING ?INST))) .\n</pre>\n<p>\nFor example, '(#$relationInstanceExists #$geographicalSubRegions #$Iran #$OilField)' means that there exists an oilfield in Iran.  Cf. #$relationExistsInstance, and see the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacbd).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacbd,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacbd,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacbd,26326).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacbd,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacbd,[]).
x_comment(x_integerBetween,"A ternary #$IntangibleObjectRelatingPredicate that holds among #$Integers only.  <code>(#$integerBetween LOW MED HIGH)</code> means that <code>HIGH</code> is #$greaterThanOrEqualTo <code>MED</code>, and <code>MED</code> is #$greaterThanOrEqualTo <code>LOW</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacbe).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacbe,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
