x_comment(x_hasBeliefSystems,"This predicate relates intelligent agents to belief-systems (such as religions, philosophical schools, political theories, etc.) that they accept. <code>(#$hasBeliefSystems AGENT SYSTEM)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> holds the #$BeliefSystem <code>SYSTEM</code>.  For example, (#$hasBeliefSystems BorisYeltsin #$RussianOrthodoxReligion) holds.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacd5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacd5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacd5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacd5,26431).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacd5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacd5,[]).
x_comment(x_diameterOfObject,"This predicate relates two- or three-dimensional circular- or spherical-shaped objects to their diameters, represented as #$Distances. <code>(#$diameterOfObject OBJECT DIAMETER)</code> means that he diameter of <code>OBJECT</code> is <code>DIAMETER</code>.  See also #$radius.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacd6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacd6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacd6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacd6,26432).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacd6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacd6,[]).
x_comment(x_DensityOfPopulation,"A #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType (q.v.) and a specialization of #$RatioQuantity (q.v.).  Quantities that are instances of #$DensityOfPopulation apply to (see #$quantityRange) #$GeographicalRegions, and measure -- typically in a generic way (e.g. via a #$GenericValueFunction) -- the number of human inhabitants per some (specified or unspecified) unit of area (e.g. #$SquareMile).\n<p>\nGeneric instances of #$DensityOfPopulation include #$UrbanPopulationDensity (defined to be equal to (#$HighToVeryHighAmountFn #$DensityOfPopulation)) and #$DesolateAreaPopulationDensity (defined to be equal to (#$VeryLowAmountFn #$DensityOfPopulation)).  A non-generic instance is\n<p>\n<pre>\n    (#$QuotientFn ((#$UnitOfCountFn #$HomoSapiens) 1000) (#$SquareMile 1)) ,\n</pre>\n<p>\nthe population density of <i>1000-people-per-square-mile</i>.\n<p>\nTo relate a given region to a #$DensityOfPopulation, use the predicate #$populationDensity.  See also #$populationOfRegion, #$populationGrowthRate, and #$populationRange.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacd7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacd7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacd7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacd7,26433).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacd7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacd7,[]).
x_comment(x_arg2Format,"An instance of #$ArgFormatPredicate (q.v.) used to place a particular #$Format (q.v.) constraint on the second (or \"arg2\") argument-place of a given predicate.  <code>(#$arg2Format PRED FORMAT)</code> means that <code>PRED</code>'s arg2 is constrained to <code>FORMAT</code>.  See the reified instances #$Format for\nfurther details.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacd8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacd8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacd8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacd8,26444).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacd8,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacd8,[]).
x_comment(x_GenericValueTypeFunction,"A specialization of #$CollectionDenotingFunction whose instances map event-like-ordered collection-types to certain instances of those collection-types.  Each instance <code>GVT-FUNC</code> of #$GenericValueTypeFunction corresponds to a certain generic, non-numerically-specified \"degree\" (e.g. <i>slightly, moderately, highly</i>) to which a property can be exemplified; when applied to a collection-type <code>COLTYPE</code> that is a #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder (q.v.), <code>GVT-FUNC</code> returns the instance <code>COL</code> of <code>COLTYPE</code> such that <code>COL</code>'s instances are those that exemplify the relevant property to the appropriate degree.\n<p>\nFor example, #$HighlyFn is a #$GenericValueTypeFunction, and #$RegionTypeByHilliness is an ordered collection-type that \"divides up\" the collection of geographical regions according to how hilly they are.  So (#$HighlyFn #$RegionTypeByHilliness) is the instance <code>HIGHLY-HILLY</code> of #$RegionTypeByHilliness that is the collection of all geographical regions that are <i>highly hilly</i> in relation to other geographical regions.  Each instance of <code>HIGHLY-HILLY</code> will have a higher degree of hilliness than the instances of any other collection (i.e. instance of #$RegionTypeByHilliness) lower in the same ordering.\n<p>\nSome other instances of #$GenericValueTypeFunction are #$SlightlyFn, #$ModeratelyFn, and #$ModeratelyToHighlyFn.\n<p>\nThe #$GenericValueTypeFunctions can be loosely thought of as type-level versions of the #$GenericValueFunctions (cf.).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacd9).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacd9,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacd9,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacd9,26446).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacd9,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacd9,[]).
x_comment(x_HighlyFn,"An instance of #$GenericValueFunction (q.v). #$HighlyFn takes a collection-type COLTYPE that is instance of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder and returns the instance COL of COLTYPE that is the collection of individuals that are regarded as <i>high</i> in the conventional ordering of COLTYPE.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$HighlyFn #$RegionTypeByHilliness) is the collection of all geographical regions that are highly hilly: those that are high in the conventional ordering of regions by hilliness.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacda).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacda,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacda,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacda,26453).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacda,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacda,[]).
x_comment(x_isa,"A #$TaxonomicSlot (q.v.) that relates things of any  kind to collections (see #$Collection) of which they are instances.   <code>(#$isa THING COL)</code> means that <code>THING</code> is an instance of the collection <code>COL</code>.   #$isa is by far the most commonly-occurring predicate in the Cyc Knowledge  Base, and is one of the relations most fundamental to the Cyc ontology. An important logical feature of #$isa is that it \"transfers through\"  #$genls (q.v.): <code>(#$isa THING COL)</code> and <code>(#$genls COL SUPERCOL)</code> jointly  imply <code>(#$isa THING SUPERCOL)</code>.  This logical feature is expressed in a  rule that -- along with various other rules that also sanction the  derivation of #$isa sentences -- has been asserted to the Knowledge Base.   As a result, only a small fraction of the #$isa based ground atomic  formulas (or \"GAF\"s; see #$CycLClosedAtomicSentence) the Cyc system  \"knows\" to be true have been manually asserted; the vast majority  have been derived, or are derivable, automatically by the Cyc inference  engine via the use of such rules.  See also #$elementOf.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacdb).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacdb,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacdb,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacdb,26456).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacdb,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacdb,[]).
x_comment(x_Language,"A specialization of #$CommunicationConvention. Each instance of this collection is a generative system of signs for transmitting information. These signs are normally conventional and arbitrary in nature, though the case of body language is a (borderline) exception to this.\n<p>\nThe reality of any given language consists in its being interpretable by some community (usually human -- cf. #$HumanLanguage). This is not quite true of #$ComputerLanguages, however such languages are only possible insofar as some kind of automation of the process of interpretation (by computers) has been effected. In the case of #$DeadLanguages, the community may not longer exist (although even if no-one can _speak_ such languages any longer, currently-interpretable texts often remain, cf., for example, #$AtticGreekDialect).\n<p>\nLanguages are most often spoken, but not always (cf. #$SignLanguage). It is also worth noting that not every system of signs is a language; a certain richness of possible expression is required. Thus #$MorseCode, for example, though a #$CommunicationConvention, is not an instance of #$Language. Finally, this collection is not intended to cover the special vocabulary and usages of a scientific, professional or other group, or a characteristic style of speech or writing (e.g. 'Shakespearean language').") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacdc).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacdc,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacdc,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacdc,26457).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacdc,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacdc,[]).
x_comment(x_agreeingAgents,"A #$CoexistingObjectsPredicate and specialization of #$normativelyBoundAgents that relates an #$Agreement (q.v.) to the agents who made or are making the agreement.  <code>(#$agreeingAgents AGREE PARTY)</code> means that <code>AGREE</code> has <code>PARTY</code> among its agreeing parties.  Thus, <code>PARTY</code> will be mentioned in the content of <code>AGREE</code>.\n<p>\nSpecializations of #$agreeingAgents include #$agreeingBuyer, #$agreeingSeller, #$policyHolder, #$insuringAgent, #$employedAgent, and #$employingAgent.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacdd).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacdd,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacdd,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacdd,26462).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacdd,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacdd,[]).
x_comment(x_BinaryPropertyPredicate,"The collection of #$BinaryPredicates that are used to attribute properties to things, by relating them either to (i) #$ScalarIntervals (especially #$Quantity[ies] or #$RealNumbers), (ii) property-like collections (i.e. collections of things that, intuitively, share an intrinsic property), or (iii) #$GenericValuePredicates (q.v.).\n<p>\nFor example, #$eyeColor is a #$BinaryPropertyPredicate that relates an #$Animal to a collection that is a #$Color, according to the color of the animal's eyes.  And #$thirstLevel is a binary property-predicate that relates an animal to a generic-value predicate (e.g. #$highAmountOf), according to how thirsty the animal is.\n<p>\nFor binary property-predicates that relates things to #$ScalarIntervals only, see the specialization #$ScalarIntervalSlot.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacde).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacde,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacde,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacde,26465).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacde,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacde,[]).
x_comment(x_playsRoleInActionTypeRegularly,"<code>(#$playsRoleInActionTypeRegularly THING SITTYPE ROLE)</code> means that the #$TemporalThing <code>THING</code> takes part in #$Situations of type <code>SITTYPE</code> in the role <code>ROLE</code> multiple times, and there is a fairly predictable temporal interval between such occurrences. If the frequency is known and/or relevant, the more specific predicate #$frequencyOfActionType can be used.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacdf).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacdf,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacdf,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacdf,26470).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacdf,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacdf,[]).
x_comment(x_NoAmountFn,"A #$GenericValueFunction (q.v.) that takes a given #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType to a #$GenericQuantity of that type.  In a given context, <code>(#$NoAmountFn QUANTTYPE)</code> is the properly interval-like quantity (and instance) of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> that is approximately <i>no-amount</i> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> with respect to that context (see #$ApproximatelyFn and #$ScalarProperInterval).\n<p>\nFor example, (#$NoAmountFn #$Elasticity) is roughly zero elasticity: a property of things that (for all intents and purposes) don't re-expand at all following compression.  It is less than (#$VeryLowAmountFn #$Elasticity).\n<p>\nSee also the corresponding predicate #$noAmountOf.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aace0).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aace0,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aace0,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aace0,26471).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aace0,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aace0,[]).
x_comment(x_QuantitySlot,"A specialization of #$ScalarIntervalSlot (q.v.).  Each instance of #$QuantitySlot is a binary predicate that is used to attribute a property to a thing, by relating it to a #$Quantity (q.v.) of some sort.\n<p>\nInstances of #$QuantitySlot include #$lengthOfObject and #$levelOfMorale.\n<p>\nSee also the specializations #$MeasurableQuantitySlot and #$NonNumericQuantitySlot.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aace1).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aace1,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
