x_comment(x_greaterThan,"A #$NumericComparisonPredicate that is a generalization of the  mathematical greater-than ( > ) relation to #$ScalarIntervals (q.v.) of all sorts, including quantitative intervals (see #$NumericInterval and #$MeasurableQuantity) as well as point values (see #$ScalarPointValue).  <code>(#$greaterThan VALUE1 VALUE2)</code> means that  <code>VALUE1</code> is greater than <code>VALUE2</code> with respect to some scale that they  are both on.  More precisely, there is some #$TotallyOrderedScalarIntervalType SCALE that both <code>VALUE1</code> and <code>VALUE2</code> are instances of and either (i) <code>SCALE</code> is a specialization of  #$NumericInterval (e.g. #$RealNumber) and the minimum (see #$minQuantValue) of <code>VALUE1</code> is greater than the maximum (see #$maxQuantValue) of <code>VALUE2</code> or (ii) <code>(#$followingValueOnScale VALUE2 VALUE1 SCALE)</code> holds.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aace6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aace6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aace6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aace6,26485).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aace6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aace6,[]).
x_comment(x_relationInstanceExistsMin,"A quaternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate stands in a certain numerical relation with respect to a given thing and a given collection.  (#$relationInstanceExistsMin BINPRED THING COL N) means that there exists at least N instances (INST(1), ..., INST(N)) of COL that THING bears the relation PRED to, such that (PRED THING INST(i)) holds (for 1 <= i <= N).  This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:\n<p>\n(#$thereExistAtLeast N ?INST\n(#$and\n(#$isa ?INST COL)\n(BINPRED THING ?INST))) .\n<p>\nFor example, `(#$relationInstanceExistsMin #$physicalParts MyLeftHand #$Thumb 1)' means that my left hand has at least one thumb.  Cf. #$relationExistsMinInstance, and see the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aace7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aace7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aace7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aace7,26486).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aace7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aace7,[]).
x_comment(x_infoTransferred,"A #$BinaryRolePredicate that is used to specify some or all of the meaningful content of a particular transferral of information (see  #$InformationTransferEvent).  <code>(#$infoTransferred TRANSFER INFO)</code> means  that <code>INFO</code> is information (see #$AspatialInformationStore) that is  transferred due to <code>TRANSFER</code>.  <code>INFO</code> originates (see #$informationOrigin) from some agent or #$InformationBearingThing (\"IBT\") and, after <code>TRANSFER</code>,  is had by another agent or is contained in another IBT (see  #$informationDestination); <code>INFO</code> may persist in the origin as well.  For example, when I read the front page of the newspaper, some  information about current world events is transferred from the  newspaper to me.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aace8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aace8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aace8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aace8,26494).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aace8,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aace8,[]).
x_comment(x_CharacterString,"A specialization of #$AbstractInformationStructure.  Each instance of #$CharacterString is a string of characters or an abstract sequence of symbols.  Note that an instance of #$CharacterString is <i>not</i> any particular physical, tangible representation, since different encodings may represent the same string of characters.  An instance of #$CharacterString is a list (see the collection #$List) of characters (instances of #$Character-Abstract) from some fixed character set. An instance of #$CharacterString may be any finite length, including zero (the zero-length string is the empty string, which has no characters).  Notable specializations of #$CharacterString include #$EMailAddress, #$AreaCode, #$PhoneNumber, and #$TelephoneCountryCode.  Note that a #$CharacterString is ordered formally as a #$List (rather than physically left-to-right or top-to-bottom); thus the 'first' character in the #$CharacterString for an Arabic word happens to be the rightmost (first pronounced) character, not the leftmost character, due to the letter-order and word-order convention for Arabic writing.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aace9).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aace9,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aace9,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
