x_comment(x_FundamentalUnitType,"A #$ConventionalClassificationType and a specialization of #$InterconvertibleUnitType (q.v.).  Each #$FundamentalUnitType is a collection of interconvertible #$UnitOfMeasures (q.v.) that is taken to be basic in the sense that it not analyzable in terms of other unit-types (see #$unitTypeDimensionalAnalysis).  Thus, the instances of a fundamental unit-type are not defined in terms of other units (see #$unitDefinitions).  Other, \"derived\" unit-types are analyzible into fundamental unit-types.\n<p>\nFor example, #$UnitOfDistance and #$UnitOfTime are standardly taken to be fundamental unit-types, but #$UnitOfSpeed (which is analyzable in terms of them) is not.\n<p>\nNote that it is a matter of convention which unit-types are considered to be fundamental.  It is possible to have a system wherein, e.g., speed and time are fundamental and distance is derived from them.  The unit-types that are standardly taken to be fundamental in modern science were chosen because they are the easiest to measure.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaceb).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaceb,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaceb,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
