x_comment(x_schemaFields,"(#$schemaFields SCHEMA FIELD) means that FIELD is a component field in the schema SCHEMA.  This is the most general relation in the SKSI vocabulary for linking a schema with its component fields.  The arg 1 may be\neither a logical schema or a physical schema (with SCHEMA an instance of #$Schema-LogicalOrPhysical), and the arg\n2 may be either a logical field or a physical field (with FIELD an instance of #$Field-LogicalOrPhysical). This predicste is partitioned by the specializations #$logicalFields and #$physicalFields.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacf3).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacf3,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacf3,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacf3,26509).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacf3,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacf3,[]).
x_comment(x_DangerousTangibleThing,"#$DangerousTangibleThings are those #$PartiallyTangibles which have some combination of density, size, material strength, etc., that makes them particularly efficient at damaging (see #$damages) other #$PartiallyTangible things. The classic examples are those things whose #$typePrimaryFunction are to be #$instrument-UsedAsWeapon, namely #$Weapons. Simply being a spec of this collection does not imply that something is intended to be used to harm others; for example, #$Automobiles are #$DangerousTangibleThings even though that's hardly their #$typePrimaryFunction.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacf4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacf4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacf4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacf4,26512).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacf4,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacf4,[]).
x_comment(x_DisjointCollectionType,"A collection of collections of collections and a specialization of #$SiblingDisjointCollectionType (q.v.).  A disjoint collection type is such that its instances are collections that are all disjoint from one another.  That is, each instance <code>DISCOLTYPE</code> of #$DisjointCollectionType is a collection whose instances are mutually disjoint collections: no two instances of <code>DISCOLTYPE</code> have any instances in common.  Instances of #$DisjointCollectionType include #$BiologicalSpecies and #$SportsTeamTypeBySport.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacf5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacf5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacf5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacf5,26513).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacf5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacf5,[]).
x_comment(x_increasesSlotFor,"(#$increasesSlotFor EVT OBJ SLOT) says that as a result of OBJ's participation in EVT, the SLOT for OBJ is increased.  For example, (#$increasesSlotFor IGainWeight Me #$massOfObject) says that in the particular weight-gaining event in question, my mass is increased.  Note that where EVT is an instance of #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent, (#$increasesSlotFor EVT OBJ SLOT) is equivalent to (#$increasesSlot EVT SLOT).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacf6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacf6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacf6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacf6,26514).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacf6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacf6,[]).
x_comment(x_NaturalTangibleStuff,"An instance of #$ExistingStuffType and a sub-collection of #$PartiallyTangible.  Each instance of #$NaturalTangibleStuff is a naturally occurring partially tangible thing. Specializations of #$NaturalTangibleStuff include #$LandStuff, #$Wood, and #$Air.  Man-made materials are <i>not</i> instances of #$NaturalTangibleStuff.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacf7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacf7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacf7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacf7,26515).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacf7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacf7,[]).
x_comment(x_TransportViaFn(x_LandTransportationDevice),"A collection of events.  An instance of (#$TransportViaFn #$LandTransportationDevice) is a transportation event in which a #$vehicle designed to travel over land is used.  Examples include instances of the collections (#$TransportViaFn #$RoadVehicle), #$Skateboarding, and #$SnowSkiing.  Note: This collection does not include instances of #$AnimalWalkingProcess, which is a #$LocomotionProcess-Animal (q.v.), not a form of transportation as defined in Cyc.  A marginal example of this would be a (fictional) event in which someone burrowed through the earth in a Jules-Vernesque real-time tunnel-digging machine.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacf8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacf8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacf8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacf8,26516).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacf8,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacf8,[]).
x_comment('x_in-ContOpen',"An instance of #$SpatialPredicate.  <code>(#$in-ContOpen OBJ CONT)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is contained in <code>CONT</code> (so that <code>CONT</code> confines or holds <code>OBJ</code> in), but <code>OBJ</code> can be removed from <code>CONT</code> without having to open any portals in <code>CONT</code>, break some part of <code>CONT</code>, or break <code>OBJ</code> into smaller pieces such that it can fit through some portal of <code>CONT</code>.  Note that <code>(#$in-ContOpen OBJ CONT)</code> is a relationship based on the size of <code>OBJ</code> and the largest portal of <code>CONT</code> through which <code>OBJ</code> might be able to move.  For example, a paperclip might be #$in-ContOpen a jail cell even though the person locked inside would be #$in-ContClosed (q.v.) the jail cell.  This is because the paper clip is small enough to fit in between the bars of the cell, whereas the person is not.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacf9).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacf9,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacf9,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacf9,26517).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacf9,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacf9,[]).
x_comment('x_traverses-Complete',"<code>(#$traverses-Complete MOVE TRAVER)</code> means that the #$Movement-TranslationEvent <code>MOVE</code> traverses a path or series of paths (#$Path-Generics forming the #$Traversal <code>TRAVER</code>) during its motion, and that <code>TRAVER</code> is the entire traversal from beginning to end of the movement.  The traversal may cross itself, go back and forth along the same path, or repeat cycles of paths (unlike the #$ActorSlot #$motionPathway-Complete, which can follow only one, non-self-crossing, pre-existing #$Path-Generic.)  See also #$traverses-Partial.  Contrast #$traverses-Complete with #$motionPathway-Complete, that relates to one existing #$Path-Generic, and with #$trajectory-Complete that relates to the pure #$Trajectory of the motion.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacfa).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacfa,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacfa,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacfa,26518).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacfa,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacfa,[]).
x_comment(x_LoanRepaying,"The process of making payments in order to bring the balance towards 0.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacfb).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacfb,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacfb,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
