x_comment(x_BorderBetweenFn,"An instance of both #$PartialFunction and #$BinaryFunction.  When applied to instances <code>REG1</code> and <code>REG2</code> of #$GeographicalRegion, #$BorderBetweenFn returns the entire #$Border (if any) that separates <code>REG1</code> and <code>REG2</code>.  \n<p>\nFor instance, (#$BorderBetweenFn (#$TerritoryFn #$France) (#$TerritoryFn #$Germany)) is the entire border between (#$TerritoryFn #$France) and (#$TerritoryFn #$Germany).  \n<p>\nNote that the border returned by #$BorderBetweenFn may be discontinuous (such as the border between France and Spain, interrupted by Andorra), or circular (such as the border between Italy and San Marino), or in abnormal cases it may even have multiple disconnected and nested cyclic fragments (e.g., the border between Belgium and the Netherlands, due to the Baarle-Nassau and Baarle-Hartog enclaves), or move back and forth periodically (such as at the Isle des Faisans).  Note also that #$BorderBetweenFn is undefined for pairs of regions that do not actually share a border, such as (#$TerritoryFn #$France) and (#$TerritoryFn #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica).  See also #$borderBetweenRegions, which is a (canonically) corresponding predicate for this function.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacfc).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacfc,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacfc,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacfc,26520).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacfc,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacfc,[]).
x_comment('x_traverses-Partial',"<code>(#$traverses-Partial MOVE TRAV)</code> means that the moving object <code>OBJ</code> in the #$Translation-SingleTrajectory <code>MOVE</code> (see #$objectMoving) is partially confined to the #$Traversal <code>TRAV</code> during <code>MOVE</code>.  To say that <code>OBJ</code> is partially confined to <code>TRAV</code> during <code>MOVE</code>, we mean that (1) there is a subevent <code>SUBEVENT</code> of <code>MOVE</code> such that <code>(#$subTranslations-SingleTrajectory MOVE SUBEVENT)</code> holds, and (2) there is a sub-Traversal <code>SUBTRAV</code> of <code>TRAV</code> (see #$subTraversals) such that <code>SUBEVENT</code> is confined to <code>SUBTRAV</code>, i.e., <code>(#$traverses-Complete SUBEVENT SUBTRAV)</code> holds.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacfd).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacfd,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacfd,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacfd,26525).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacfd,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacfd,[]).
x_comment(x_borderBetweenRegions,"<code>(#$borderBetweenRegions BORDER REG1 REG2)</code> means that <code>BORDER</code> is the entire intangible border shared by the adjacent geographical regions <code>REG1</code> and <code>REG2</code>.  Note that <code>BORDER</code> might be discontinuous (such as the border between France and Spain, interrupted by Andorra), or circular (such as the border between Italy and San Marino), or even have multiple disconnected and nested cyclic fragments (the border between Belgium and Netherlands, due to the Baarle-Nassau and Baarle-Hartog enclaves).  To identify arbitrarily large or small segments of borders, see #$formsBorderPart.  See also #$BorderBetweenFn, to which #$borderBetweenRegions is a (canonically) corresponding predicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacfe).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacfe,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacfe,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacfe,26526).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacfe,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacfe,[]).
x_comment(x_formsBorderPart,"This predicate is used to assert that a particular #$SpatialThing-Localized is part of the conventional abstraction that is a #$GeopoliticalBorder. <code>(#$formsBorderPart PART WHOLE)</code> means that all of the points along the border-part <code>PART</code> are also points along the border <code>WHOLE</code>. We would use this predicate, eg, to say that the border between Texas and Mexico forms part of the border of the United States:  (#$formsBorderPart (#$BorderBetweenFn #$Texas-State #$Mexico) (#$BorderFn #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica)).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aacff).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aacff,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aacff,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aacff,26527).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aacff,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aacff,[]).
x_comment(x_TransportationEvent,"A specialization of both #$Conveying-Generic and #$Translation-Complete (qq.v.), instances of which are events in which one or more objects transport one or more other objects.  Each instance  of #$TransportationEvent is an event in which an object (in the role of #$transporter) aids in the translational movement of another object (having the role of #$transportees), so that both objects move together along the same complete pathway (see #$motionPathway-Complete).  Optionally, one of these objects, or some third object moving along with them, provides the force to make the movement happen (see #$providerOfMotiveForce).\n<p>\nExamples of transportation events include automobile transportation, riding a bicycle, dogs pulling goods on a sled, a wagon with groceries rolling down a hill, a person carrying clothes in a suitcase, etc.  In that last case, note that the #$transporter is the suitcase, not the person.  \n<p>\nNote that the transporter in a transportation event need not be in motion relative to its destination throughout the transportation event; an automobile transportation is a single transportation event even if it has sub-events in which the driver and all of the passengers disembark while the car is parked and refueled.  A single transportation event may also have more than one transporter.  For example, a sofa may be transported accross a living room floor by two people working together.  \n<p>\nEvents which are <i>not</i> #$TransportationEvents include a river conveying some flotsam, the wind blowing a leaf, a conveyor belt moving a widget to the next person on an assembly line, or a walking beast of burden that is carrying nothing.  The first three of these negative examples are instances of #$Conveying-Stationary, since the would-be #$transporter doesn't actually change its overall location (see #$conveyor-Stationary); in the fourth case the unburdened beast has no #$transportees and the event is an instance of #$AnimalWalkingProcess.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aad00).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aad00,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aad00,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aad00,26528).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aad00,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aad00,[]).
x_comment(x_trueSentence,"A #$KBDependentRelation (q.v.) and specialization of #$consistent (q.v.) that is used to state that a given CycL sentence is true.  <code>(#$trueSentence SENT)</code> means that <code>SENT</code> is true.\n<p>\n#$trueSentence is a non-assertible predicate (see  #$notAssertible).  But this puts no real limitation on the expressive power of the Cyc system since one can assert that <code>SENT</code> is true by simply asserting <code>SENT</code> itself.  \n<p>\nMost occurrences of the expression '#$trueSentence' in CycL assertions have been added by the canonicalizer, so that certain nested sentences (or variables occurring in argument-positions intended for sentences) can receive special handling during canonicalization or inference. See the accompanying #$cyclistNotes for details.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aad01).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aad01,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aad01,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aad01,26535).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aad01,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aad01,[]).
x_comment(x_transporterTypeOfTransportType,"The corresponding predicate of #$TransportViaFn.  <code>(#$transporterTypeOfTransportType EVENT-TYPE TRANS-TYPE)</code> means that in events that are instances of <code>EVENT-TYPE</code>, instances of <code>TRANS-TYPE</code> are the types of transportation devices used.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aad02).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aad02,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aad02,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
