x_comment(x_sizeParameterOfObject,"A #$PhysicalFeatureBinaryPredicate and #$BinaryQuantityPredicate for indicating the size of a given spatial thing with respect to some spatial parameter or dimension.  <code>(#$sizeParameterOfObject OBJECT SIZE)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> measures <code>SIZE</code> in some (unspecified) parameter, where <code>SIZE</code> is either a #$Distance (q.v.) or some other sort of #$SpatialQuantity (q.v.) that is analyzable in terms of distances (e.g. an #$Area or a #$Volume). Thus <code>SIZE</code> might be <code>OBJECT</code>'s length, width, surface area, volume, or other distance-derived quantity.\n<p>\n#$sizeParameterOfObject is the common generalization of all those #$PhysicalAmountSlots (q.v.) that relate objects to spatial quantities.  As such, it is a general size predicate for expressing (in a parameter-nonspecific way) all sorts of size information about an object or other spatial thing.  For parameter-specific size predicates, see the various specializations of #$sizeParameterOfObject.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aad32).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aad32,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
