x_comment(x_PassivizableFrame,"The collection of all frames which can be used in the passive voice, i.e. where an object of the active voice use of this frame 'becomes' the syntactic (though not semantic) subject in the passive voice, and the subject of the active voice use of the frame can optionally become a 'by'-PP syntactic (though not semantic) object. Examples are #$TransitiveNPFrameType, #$DitransitiveNP-GenericFrameType and the #$DitransitivePPFrameTypes:. 'John likes Mary' -> 'Mary is liked (by John)', 'John gives Mary a book' -> 'Mary is given a book (by John)', 'John gives a book to Mary' -> 'A book is given to Mary (by John)'") :- x_cid(a65b16526aad8c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aad8c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aad8c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aad8c,26795).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aad8c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aad8c,[]).
x_comment(x_agreementPeriod,"The predicate #$agreementPeriod is used to indicate the period of time during which a particular agreement is in force.  <code>(#$agreementPeriod AGREE TIME)</code> means that the #$Agreement <code>AGREE</code> holds during the #$TimeInterval <code>TIME</code>.  <code>TIME</code> may or may not begin at the moment that <code>AGREE</code> is created; e.g., a person may sign an employment contract on the very day she begins working or several weeks before, to begin on a specified future date.  An #$agreementPeriod could even begin before the agreement was made, e.g., when an agent agrees to pay disputed royalties retroactively.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aad8d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aad8d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aad8d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aad8d,26796).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aad8d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aad8d,[]).
x_comment(x_interestRate,"<code>(#$interestRate AGREE RATE)</code> means that the interest rate on #$Agreement <code>AGREE</code> is <code>RATE</code> expressed as a percent per year (or other unit of time).  The second argument should be expressed as in the following example: <code>(#$interestRate LOAN ((#$PerFn #$Unity DURATION) (#$Percent N)))</code> where <code>DURATION</code> is some amount of time such as #$YearsDuration.  An #$interestRate may be on some #$LoanAgreement or it may be on some other #$Agreement, such as a #$Stock.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aad8e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aad8e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aad8e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aad8e,26797).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aad8e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aad8e,[]).
x_comment(x_minimumDurationOfType,"This predicate relates collections of temporal things to the minimum duration of typical instances of that type.\nErr on the side of caution when making assertions with this predicate, but also keep in mind that it is a default rule.  For instance, in the #$UnitedStatesSocialLifeMt one might assert that (#$minimumDurationOfType #$Person (#$YearsDuration 40)) because it is true that the vast majority of Americans in the late 20th century live for at least 40 years; this despite the fact that there are numerous counterexamples of Americans who die young.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aad8f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aad8f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aad8f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aad8f,26798).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aad8f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aad8f,[]).
x_comment(x_ComplexNumber,"A specialization of both #$Number-General and  #$ScalarPointValue.  Each instance of #$ComplexNumber can be thought of as a vector of two numbers, which are usually called the \"real part\" and the \"imaginary part\" of the complex number.  \n<p>\nEvery complex number can be represented as <i>a</i> + i<i>b</i> where i is the square root of negative one (see #$I-TheNumber), and <i>a</i> and <i>b</i> are reals (see #$RealNumber).  When <i>b</i>=0, the complex number has no imaginary part, and therefore is a real number.  When <i>b</i> is not zero the complex number has an imginary part and so it is an imaginary number (see #$ImaginaryNumber).  \n<p>\nComplex numbers may also be considered as corresponding to points in the real plane, where the x axis determines the real component of a complex number and the y axis the imaginary component.  The unit value on the real number line is 1, the unit value on the imaginary number line is the square root of -1, generally written 'i' in mathematics and 'j' in engineering.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aad90).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aad90,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aad90,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aad90,26800).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aad90,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aad90,[]).
x_comment(x_ExistingObjectType,"A collection of collections.  Each instance of #$ExistingObjectType is a specialization of #$PartiallyTangible (q.v.).  Moreover, each instance of #$ExistingObjectType is a collection that is temporally stuff-like (see #$TemporalStuffType) but physically-spatially object-like (see #$ObjectType) -- i.e.  object-like with respect to some physical-spatial part-whole predicate (e.g. some specialization of #$properPhysicalDecompositions).\n<p>\nConsider the collection #$BookCopy. #$BookCopy is a specialization of #$PartiallyTangible. Further, #$BookCopy is temporally stuff-like: any one of the #$timeSlices of a given copy <code>COPY</code> of (say) \"Moby Dick\" is still a copy of \"Moby Dick\". (Most collections of tangible objects are temporally stuff-like in this way.)   But #$BookCopy is spatially <i>object-like</i>: if we take a scalpel and slice <code>COPY</code> into ten pieces, each piece is not a copy of \"Moby Dick\".  So #$BookCopy is an instance of #$ExistingObjectType.\n<p>\nSee also #$StuffType and #$ExistingStuffType.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aad91).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aad91,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aad91,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aad91,26805).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aad91,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aad91,[]).
x_comment('x_Volatility-Physical',"A specialization of #$TangiblePhysicalQuantity and #$DispositionalQuantity.  Each instance of #$Volatility-Physical is a specific readiness of some liquid to evaporate.  Higher volatility indicates a faster rate of evaporation.  Volatilities of substances are indicated with the predicate #$volatilityOfSubstance (q.v.).") :- x_cid(a65b16526aad92).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aad92,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
