x_comment('x_Gearing-Mechanical',"Spinning of a #$Gear along a fixed #$Axle [sometimes called 'shaft'] (through its center of gravity) while constrained by a #$Gear along the surface. #$Gearing-Mechanical is used for transmission of energy between the #$Axle and the surface in either or both directions.  There is no #$Slipping in #$Gearing-Mechanical between the #$Gear and the external surface (usually a #$Gear) with which it is in contact.  Both the #$Gear and the surface with which it is in contact move.  If a #$Gear is merely rolling along a fixed track, the process is considered #$WheelRolling (albeit with slippage protection) instead of #$Gearing-Mechanical.  Gear teeth may be #$Sliding-Generic against each other by design.  The ratio of rotational velocities between two gears is a design feature, dependent upon the number, shape, and arrangement of teeth on each #$Gear, called the gear ratio.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aad9b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aad9b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aad9b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aad9b,26819).
