x_comment(x_argCardinality,"An #$ArgConstraintPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to put a cardinality-based constraint on a predicate with respect to a given argument-place. (#$argCardinality PRED N CARD) means that, for any particular way of fixing the other arguments to PRED besides the Nth, there are at most CARD distinct things such that, if any one of them is taken as the _Nth_ argument, PRED holds of those arguments.  That is, there are at most CARD distinct sequences of the form <... ARGN(i) ...> that differ from one another only in their Nth members (their respective ARGN(i)) and are such that (PRED ... ARGN(i) ...) holds.\n<p>\nNote that CARD is simply the _maximum_ number of such sequences that there can be.  There is no guarantee that there will be _at_least_ CARD such sequences; in any given case there might be fewer than CARD such sequences, and possibly none at all.  On the other hand, there is a default assumption that the presence of an assertion of the form (#$argCardinality PRED N CARD) in the Knowledge Base indicates that the relation PRED is such that no number lower than CARD could correctly have been used instead.  (Given that the above argument-cardinality assertion is true, (#$argCardinality PRED N CARD+1) is trivially true as well;  the default assumption reflects the idea that it would nevertheless be misleading to assert the later.)\n<p>\nMost of the argument-cardinality sentences worth asserting are where CARD is 1, which entails that PRED is \"strictly functional\" in its Nth argument (see #$strictlyFunctionalInArgs).  As an upper limit, CARD might be #$PlusInfinity, which amounts to an explicit denial that any finite cardinality restriction is correct for the argument-place in question, which amounts to no restriction at all; so such sentences are worth asserting to the KB only in cases where this lack of any cardinality restriction is likely to be surprising or unexpected, in order to document the fact that the question has already been considered and thereby reduce the potential for an unnecessary duplication of effort.  As a lower limit, CARD might be 0, which entails that PRED holds of _no_ argument-sequence whatsoever; but it is unlikely that any such assertion should appear in the KB, since there would be little point to having such a predicate in the CycL language in the first place.  See also #$interArgCardinality, #$assertedArgCardinality, and #$intraArgReln.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaf13).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaf13,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaf13,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aaf13,27480).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aaf13,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aaf13,[]).
x_comment(x_interArgCardinality,"An instance of both #$ArgCardinalityPredicate and #$InterArgConstraintPredicate (qq.v.) that can be used to put a conditional argument-cardinality constraint on a specified argument-place of a predicate, dependent upon the type of thing occupying that or another specified argument-place.\n<p>\n(#$interArgCardinality PRED M COL N CARD) means that when PRED's Mth argument is an instance of COL then its Nth argument is constrained to a cardinality of CARD.  Thus for any particular way of fixing the other arguments to PRED besides the Nth, if the Mth argument is an instance of COL, then there are at most CARD distinct things such that, if any one of them is taken as the _Nth_ argument while keeping the other arguments as already fixed (and keeping the Mth argument an instance of COL if N = M), PRED holds of those arguments.  That is (assuming without loss of generality that M > N), there are at most CARD distinct sequences of the form <... ARGM ... ARGN(i) ...> with an Mth member (ARGM) that is an instance of COL and that differ from one another _only_ in their Nth members (their respective ARGN(i)) and are such that (PRED ... ARGM ... ARGN(i) ...) holds.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$interArgCardinality #$performedBy 1 #$Breathing 2 1) means that for any event BREATH that is a #$Breathing, there is at most one thing BREATHER such that (#$performedBy BREATH BREATHER) holds.  Simply put, an act of breathing is never performed by more than one agent.\n<p>\nAs in the case of the unconditional constraint predicate #$argCardinality (q.v.), most #$interArgCardinality sentences asserted to the Knowledge Base are such that CARD is 1.  For more discussion on argument-cardinality constraints in general, see the comment on #$argCardinality.  See also #$assertedArgCardinality, and #$intraArgReln.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaf14).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaf14,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaf14,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aaf14,27485).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aaf14,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aaf14,[]).
x_comment(x_intersectsWith,"A #$TaxonomicSlot that relates collections whose extents (see #$extent) intersect or overlap.  <code>(#$intersectsWith COL1 COL2)</code>  means that <code>COL1</code> and <code>COL2</code> have at least one instance in common.   Note that this predicate is <i>not</i> reflexive: it is trivially  possible for a #$Collection to fail to intersect with itself, though only if it is empty (i.e. has no instances).  Thus, for  example, (#$intersectsWith #$Nothing #$Nothing) <i>fails</i> to hold. Cf. #$disjointWith.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaf15).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaf15,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaf15,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aaf15,27492).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526aaf15,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526aaf15,[]).
x_comment(x_coextensionalSetOrCollectionOrRelations,"An instance of #$TaxonomicSlot and a generalization of  #$coextensionalSetOrCollections.   #$coextensionalSetOrCollectionOrRelations relates predicates and  functions as well as sets and collections; thus it holds between  coextensional #$SetOrCollections (q.v.), between coextensional  #$Predicates (q.v.), and between coextensional  #$Function-Denotationals (q.v.).   (#$coextensionalSetOrCollectionOrRelations SET|COL|REL-1 SET|COL|REL-2)  means that SET|COL|REL-1 and SET|COL|REL-2 are coextensional.  More  precisely: (i) if SET|COL|REL-1 and SET|COL|REL-2 are both  #$SetOrCollections, then they have all their elements in common  (see #$coextensionalSetOrCollections); (ii) if SET|COL|REL-1 and  SET|COL|REL-2 are both #$Predicates, then they hold of exactly the same argument-sequences; and (iii) if SET|COL|REL-1 and SET|COL|REL-2  are both #$Function-Denotationals, then they are defined for exactly the same argument-sequences and both have the same value for any such sequence.  Note that, since coextensionality entails identity  (see #$equals) in the case of sets but _not_ in the cases of  collections or relations, it _cannot_ generally be inferred from  the above that SET|COL|REL-1 and SET|COL|REL-2 are identical.") :- x_cid(a65b16526aaf16).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526aaf16,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526aaf16,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526aaf16,27493).
