x_comment(x_monetaryValueOfThing,"(#$monetaryValueOfThing THING VALUE) means that #$TemporalThing THING (which may be goods or services) is in some sense worth #$MonetaryValue VALUE. This may be due to its appraised value (for which see #$appraisedValue), due to its having been bought for the price VALUE some time in the past (for which see #$bookValue), due to its being currently for sale at the price VALUE (for which see #$basicPrice), due to its being a piece of #$Currency that has been manufactured to stand for the amount of money VALUE (for which see #$tenderValue), or for other reasons. This predicate is thus very much an umbrella term. See also the ternary predicate #$monetaryValueOfThingTo.") :- x_cid(a65b16526ab2f1).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526ab2f1,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526ab2f1,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526ab2f1,29146).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526ab2f1,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526ab2f1,[]).
x_comment('x_increasesSitSlot-Subjective',"(#$increasesSitSlot-Subjective SIT THING SLOT) expresses the fact\nthat, everything else being equal, in the situation SIT the value of the\n#$QuantitySlot SLOT (i.e. the arg2 to SLOT) to be attributed to THING would\nbe higher than some default or presupposed base value. The `everything else\nbeing equal' clause indicates that the value of SLOT for THING might\nchange, even *de*crease, were more information to become available about\nSIT. This predicate captures an important aspect of probability\nassignments: they tend to be subjective, i.e., based on the amount of\ninformation available to an evaluator, and they are not *persistent* with\nrespect to situations.  Consider an example: A situation in which I board a\nplane from Austin to Chicago at 7am on a given day may *or may not* be one\nin which it is more likely that I will be in Chicago on time for a 3pm meeting\nthan a situation in which I drive a car, because other undetermined factors\nmight play a negative role. For example, that particular situation might\nalso be a situation in which fierce storms shut down air traffic into\nChicago for 2 days. However, barring additional factors and constraints\n(`everything else being equal'), if all I know about a situation is that in\nit I take a plane to Chicago at 7am, I would be a fool not to assign a\nhigher probability to my being on time for my meeting in such a\nsituation. The relation expressed by #$increasesSitSlot-Subjective is not \n(necessarily) causal. Also, note that (#$increasesSitSlot-Subjective SIT\nTHING SLOT) doesn't entail (#$increases-Generic SIT THING SLOT) since the\nlatter implies that the value of SLOT for THING *is actually* higher after\nthe beginning of SIT. Nor does (#$increases-Generic SIT THING SLOT) entail \n(#$increasesSitSlot-Subjective SIT THING SLOT). Finally, the meaning of\nthis predicate is not inherently temporal, unlike #$increases-Generic.") :- x_cid(a65b16526ab2f2).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526ab2f2,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526ab2f2,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526ab2f2,29147).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526ab2f2,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526ab2f2,[]).
x_comment('x_increasesSitTypeSlot-Subjective',"This predicate is a type-level cognate of\n#$increasesSitSlot-Subjective (q.v.). (#$increasesSitTypeSlot-Subjective\nTYPE THING SLOT) expresses the fact that, everything else being equal, in\nsituations that are instances of TYPE the value of the #$QuantitySlot SLOT\n(i.e. the arg2 to SLOT) to be attributed to THING would be higher than some\ndefault or presupposed base value. Note that the `everything else being equal' clause\nindicates that the value of SLOT for THING might change, even *de*crease,\nwere more information to become available about TYPE: this means that this\npredicate is *not* inverse-transitive w.r.t. the #$genls relation in the\nfirst argument. See also the documentation available for\n#$increasesSitSlot-Subjective.") :- x_cid(a65b16526ab2f3).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526ab2f3,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526ab2f3,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526ab2f3,29173).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526ab2f3,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526ab2f3,[]).
x_comment('x_decreasesPropSlot-Subjective',"(#$decreasesPropSlot-Subjective SENT THING SLOT) expresses the fact that, everything else being equal, if SENT is true (asserted) the value of the #$QuantitySlot SLOT (i.e. the arg2 to SLOT) to be attributed to THING is lower than some default or presupposed base  value. The `everything else being equal' clause indicates that the value of  SLOT for THING might change, even increase, were more information to  become available -- that is, the SLOT-value assignment to THING might  change, even holding SENT true, if additional sentences were known to be  true. This is the propositional version of #$decreasesSitSlot-Subjective. See #$increasesSitSlot-Subjective for more extensive documentation.") :- x_cid(a65b16526ab2f4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526ab2f4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526ab2f4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526ab2f4,29184).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526ab2f4,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526ab2f4,[]).
x_comment('x_decreasesSitSlot-Subjective',"(#$decreasesSitSlot-Subjective SIT THING SLOT) expresses the fact\nthat, everything else being equal, in the situation SIT the value of the\n#$QuantitySlot SLOT (i.e., the arg2 of SLOT) attributed to THING would be\nlower than some default or presupposed base value. This predicate is\nespecially useful for reasoning about probability assignments. See the documentation\nfor #$increasesSitSlot-Subjective for examples, and for comparing this\npredicates to similar ones like #$increases-Generic and\n#$decreases-Generic.") :- x_cid(a65b16526ab2f5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526ab2f5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
