x_comment(x_uniformColorOfObject,"The formula (#$uniformColorOfObject OBJ COL) means that all of the visible parts of the #$PartiallyTangible OBJ have the color the #$Color COL.  Hence, this predicate only applies to objects that are entirely one color. For example, the assertion (#$relationAllInstance #$uniformColorOfObject (#$LiquidFn Gold) #$GoldColor) means that all #$visibleParts of all instances of (#$LiquidFn #$Gold) are #$GoldColor.  This predicate should be used carefully, as it has far more strength than the other color predicates.  See also #$mainColorOfObject and #$objectHasColor.") :- x_cid(a65b16526ab33f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526ab33f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526ab33f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526ab33f,29313).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526ab33f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526ab33f,[]).
x_comment(x_mainColorOfObject,"The formula (#$mainColorOfObject ?OBJ ?COL) means that the #$PartiallyTangible ?OBJ is almost entirely colored with the #$Color ?COL.  For example, a #$BrownBear may have green eyes and white claws, but we would still say that the bear is brown.  Therefore, (#$mainColorOfObject #$BrownBear #$BrownColor) would be true.  The green and white would be referenced with the predicate, #$objectHasColor.  An example where it is NOT  appropriate to use this predicate is to describe a #$Zebra.  Since a #$Zebra is roughly equally black and white, we would not say that it is primarily either of those colors.  In this case, it would be better to use #$significantColorOfObject.  One should think of the expressions of the form 'The OBJECT is COLOR' when using this predicate.  For example, even though an apple may have a brown stem and a green leaf, we would still say 'The apple is red'.  Contrarily, we would NOT say 'That zebra is black'.  Likewise, if we had a shirt that was 49% red and 51% green, we would NOT say that the color of that shirt is green.  If we had a shirt that was green with a small, white insignia on the pocket, we WOULD, in this case, say that the #$mainColorOfObject, this shirt, is green.  See also #$mainColorOfObject, #$objectHasColor and #$uniformColorOfObject.") :- x_cid(a65b16526ab340).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526ab340,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526ab340,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526ab340,29314).
