x_comment(x_functionCorrespondsToPredicate,"A #$MetaRelation and specialization of #$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Generic that is used to state that the relationship of the given function to its arguments is expressed by the given predicate.\n<p>\n(#$functionCorrespondsToPredicate FUNC PRED N) means that FUNC maps a given argument-sequence ARGS (say of length M) to the value VAL only if PRED holds of the (M+1 length) sequence ARGS-N-VAL, where ARGS-N-VAL is just like ARGS except for having VAL as its Nth member and having ARGS's Nth to Mth members as its (N+1)th to (M+1)th members.  If PRED is a #$FunctionalPredicate, with its Nth argument-place being a \"functional\" one (see #$functionalInArgs), then the more specific relationship, #$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical (q.v.) applies.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$functionCorrespondsToPredicate #$MovieNamedFn #$properNameStrings 1) implies that (#$properNameStrings (#$MovieNamedFn \"Gandhi\") \"Gandhi\"), but (#$properNameStrings #$MahatmaGandhi \"Gandhi\") does not mean that #$MahatmaGandhi and (#$MovieNamedFn \"Gandhi\") represent the same thing.  See also #$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical, #$functionCorrespondingPredicate, #$functionCorrespondsToShuffledPredicate, and #$functionCorrespondsToShuffledPredicate-Symmetric.") :- x_cid(a65b16526ab456).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526ab456,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
