x_comment('x_proximityConstraints-Type',"(#$proximityConstraints-Type SIT-TYPE ROLE1 ROLE2 RANGE) means that RANGE is the range of distance that object OBJ can be from the location in which something plays ROLE2 in an instance SIT of #$Situation SIT-TYPE in order for OBJ to play the role ROLE1 in SIT.  For example, (#$proximityConstraints-Type (#$KillingByMeansOfFn #$Hatchet) #$performedBy #$organismKilled (#$Meter .25 30) means that whoever kills with a hatchet must be between a quarter meter and 30 meters from the organism killed; (#$proximityConstraints-Type (#$KillingByMeansOfFn #$Hatchet) #$deviceUsed #$organismKilled #$ActuallyTouching) means that a hatchet must actually touch any organism it kills.  Compare this predicate to the instance-level predicate #$proximityConstraints.") :- x_cid(a65b16526ab639).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526ab639,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526ab639,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526ab639,30521).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526ab639,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526ab639,[]).
x_comment(x_massNumberOfAtom,"(#$massNumberOfAtom ATOM NUM) is true iff ATOM is an instance\nof #$Atom with a mass number of NUM. The mass number of an\natom is the sum of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom of\nX. All atoms of a particular instance of\n#$ElementStuffTypeByNumberOfProtons have the same number of protons in the\nnucleus   (expressed as the #$atomicNumber of the element). Isotopes of an\nelement  (cf. #$IsotopeFn) have a different number of neutrons. Thus the\nnumber of neutrons in an atom of X (where X an element or isotope) equals: the\nmass number of the atom minus the atomic number of X. Isotopes are identified by\nthe mass number of their atoms: e.g. (#$IsotopeFn Carbon 14).") :- x_cid(a65b16526ab63a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526ab63a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526ab63a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526ab63a,30522).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526ab63a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526ab63a,[]).
x_comment(x_LogarithmFn,"A #$BinaryFunction which returns the result of computing the logarithm of a specified number to a specified base. (#$LogarithmFn NUMBER BASE) returns log_BASE(NUMBER), i.e. the logarithm of NUMBER in base BASE, where NUMBER is an instance of #$PositiveNumber and BASE is an instance of #$NonNegativeNumber. It returns instances of #$RealNumber.\n<p>\nNote that (#$ExponentFn BASE (#$LogarithmFn NUMBER BASE)) = NUMBER.\n<p>\nSee also #$LogFn, a unary function which returns the logarithm of a number in base #$E-LogarithmBase.") :- x_cid(a65b16526ab63b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526ab63b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526ab63b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526ab63b,30532).
xc_kb_names(a65b16526ab63b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16526ab63b,[]).
