x_comment(x_HighExplosive,"A specialization of #$ExplosiveMaterial (q.v.).  Each instance of #$HighExplosive is an explosive that releases its energy fast enough to sustain a detonation wave (see #$DetonationWave). High explosives decompose almost instantaneously (i.e., at rate of over 1000 meters per second), either in a manner similar to a rapid combustion or with rupture and rearrangement of the molecules that comprise the explosive.  High explosives are conventionally subdivided into two categories that are differentiated by sensitivity: primary explosives, which are extremely sensitive to shock, friction, and heat (see #$PrimaryDetonatorExplosiveType); secondary explosives, which are relatively insensitive to shock, friction, and heat (see #$SecondaryDetonatorExplosiveType). See also #$LowExplosive.  Note that high explosives detonate (see #$Detonation), whereas low explosives deflagrate (see #$Deflagration).") :- x_cid(a65b16526ab656).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526ab656,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526ab656,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16526ab656,30575).
