x_comment('x_patchFixesFaultOf-ReportIndexed',"(#$patchFixesFaultOf-ReportIndexed PATCH  FAULT  FAULT-REPORT LIST), where LIST is (#$TheList SOFTWARE-OBJECT ... SOFTWARE-OBJECT-N), means that PATCH fixes the fault FAULT of the version of SOFTWARE-OBJECT-N that is packaged with SOFTWARE-OBJECT N-1 that is packaged with ... SOFTWARE-OBJECT-1.  (That is, SOFTWARE-OBJECT-N (the last member of LIST) is the \"sub-program\" and SOFTWARE-OBJECT-1 the \"main program\" of which SOFTWARE-OBJECT-N is a part.  DATABASE and ID-STRING indicate the security database (and its reference no.) where the fault-information is recorded.  For example: (#$patchFixesFaultOf-ReportIndexed #$Q252795-W2K-SP3-X86-En-TheSoftwarePatch #$BufferOverflowFault-CommandExecution (#$ComputerVulnerabilityReportFn #$BugtraqFaultDatabase \"3215\") #$MSWindows2000) means that the buffer overflow fault with MS Windows 2000 reported in Bugtraq entry number 3215 will not render a computer vulnerable if patch Q252795 has been applied to the computer in question. Sometimes the \"patch\" is actually an updated version of the program being patched.") :- x_cid(a65b16526ab861).
xc_microtheory(a65b16526ab861,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16526ab861,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part05.krf').
